/* General's questions and content, added a section at a time; each is checked with tools/pool-check.js general. */
window.DECKS=[{"id":"g1","code":"G1","name":"FCC rules","exam":5,"G":{"G1A":"Frequency privileges and allocations","G1B":"Antennas, beacons, what may be sent","G1C":"Power limits, digital rules, 60 meters","G1D":"Examiners, exam credit, remote operation","G1E":"Third party, repeaters, automatic stations"},"Q":[["G1A01","On which HF and/or MF amateur bands are there portions where General class licensees cannot transmit?",["60 meters, 30 meters, 17 meters, and 12 meters","160 meters, 60 meters, 15 meters, and 12 meters","80 meters, 40 meters, 20 meters, and 15 meters","80 meters, 20 meters, 15 meters, and 10 meters"],2,"General is limited on 80, 40, 20 and 15 meters, where parts are reserved for Extra licensees. The other HF bands are all General."],["G1A02","On which of the following bands is phone operation prohibited?",["160 meters","30 meters","17 meters","12 meters"],1,"No phone is allowed on 30 meters. It's a narrow band shared with other services, so only CW and data are permitted."],["G1A03","On which of the following bands is image transmission prohibited?",["160 meters","30 meters","20 meters","12 meters"],1,"Image transmission, like slow-scan TV, is prohibited on 30 meters, for the same reason as phone: the band is narrow and shared."],["G1A05","On which of the following frequencies are General class licensees prohibited from operating as control operator?",["7.125 MHz to 7.175 MHz","28.000 MHz to 28.025 MHz","21.275 MHz to 21.300 MHz","All these choices are correct"],0,"7.125 to 7.175 MHz is reserved for Extra and Advanced licensees. General phone on 40 meters starts at 7.175 MHz."],["G1A06","Which of the following applies when the FCC rules designate the amateur service as a secondary user on a band?",["Amateur stations must record the call sign of the primary service station before operating on a frequency assigned to that station","Amateur stations may use the band only during emergencies","Amateur stations must not cause harmful interference to primary users and must accept interference from primary users","Amateur stations may only operate during specific hours of the day, while primary users are permitted 24-hour use of the band"],2,"As a secondary user, amateurs must not cause harmful interference to primary users and must accept interference from them."],["G1A07","On which amateur frequencies in the 10-meter band may stations with a General class control operator transmit CW emissions?",["28.000 MHz to 28.025 MHz only","28.000 MHz to 28.300 MHz only","28.025 MHz to 28.300 MHz only","The entire band"],3,"CW is allowed on any frequency your license covers, so a General may send CW across the entire 10-meter band."],["G1A08","Which HF bands have segments exclusively allocated to Amateur Extra licensees?",["All HF bands","80 meters, 40 meters, 20 meters, and 15 meters","All HF bands except 160 meters and 10 meters","60 meters, 30 meters, 17 meters, and 12 meters"],1,"80, 40, 20 and 15 meters have segments reserved for Amateur Extra licensees."],["G1A09","Which of the following frequencies is within the General class portion of the 15-meter band?",["14250 kHz","18155 kHz","21300 kHz","24900 kHz"],2,"21300 kHz is 21.300 MHz, inside the General phone segment of 15 meters (21.275 to 21.450 MHz)."],["G1A10","What portion of the 10-meter band is available for repeater use?",["The entire band","The portion between 28.1 MHz and 28.2 MHz","The portion between 28.3 MHz and 28.5 MHz","The portion above 29.5 MHz"],3,"On 10 meters, repeaters may operate only above 29.5 MHz."],["G1A11","When General class licensees are not permitted to use the entire voice portion of a band, which portion of the voice segment is available to them?",["The lower frequency portion","The upper frequency portion","The lower frequency portion on frequencies below 7.3 MHz, and the upper portion on frequencies above 14.150 MHz","The upper frequency portion on frequencies below 7.3 MHz, and the lower portion on frequencies above 14.150 MHz"],1,"Where a General gets only part of a voice segment, it's the upper part. The lower part is reserved for Extra (and Advanced) licensees."],["G1B01","What is the maximum height above ground for an antenna structure not near a public use airport without requiring notification to the FAA and registration with the FCC?",["50 feet","100 feet","200 feet","250 feet"],2,"200 feet. Taller, and the structure needs FAA notification and FCC registration; near a public-use airport it may need them at lower heights."],["G1B02","With which of the following conditions must beacon stations comply?",["No more than one beacon station may transmit in the same band from the same station location","The frequency must be coordinated with the National Beacon Organization","The frequency must be posted on the internet or published in a national periodical","All these choices are correct"],0,"No more than one beacon may transmit in the same band from the same station location."],["G1B03","Which of the following is a purpose of a beacon station as identified in the FCC rules?",["Observation of propagation and reception","Automatic identification of repeaters","Transmission of bulletins of general interest to amateur radio licensees","All these choices are correct"],0,"A beacon is for observing propagation and reception. You listen for it to learn whether a band is open to its location."],["G1B04","Which of the following transmissions is permitted for all amateur stations?",["Unidentified transmissions of less than 10 seconds duration for test purposes only","Automatic retransmission of other amateur signals by any amateur station","Occasional retransmission of weather and propagation forecast information from US government stations","Encrypted messages, if not intended to facilitate a criminal act"],2,"Occasional retransmission of US government weather and propagation forecasts is allowed. Apart from that and NASA-approved spacecraft communications, non-amateur signals may not be relayed."],["G1B05","Which of the following one-way transmissions are permitted?",["Unidentified test transmissions of less than 10 seconds in duration","Transmissions to assist with learning the International Morse code","Regular transmissions offering equipment for sale, if intended for amateur radio use","All these choices are correct"],1,"Transmissions that help people learn Morse code are one of the permitted one-way transmissions."],["G1B06","Under what conditions are state and local governments permitted to regulate amateur radio antenna structures?",["Under no circumstances, FCC rules take priority","At any time and to any extent necessary to accomplish a legitimate purpose of the state or local entity, provided that proper filings are made with the FCC","Only when such structures exceed 50 feet in height and are clearly visible 1,000 feet from the structure","Amateur Service communications must be reasonably accommodated, and regulations must constitute the minimum practical to accommodate a legitimate purpose of the state or local entity"],3,"Under PRB-1, local rules must reasonably accommodate amateur radio and be the minimum practical regulation for their legitimate purpose."],["G1B07","What are the restrictions on the use of abbreviations or procedural signals in the amateur service?",["Only “Q” signals are permitted","They may be used if they do not obscure the meaning of a message","They are not permitted","They are limited to those expressly listed in Part 97 of the FCC rules"],1,"Abbreviations and procedural signals are fine as long as they don't obscure the meaning of a message."],["G1B08","When is it permissible to communicate with amateur stations in countries outside the areas administered by the Federal Communications Commission?",["Only when the foreign country has a formal third-party agreement filed with the FCC","When the contact is with amateurs in any country except those whose administrations have notified the ITU that they object to such communications","Only when the contact is with amateurs licensed by a country which is a member of the United Nations, or by a territory possessed by such a country","Only when the contact is with amateurs licensed by a country which is a member of the International Amateur Radio Union, or by a territory possessed by such a country"],1,"You may contact amateurs in any country except one whose government has notified the ITU that it objects."],["G1B09","On what HF frequencies are automatically controlled beacons permitted?",["On any frequency if power is less than 1 watt","On any frequency if transmissions are in Morse code","21.08 MHz to 21.09 MHz","28.20 MHz to 28.30 MHz"],3,"On HF, automatically controlled beacons are permitted only from 28.20 to 28.30 MHz."],["G1B10","What is the power limit for beacon stations?",["10 watts PEP output","20 watts PEP output","100 watts PEP output","200 watts PEP output"],2,"A beacon may run at most 100 watts."],["G1B11","Who or what determines “good engineering and good amateur practice,” as applied to the operation of an amateur station in all respects not covered by the Part 97 rules?",["The FCC","The control operator","The IEEE","The ITU"],0,"The FCC decides what counts as good engineering and good amateur practice wherever Part 97 doesn't spell it out."],["G1C01","What is the maximum transmitter power an amateur station may use on 10.140 MHz?",["200 watts PEP output","1000 watts PEP output","1500 watts PEP output","2000 watts PEP output"],0,"10.140 MHz is on 30 meters, where the limit is 200 watts PEP output."],["G1C02","What is the maximum transmitter power an amateur station may use on the 12-meter band?",["50 watts PEP output","200 watts PEP output","1500 watts PEP output","An effective radiated power equivalent to 100 watts from a half-wave dipole"],2,"12 meters has the standard limit, 1500 watts PEP output."],["G1C03","What is the maximum bandwidth permitted by FCC rules for amateur radio stations transmitting on USB frequencies in the 60-meter band?",["2.8 kHz","5.6 kHz","1.8 kHz","3 kHz"],0,"On 60 meters, signals may be no wider than 2.8 kHz."],["G1C04","Which of the following is required by the FCC rules when operating in the 60-meter band?",["If you are using an antenna other than a dipole, you must keep a record of the gain of your antenna","You must keep a record of the date, time, frequency, power level, and stations worked","You must keep a record of all third-party traffic","You must keep a record of the manufacturer of your equipment and the antenna used"],0,"Using an antenna other than a dipole on 60 meters, you must keep a record of its gain."],["G1C05","What is the limit for transmitter power on the 28 MHz band for a General Class control operator?",["100 watts PEP output","1000 watts PEP output","1500 watts PEP output","2000 watts PEP output"],2,"A General may run 1500 watts PEP output on 10 meters (28 MHz)."],["G1C06","What is the limit for transmitter power on the 1.8 MHz band?",["200 watts PEP output","1000 watts PEP output","1200 watts PEP output","1500 watts PEP output"],3,"160 meters (1.8 MHz) has the standard limit, 1500 watts PEP output."],["G1C07","What must be done before using a new digital protocol on the air?",["Type-certify equipment to FCC standards","Obtain an experimental license from the FCC","Publicly document the technical characteristics of the protocol","Submit a rule-making proposal to the FCC describing the codes and methods of the technique"],2,"A new digital protocol must have its technical characteristics publicly documented before use on the air."],["G1C11","What measurement is specified by FCC rules that regulate maximum power?",["RMS output from the transmitter","RMS input to the antenna","PEP input to the antenna","PEP output from the transmitter"],3,"Power limits are measured as PEP (peak envelope power) output from the transmitter."],["G1D01","Who may receive partial credit for the elements represented by an expired amateur radio license?",["Any person who can demonstrate that they once held an FCC-issued General, Advanced, or Amateur Extra class license that was not revoked by the FCC","Anyone who held an FCC-issued amateur radio license that expired not less than 5 and not more than 15 years ago","Any person who previously held an amateur license issued by another country, but only if that country has a current reciprocal licensing agreement with the FCC","Only persons who once held an FCC issued Novice, Technician, or Technician Plus license"],0,"Anyone who once held a General, Advanced or Extra license that wasn't revoked gets credit for the General (and Extra) elements."],["G1D02","What license examinations may you administer as an accredited Volunteer Examiner holding a General class operator license?",["General and Technician","None, only Amateur Extra class licensees may be accredited","Technician only","Amateur Extra, General, and Technician"],2,"A General-class VE may give only the Technician exam."],["G1D03","On which of the following band segments may you operate if you are a Technician class operator and have an unexpired Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination (CSCE) for General class privileges?",["Only the Technician band segments until your upgrade is posted in the FCC database","Only on the Technician band segments until you have a receipt for the FCC application fee payment","On any General or Technician class band segment","On any General or Technician class band segment except 30 meters and 60 meters"],2,"With a CSCE for General, a Technician may use any General or Technician band segment right away."],["G1D04","Who must observe the administration of a Technician class license examination?",["At least three Volunteer Examiners of General class or higher","At least two Volunteer Examiners of General class or higher","At least two Volunteer Examiners of Technician class or higher","At least three Volunteer Examiners of Technician class"],0,"A Technician exam needs at least three Volunteer Examiners of General class or higher."],["G1D05","When operating a US station by remote control from outside the country, what license is required of the control operator?",["A US operator/primary station license","Only an appropriate US operator/primary license and a special remote station permit from the FCC","Only a license from the foreign country, as long as the call sign includes identification of portable operation in the US","A license from the foreign country and a special remote station permit from the FCC"],0,"Controlling a US station from abroad still requires a US license (apart from reciprocal arrangements for non-US citizens)."],["G1D06","Until an upgrade to General class is shown in the FCC database, when must a Technician licensee identify with “AG” after their call sign?",["Whenever they operate using General class frequency privileges","Whenever they operate on any amateur frequency","Whenever they operate using Technician frequency privileges","A special identifier is not required if their General class license application has been filed with the FCC"],0,"Use \"AG\" after your call sign whenever you use General privileges before the upgrade appears in the FCC database."],["G1D07","Volunteer Examiners are accredited by what organization?",["The Federal Communications Commission","The Universal Licensing System","A Volunteer Examiner Coordinator","The Wireless Telecommunications Bureau"],2,"Volunteer Examiners are accredited by a Volunteer Examiner Coordinator (VEC)."],["G1D08","Which of the following criteria must be met for a non-US citizen to be an accredited Volunteer Examiner?",["The person must be a resident of the US for a minimum of 5 years","The person must hold an FCC granted amateur radio license of General class or above","The person’s home citizenship must be in ITU region 2","None of these choices is correct; a non-US citizen cannot be a Volunteer Examiner"],1,"A non-US citizen can be a VE if they hold an FCC amateur license of General class or above."],["G1D09","How long is a Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination (CSCE) valid for exam element credit?",["30 days","180 days","365 days","For as long as your current license is valid"],2,"A CSCE is valid for exam element credit for 365 days."],["G1D10","What is the minimum age that one must be to qualify as an accredited Volunteer Examiner?",["16 years","18 years","21 years","There is no age limit"],1,"Volunteer Examiners must be at least 18."],["G1D11","What action is required to obtain a new General class license after a previously held license has expired and the two-year grace period has passed?",["They must have a letter from the FCC showing they once held an amateur or commercial license","There are no requirements other than being able to show a copy of the expired license","Contact the FCC to have the license reinstated","The applicant must show proof of the appropriate expired license grant and pass the current Element 2 exam"],3,"Past the two-year grace period, you show proof of the expired license and pass the current Element 2 exam."],["G1D12","When operating a station in South America by remote control over the internet from the US, what regulations apply?",["Those of both the remote station’s country and the FCC","Those of the remote station’s country and the FCC’s third-party regulations","Only those of the remote station’s country","Only those of the FCC"],2,"Operating a station in South America remotely from the US, only that country's rules apply."],["G1E01","Which of the following would disqualify a third party from participating in sending a message via an amateur station?",["The third party’s amateur license has been revoked and not reinstated","The third party is not a US citizen","The third party is speaking in a language other than English","All these choices are correct"],0,"A person whose amateur license was revoked and not reinstated may not be a third party."],["G1E02","When may a 10-meter repeater retransmit the 2-meter signal from a station that has a Technician class control operator?",["Under no circumstances","Only if the station on 10-meters is operating under a Special Temporary Authorization allowing such retransmission","Only during an FCC-declared general state of communications emergency","Only if the 10-meter repeater control operator holds at least a General class license"],3,"Only if the 10-meter repeater's control operator holds at least a General license."],["G1E03","What is required to conduct communications with a digital station operating under automatic control outside the automatic control band segments?",["The station initiating the contact must be under local or remote control","The interrogating transmission must be made by another automatically controlled station","No third-party traffic may be transmitted","The control operator of the interrogating station must hold an Amateur Extra class license"],0,"Outside the automatic segments, the station starting the contact must be under local or remote control."],["G1E04","Which of the following conditions require a licensed amateur radio operator to take specific steps to avoid harmful interference to other users or facilities?",["When operating within one mile of an FCC Monitoring Station","When using a band where the Amateur Service is secondary","When a station is transmitting spread spectrum emissions","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: near an FCC monitoring station, on bands where amateurs are secondary, and with spread spectrum."],["G1E05","What are the restrictions on messages sent to a third party in a country with which there is a Third-Party Agreement?",["They must relate to emergencies or disaster relief","They must be for other licensed amateurs","They must relate to amateur radio, or remarks of a personal character, or messages relating to emergencies or disaster relief","The message must be limited to no longer than 1 minute in duration and the name of the third party must be recorded in the station log"],2,"With a third-party-agreement country, messages must be about amateur radio, personal remarks, or emergencies and disaster relief."],["G1E06","The frequency allocations of which ITU region apply to radio amateurs operating in North and South America?",["Region 4","Region 3","Region 2","Region 1"],2,"North and South America are ITU Region 2."],["G1E07","In what part of the 2.4 GHz band may an amateur station communicate with non-licensed Wi-Fi stations?",["Anywhere in the band","Channels 1 through 4","Channels 42 through 45","No part"],3,"No part. Amateur stations may not communicate with unlicensed Wi-Fi stations."],["G1E08","What is the maximum PEP output allowed for spread spectrum transmissions?",["100 milliwatts","10 watts","100 watts","1500 watts"],1,"Spread spectrum is limited to 10 watts PEP output."],["G1E10","Why should an amateur operator normally avoid transmitting on 14.100, 18.110, 21.150, 24.930 and 28.200 MHz?",["A system of propagation beacon stations operates on those frequencies","A system of automatic digital stations operates on those frequencies","These frequencies are set aside for emergency operations","These frequencies are set aside for bulletins from the FCC"],0,"Those frequencies carry a worldwide system of propagation beacons."],["G1E11","On what bands may automatically controlled stations transmitting RTTY or data emissions communicate with other automatically controlled digital stations?",["On any band segment where digital operation is permitted","Anywhere in the non-phone segments of the 10-meter or shorter wavelength bands","Only in the non-phone Extra Class segments of the bands","Anywhere in the 6-meter or shorter wavelength bands, and in limited segments of some of the HF bands"],3,"On 6 meters and shorter, and in limited segments of some HF bands."],["G1E12","When may third-party messages be transmitted via remote control?",["Under any circumstances in which third party messages are permitted by FCC rules","Under no circumstances except for emergencies","Only when the message is intended for licensed radio amateurs","Only when the message is intended for third parties in areas where licensing is controlled by the FCC"],0,"Third-party messages may go by remote control whenever the rules permit third-party messages."]],"P":{"bands":["Where a General may transmit","<p>General adds most of HF. On <b>160, 60, 30, 17, 12 and 10 meters</b> a General has the whole band. On <b>80, 40, 20 and 15 meters</b> parts are reserved for Amateur Extra (and old Advanced) licensees.</p><p>Those reserved parts sit at the <b>bottom</b> of each segment. Where a General gets only part of a voice segment, it's the <b>upper</b> part: 80 m phone 3.800-4.000, 40 m 7.175-7.300, 20 m 14.225-14.350, 15 m 21.275-21.450 MHz. CW and data: 80 m 3.525-3.600, 40 m 7.025-7.125, 20 m 14.025-14.150, 15 m 21.025-21.200 MHz.</p><p>North and South America are ITU Region 2, whose allocations US hams follow.</p>"],"modes":["Which modes go where","<p>CW is allowed on any frequency your license covers, with only rare exceptions, so a General may send CW across all of 10 meters.</p><p><b>30 meters</b> (10.100-10.150 MHz) is narrow and shared: no phone and no image transmission there, only CW and data.</p><p>On 10 meters, repeaters may operate only <b>above 29.5 MHz</b>. A 10 m repeater's control operator needs General privileges there, so it may relay a Technician's 2 m signal only if that control operator holds General or higher.</p>"],"secondary":["Sharing bands","<p>On some bands amateurs are a <b>secondary</b> user. Secondary means you must not cause harmful interference to the primary users and must accept interference from them.</p><p>You must also take care near FCC monitoring stations and when sending spread spectrum, which can interfere widely.</p>"],"antenna":["Antenna structures","<p>An antenna structure taller than <b>200 feet</b> above ground needs FAA notification and FCC registration, and one near a public-use airport may too, depending on its height and distance.</p><p>Under the FCC's <b>PRB-1</b> ruling, state and local governments may regulate antennas, but they must reasonably accommodate amateur communications and use the minimum practical regulation to serve their legitimate purpose.</p>"],"beacons":["Beacons","<p>A beacon transmits so others can <b>observe propagation and reception</b>. Limits: <b>100 watts</b>, and only one beacon per band from the same location. On HF an automatically controlled beacon must stay in <b>28.20-28.30 MHz</b>.</p><p>The worldwide beacon network (NCDXF/IARU) transmits on 14.100, 18.110, 21.150, 24.930 and 28.200 MHz, so avoid transmitting there.</p>"],"content":["What may be sent","<p>Amateurs talk with amateurs: you may contact hams in any country except those that have told the <b>ITU</b> they object. You may not contact unlicensed devices such as Wi-Fi.</p><p>One-way transmissions are allowed for a few purposes, including <b>Morse code practice</b>. Retransmitting non-amateur stations is prohibited, except occasional US government weather and propagation forecasts and NASA-approved spacecraft communications. Abbreviations and procedural signals are fine as long as they don't obscure the meaning.</p><p>Where the rules are silent, you must follow <b>good engineering and good amateur practice</b>, as the FCC determines it.</p>"],"power":["Power limits","<p>Power is measured as <b>PEP output</b> from the transmitter. The general limit is <b>1500 watts PEP</b>, which applies to a General on 160, 12 and 10 meters, among others.</p><p>Exceptions to know: <b>200 watts PEP on 30 meters</b> (10.100-10.150 MHz) for everyone, and <b>10 watts PEP</b> for spread spectrum.</p>"],"sixty":["60 meters and new digital modes","<p>60 meters (around 5.3-5.4 MHz) is shared with government users. Since 2026 amateurs have a 15 kHz segment (5.3515-5.3665 MHz, limited to 9.15 W ERP) plus four channels (100 W ERP). Signals there may be no wider than <b>2.8 kHz</b>. If you use an antenna other than a half-wave dipole, you must keep a record of its gain, since the limits are in effective radiated power.</p><p>Before using a new digital protocol on the air, its technical characteristics must be <b>publicly documented</b>, so anyone can decode it.</p>"],"upgrade":["Upgrading and exam credit","<p>Pass the General exam and you get a <b>CSCE</b> (Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination). It's good for exam credit for <b>365 days</b>, and with it you may use General privileges right away, before the upgrade shows in the FCC database. Until it does, add <b>\"AG\"</b> after your call sign when using General privileges.</p><p>Someone whose General, Advanced or Extra license expired (and wasn't revoked) gets credit for Element 3 (and Element 4 if it was Extra), but must pass the current Element 2 (Technician) exam.</p>"],"ves":["Volunteer Examiners","<p>Exams are given by a team of at least <b>three Volunteer Examiners</b> (VEs). VEs are accredited by a <b>Volunteer Examiner Coordinator</b> (VEC), must be at least <b>18</b>, and must hold an FCC license of General class or higher. Citizenship doesn't matter.</p><p>A General-class VE may give only the <b>Technician</b> exam. Giving the General exam takes an Advanced or Extra VE, and the Extra exam takes an Extra.</p>"],"remote":["Operating by remote control","<p>The rules follow the <b>transmitter's location</b>. Controlling a US station from abroad still requires a US license (apart from reciprocal arrangements for non-US citizens). Controlling a station in another country over the internet puts you under that country's rules only.</p><p>Third-party messages may go by remote control whenever the rules allow third-party messages at all.</p>"],"thirdparty":["Third-party traffic","<p>A <b>third party</b> is anyone the control operator sends a message for, often but not always an unlicensed person. Almost anyone may take part as a third party. The main exception is a former licensee whose license was <b>revoked</b> (or suspended) and who hasn't been relicensed.</p><p>With a country that has a third-party agreement, messages must be about amateur radio, personal remarks, or emergencies and disaster relief.</p>"],"auto":["Automatically controlled digital stations","<p>A station may run unattended (automatic control) sending RTTY or data on <b>6 meters and shorter</b>, and in small listed segments of some HF bands.</p><p>Outside those segments, an automatic station may answer only when a station under <b>local or remote control</b> starts the contact, and no wider than 500 Hz.</p>"]},"CON":{"G1A01":"bands","G1A02":"modes","G1A03":"modes","G1A05":"bands","G1A06":"secondary","G1A07":"modes","G1A08":"bands","G1A09":"bands","G1A10":"modes","G1A11":"bands","G1B01":"antenna","G1B02":"beacons","G1B03":"beacons","G1B04":"content","G1B05":"content","G1B06":"antenna","G1B07":"content","G1B08":"content","G1B09":"beacons","G1B10":"beacons","G1B11":"content","G1C01":"power","G1C02":"power","G1C03":"sixty","G1C04":"sixty","G1C05":"power","G1C06":"power","G1C07":"sixty","G1C11":"power","G1D01":"upgrade","G1D02":"ves","G1D03":"upgrade","G1D04":"ves","G1D05":"remote","G1D06":"upgrade","G1D07":"ves","G1D08":"ves","G1D09":"upgrade","G1D10":"ves","G1D11":"upgrade","G1D12":"remote","G1E01":"thirdparty","G1E02":"modes","G1E03":"auto","G1E04":"secondary","G1E05":"thirdparty","G1E06":"bands","G1E07":"content","G1E08":"power","G1E10":"beacons","G1E11":"auto","G1E12":"remote"}},{"id":"g2","code":"G2","name":"Operating procedures","exam":5,"G":{"G2A":"Phone: sidebands, breaking in, VOX, ALC","G2B":"Sharing frequencies, band plans, emergencies","G2C":"CW procedure, prosigns, Q signals","G2D":"Volunteer monitors, HF operating","G2E":"Digital modes: RTTY, FT8, Winlink"},"Q":[["G2A01","Which mode is most commonly used for voice communications on frequencies of 14 MHz or higher?",["Upper sideband","Lower sideband","Suppressed sideband","Double sideband"],0,"Upper sideband is the custom for voice on 14 MHz and higher."],["G2A02","Which mode is most commonly used for voice communications on the 160-, 75-, and 40-meter bands?",["Upper sideband","Lower sideband","Suppressed sideband","Double sideband"],1,"Lower sideband is the custom on 160, 75 and 40 meters."],["G2A03","Which mode is most commonly used for SSB voice communications in the VHF and UHF bands?",["Upper sideband","Lower sideband","Suppressed sideband","Double sideband"],0,"SSB voice on VHF and UHF uses upper sideband."],["G2A04","Which mode is most commonly used for voice communications on the 17- and 12-meter bands?",["Upper sideband","Lower sideband","Suppressed sideband","Double sideband"],0,"17 and 12 meters are above 10 MHz, so upper sideband is the custom."],["G2A05","Which mode of voice communication is most commonly used on the HF amateur bands?",["Frequency modulation","Double sideband","Single sideband","Single phase modulation"],2,"Single sideband is the most common voice mode on the HF bands."],["G2A06","Which of the following is an advantage of using single sideband, as compared to other analog voice modes on the HF amateur bands?",["Very high-fidelity voice modulation","Less subject to interference from atmospheric static crashes","Ease of tuning on receive and immunity to impulse noise","Less bandwidth used and greater power efficiency"],3,"SSB uses less bandwidth and is more power-efficient than other analog voice modes."],["G2A07","Which of the following statements is true of single sideband (SSB)?",["Only one sideband and the carrier are transmitted; the other sideband is suppressed","Only one sideband is transmitted; the other sideband and carrier are suppressed","SSB is the only voice mode authorized on the 20-, 15-, and 10-meter amateur bands","SSB is the only voice mode authorized on the 160-, 75-, and 40-meter amateur bands"],1,"SSB sends only one sideband; the other sideband and the carrier are suppressed."],["G2A08","What is the recommended way to break into a phone contact?",["Say “QRZ” several times, followed by your call sign","Say your call sign once","Say “Breaker Breaker”","Say “CQ” followed by the call sign of either station"],1,"To break into a phone contact, say your call sign once."],["G2A09","Why do most amateur stations use lower sideband on the 160-, 75-, and 40-meter bands?",["Lower sideband is more efficient than upper sideband at these frequencies","Lower sideband is the only sideband legal on these frequency bands","Because it is fully compatible with an AM detector","It is commonly accepted amateur practice"],3,"LSB on 160, 75 and 40 meters is simply accepted amateur practice."],["G2A10","Which of the following statements is true of VOX operation versus PTT operation?",["The received signal is more natural sounding","It allows “hands free” operation","It occupies less bandwidth","It provides more power output"],1,"VOX switches your transmitter on when you speak, allowing hands-free operation."],["G2A11","Generally, who should respond to a station in the contiguous 48 states calling “CQ DX”?",["Any caller is welcome to respond","Only stations in Germany","Any stations outside the lower 48 states","Only contest stations"],2,"A lower-48 station calling \"CQ DX\" wants stations outside the lower 48 states."],["G2A12","What control is typically adjusted for proper ALC setting on a single sideband transceiver?",["RF clipping level","Transmit audio or microphone gain","Antenna inductance or capacitance","Attenuator level"],1,"Proper ALC is set by adjusting the transmit audio or microphone gain."],["G2B01","Which of the following is true concerning access to frequencies?",["Nets have priority","QSOs in progress have priority","Except during emergencies, no amateur station has priority access to any frequency","Contest operations should yield to non-contest use of frequencies"],2,"Except in emergencies, no station has priority on any frequency."],["G2B02","What is the first thing you should do if you are communicating with another amateur station and hear a station in distress break in?",["Inform your local emergency coordinator","Acknowledge the station in distress and determine what assistance may be needed","Immediately decrease power to avoid interfering with the station in distress","Immediately cease all transmissions"],1,"Acknowledge the station in distress and find out what help is needed."],["G2B03","What is good amateur practice if propagation changes during a contact creating interference from other stations using the frequency?",["Advise the interfering stations that you are on the frequency and that you have priority","Decrease power and continue to transmit","Attempt to resolve the interference problem with the other stations in a mutually acceptable manner","Switch to the opposite sideband"],2,"When propagation brings in other stations, try to resolve it with them in a mutually acceptable way."],["G2B04","When selecting a CW transmitting frequency, what minimum separation from other stations should be used to minimize interference to stations on adjacent frequencies?",["5 Hz to 50 Hz","150 Hz to 500 Hz","1 kHz to 3 kHz","3 kHz to 6 kHz"],1,"Leave about 150 to 500 Hz between CW signals."],["G2B05","When selecting an SSB transmitting frequency, what minimum separation should be used to minimize interference to stations on adjacent frequencies?",["5 Hz to 50 Hz","150 Hz to 500 Hz","2 kHz to 3 kHz","Approximately 6 kHz"],2,"Leave about 2 to 3 kHz between SSB signals."],["G2B06","How can you avoid harmful interference on an apparently clear frequency before calling CQ on CW or phone?",["Send “QRL?” on CW, followed by your call sign; or, if using phone, ask if the frequency is in use, followed by your call sign","Listen for 2 minutes before calling CQ","Send the letter “V” in Morse code several times and listen for a response, or say “test” several times and listen for a response","Send “QSY” on CW or if using phone, announce “the frequency is in use,” then give your call sign and listen for a response"],0,"Before calling CQ, send \"QRL?\" on CW, or ask if the frequency is in use on phone, then your call sign."],["G2B07","Which of the following complies with commonly accepted amateur practice when choosing a frequency on which to initiate a call?",["Listen on the frequency for at least two minutes to be sure it is clear","Identify your station by transmitting your call sign at least 3 times","Follow the voluntary band plan","All these choices are correct"],2,"Follow the voluntary band plan when choosing a frequency."],["G2B08","What is the voluntary band plan restriction for US stations transmitting within the 48 contiguous states in the 50.1 MHz to 50.125 MHz band segment?",["Only contacts with stations not within the 48 contiguous states","Only contacts with other stations within the 48 contiguous states","Only digital contacts","Only SSTV contacts"],0,"In the lower 48, 50.100 to 50.125 MHz is kept for contacts with stations outside the lower 48."],["G2B09","Who may be the control operator of an amateur station transmitting in RACES to assist relief operations during a disaster?",["Only a person holding an FCC-issued amateur operator license","Only a RACES net control operator","A person holding an FCC-issued amateur operator license or an appropriate government official","Any control operator when normal communication systems are operational"],0,"Only a person holding an FCC-issued amateur license may be control operator of a RACES station."],["G2B10","Which of the following is good amateur practice for net management?",["Always use multiple sets of phonetics during check-in","Have a backup frequency in case of interference or poor conditions","Transmit the full net roster at the beginning of every session","All these choices are correct"],1,"Have a backup frequency in case of interference or poor conditions."],["G2B11","How often may RACES training drills and tests be routinely conducted without special authorization?",["No more than 1 hour per month","No more than 2 hours per month","No more than 1 hour per week","No more than 2 hours per week"],2,"Routine RACES drills and tests may run up to one hour per week."],["G2C01","Which of the following describes full break-in CW operation (QSK)?",["Breaking stations send the Morse code prosign “BK”","Automatic keyers, instead of hand keys, are used to send Morse code","An operator must activate a manual send/receive switch before and after every transmission","Transmitting stations can receive between code characters and elements"],3,"With full break-in (QSK), the transmitting station can hear between code characters and elements."],["G2C02","What should you do if a CW station sends “QRS?”",["Send slower","Change frequency","Increase your power","Repeat everything twice"],0,"\"QRS?\" asks you to send slower."],["G2C03","What does it mean when a CW operator sends “KN” at the end of a transmission?",["No US stations should call","Operating full break-in","Listening only for a specific station or stations","Closing station now"],2,"KN at the end means \"go ahead, only the station I'm in contact with.\""],["G2C04","What does the Q signal “QRL?” mean?",["“Will you keep the frequency clear?”","“Are you operating full break-in?” or “Can you operate full break-in?”","“Are you listening only for a specific station?”","“Are you busy?” or “Is this frequency in use?”"],3,"\"QRL?\" asks \"Are you busy?\" or \"Is this frequency in use?\""],["G2C05","What is the best speed to use when answering a CQ in Morse code?",["The fastest speed at which you are comfortable copying, but no slower than the CQ","The fastest speed at which you are comfortable copying, but no faster than the CQ","At the standard calling speed of 10 wpm","At the standard calling speed of 5 wpm"],1,"Answer a CQ at the fastest speed you can copy comfortably, but no faster than the CQ."],["G2C06","What does the term “zero beat” mean in CW operation?",["Matching the speed of the transmitting station","Operating split to avoid interference on frequency","Sending without error","Matching the transmit frequency to the frequency of a received signal"],3,"Zero beat means matching your transmit frequency to the received signal's frequency."],["G2C07","When sending CW, what does a “C” mean when added to the RST report?",["Chirpy or unstable signal","Report was read from an S meter rather than estimated","100 percent copy","Key clicks"],0,"A \"C\" added to an RST report means the signal is chirpy or unstable."],["G2C08","What prosign is sent to indicate the end of a formal message when using CW?",["SK","BK","AR","KN"],2,"AR marks the end of a formal message on CW."],["G2C09","What does the Q signal “QSL” mean?",["Send slower","We have already confirmed the contact","I have received and understood","We have worked before"],2,"\"QSL\" means \"I have received and understood.\""],["G2C10","What does the Q signal “QRN” mean?",["Send more slowly","Stop sending","Zero beat my signal","I am troubled by static"],3,"\"QRN\" means \"I am troubled by static.\""],["G2C11","What does the Q signal “QRV” mean?",["You are sending too fast","There is interference on the frequency","I am quitting for the day","I am ready to receive"],3,"\"QRV\" means \"I am ready to receive.\""],["G2D01","What is the Volunteer Monitor Program?",["Amateur volunteers who are formally enlisted to monitor the airwaves for rules violations","Amateur volunteers who conduct amateur licensing examinations","Amateur volunteers who conduct frequency coordination for amateur VHF repeaters","Amateur volunteers who use their station equipment to help civil defense organizations in times of emergency"],0,"The Volunteer Monitor Program is formally enlisted amateur volunteers who monitor the airwaves for rules violations."],["G2D02","Which of the following are objectives of the Volunteer Monitor Program?",["To conduct efficient and orderly amateur licensing examinations","To provide emergency and public safety communications","To coordinate repeaters for efficient and orderly spectrum usage","To encourage amateur radio operators to self-regulate and comply with the rules"],3,"Its objective is to encourage amateurs to self-regulate and comply with the rules."],["G2D03","What procedure may be used by Volunteer Monitors to localize a station whose continuous carrier is holding a repeater on in their area?",["Compare vertical and horizontal signal strengths on the input frequency","Compare beam headings on the repeater input from their home locations with that of other Volunteer Monitors","Compare signal strengths between the input and output of the repeater","All these choices are correct"],1,"Monitors compare beam headings on the repeater's input from their locations with those of other monitors."],["G2D04","Which of the following describes an azimuthal projection map?",["A map that shows accurate land masses","A map that shows true bearings and distances from a specific location","A map that shows the angle at which an amateur satellite crosses the equator","A map that shows the number of degrees longitude that an amateur satellite appears to move westward at the equator with each orbit"],1,"An azimuthal projection map shows true bearings and distances from one specific location."],["G2D05","Which of the following indicates that you are looking for an HF contact with any station?",["Sign your call sign once, followed by the words “listening for a call” -- if no answer, change frequency and repeat","Say “QTC” followed by “this is” and your call sign -- if no answer, change frequency and repeat","Repeat “CQ” a few times, followed by “this is,” then your call sign a few times, then pause to listen, repeat as necessary","Transmit an unmodulated carried for approximately 10 seconds, followed by “this is” and your call sign, and pause to listen -- repeat as necessary"],2,"Call CQ a few times, then \"this is\" and your call sign a few times, then listen."],["G2D06","How is a directional antenna pointed when making a “long-path” contact with another station?",["Toward the rising sun","Along the gray line","180 degrees from the station’s short-path heading","Toward the north"],2,"For long path, point the beam 180 degrees from the short-path heading."],["G2D07","Which of the following are examples of the NATO Phonetic Alphabet?",["Able, Baker, Charlie, Dog","Adam, Boy, Charles, David","America, Boston, Canada, Denmark","Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta"],3,"Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta are NATO phonetics."],["G2D08","Why do many amateurs keep a station log?",["The FCC requires a log of all international contacts","The FCC requires a log of all international third-party traffic","The log provides evidence of operation needed to renew a license without retest","To help with a reply if the FCC requests information about your station"],3,"A log helps you answer if the FCC asks about your station."],["G2D09","Which of the following is required when participating in a contest on HF frequencies?",["Submit a log to the contest sponsor","Send a QSL card to the stations worked, or QSL via Logbook of The World","Identify your station according to normal FCC regulations","All these choices are correct"],2,"In a contest you must still identify your station according to normal FCC rules."],["G2D10","What is QRP operation?",["Remote piloted model control","Low-power transmit operation","Transmission using Quick Response Protocol","Traffic relay procedure net operation"],1,"QRP means low-power operation."],["G2D11","Why are signal reports typically exchanged at the beginning of an HF contact?",["To allow each station to operate according to conditions","To be sure the contact will count for award programs","To follow standard radiogram structure","To allow each station to calibrate their frequency display"],0,"Signal reports at the start of a contact let each station adjust to conditions."],["G2E01","Which mode is normally used when sending RTTY signals via AFSK with an SSB transmitter?",["USB","DSB","CW","LSB"],3,"RTTY by AFSK through an SSB transmitter normally uses LSB."],["G2E02","What is VARA?",["A low signal-to-noise digital mode used for EME (moonbounce)","A digital protocol used with Winlink","A radio direction finding system used on VHF and UHF","A DX spotting system using a network of software defined radios"],1,"VARA is a digital protocol used with Winlink."],["G2E03","What symptoms may result from other signals interfering with a PACTOR or VARA transmission?",["Frequent retries or timeouts","Long pauses in message transmission","Failure to establish a connection between stations","All these choices are correct"],3,"Interference on PACTOR or VARA can cause retries, timeouts, long pauses and failed connections."],["G2E04","Which of the following is good practice when choosing a transmitting frequency to answer a station calling CQ using FT8?",["Always call on the station’s frequency","Call on any frequency in the waterfall except the station’s frequency","Find a clear frequency during the same time slot as the calling station","Find a clear frequency during the alternate time slot to the calling station"],3,"Answer an FT8 CQ on a clear frequency, in the alternate time slot to the calling station."],["G2E05","What is the standard sideband for JT65, JT9, FT4, or FT8 digital signal when using AFSK?",["LSB","USB","DSB","SSB"],1,"FT8, FT4, JT65 and JT9 use upper sideband with AFSK."],["G2E06","What is the most common frequency shift for RTTY emissions in the amateur HF bands?",["85 Hz","170 Hz","425 Hz","850 Hz"],1,"The usual RTTY shift on HF is 170 Hz."],["G2E07","Which of the following is required when using FT8?",["A special hardware modem","Computer time accurate to within approximately 1 second","Receiver attenuator set to -12 dB","A vertically polarized antenna"],1,"FT8 requires your computer's clock to be accurate to within about one second."],["G2E08","In what segment of the 20-meter band are most digital mode operations commonly found?",["At the bottom of the slow-scan TV segment, near 14.230 MHz","At the top of the SSB phone segment, near 14.325 MHz","In the middle of the CW segment, near 14.100 MHz","Between 14.070 MHz and 14.100 MHz"],3,"Most 20-meter digital activity is between 14.070 and 14.100 MHz."],["G2E09","How do you join a contact between two stations using the PACTOR protocol?",["Send broadcast packets containing your call sign while in MONITOR mode","Transmit a steady carrier until the PACTOR protocol times out and disconnects","Joining an existing contact is not possible, PACTOR connections are limited to two stations","Send a NAK code"],2,"You can't join a PACTOR contact; PACTOR links only two stations."],["G2E10","Which of the following is a way to establish contact with a digital messaging system gateway station?",["Send an email to the system control operator","Send QRL in Morse code","Respond when the station broadcasts its SSID","Transmit a connect message on the station’s published frequency"],3,"To reach a digital messaging gateway, transmit a connect message on its published frequency."],["G2E11","What is the primary purpose of an Amateur Radio Emergency Data Network (AREDN) mesh network?",["To provide FM repeater coverage in remote areas","To provide real time propagation data by monitoring amateur radio transmissions worldwide","To provide high-speed data services during an emergency or community event","To provide DX spotting reports to aid contesters and DXers"],2,"AREDN mesh networks provide high-speed data services during emergencies and community events."],["G2E12","Which of the following describes Winlink?",["An amateur radio wireless network to send and receive email on the internet","A form of Packet Radio","A wireless network capable of both VHF and HF band operation","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: Winlink sends internet email by radio, can use packet, and works on VHF and HF."],["G2E13","What is another name for a Winlink Remote Message Server?",["Terminal Node Controller","Gateway","RJ-45","Printer/Server"],1,"A Winlink Remote Message Server is also called a gateway."],["G2E14","What could be wrong if you cannot decode an RTTY or other FSK signal even though it is apparently tuned in properly?",["The mark and space frequencies may be reversed","You may have selected the wrong baud rate","You may be listening on the wrong sideband","All these choices are correct"],3,"Reversed mark and space, the wrong baud rate, or the wrong sideband can each stop an RTTY signal decoding."],["G2E15","Which of the following is a common location for FT8?",["Anywhere in the voice portion of the band","Anywhere in the CW portion of the band","Approximately 14.074 MHz to 14.077 MHz","Approximately 14.110 MHz to 14.113 MHz"],2,"On 20 meters, FT8 is found around 14.074 to 14.077 MHz."]],"P":{"sideband":["Which sideband","<p><b>Single sideband</b> (SSB) is the usual HF voice mode. It sends one sideband and suppresses the carrier and the other sideband, so it uses less bandwidth and puts all its power into speech.</p><p>By long-standing custom, not law: <b>lower sideband (LSB) below 10 MHz</b> (160, 75/80 and 40 meters; 60 meters is the exception and uses USB) and <b>upper sideband (USB) on 14 MHz and up</b>, including 17 and 12 meters and SSB on VHF and UHF.</p>"],"phoneops":["Getting on the air by voice","<p>To join a contact in progress, say your call sign <b>once</b> between transmissions. A station calling <b>\"CQ DX\"</b> from the lower 48 states wants stations outside them.</p><p><b>VOX</b> switches to transmit when you speak, for hands-free operation. To set the <b>ALC</b> (automatic level control) properly, adjust the microphone or transmit audio gain.</p>"],"frequency":["Sharing frequencies","<p>No station has priority on any frequency, except that emergency communications always come first. When conditions change and stations start overlapping, work it out politely with them.</p><p>Leave room: about <b>150 to 500 Hz</b> from other CW signals and <b>2 to 3 kHz</b> from SSB signals. Before calling CQ, ask whether the frequency is in use: <b>\"QRL?\"</b> on CW, or \"Is this frequency in use?\" on phone, followed by your call sign. Follow the voluntary <b>band plans</b>, such as keeping 50.100-50.125 MHz for contacts with stations outside the lower 48 states.</p>"],"emergency":["Distress, nets and RACES","<p>If a station in distress breaks in, <b>acknowledge it</b> and find out what help is needed.</p><p><b>RACES</b> (Radio Amateur Civil Emergency Service) operates for civil defense organizations. Its control operators must hold an FCC-issued amateur license, and routine drills are limited to <b>one hour a week</b>. Good nets keep a backup frequency ready.</p>"],"cw":["CW procedure","<p><b>Full break-in</b> (QSK) lets you hear between the dots and dashes you send. Answer a CQ at a speed you can copy comfortably, <b>no faster</b> than the CQ. <b>Zero beat</b> means matching your transmit frequency to the station you hear.</p><p>Prosigns: <b>AR</b> ends a formal message, <b>KN</b> means \"go ahead, only the station I'm working\". A <b>C</b> after an RST report means the signal is chirpy.</p>"],"qsignals":["Q signals","<p>Q signals are three-letter shorthand; with a question mark they ask, without they tell. <b>QRS</b>: send slower. <b>QRL?</b>: is this frequency in use? <b>QSL</b>: received and understood. <b>QRN</b>: troubled by static. <b>QRV</b>: ready to receive. <b>QRP</b>: low power.</p>"],"monitor":["Volunteer Monitor Program","<p>The Volunteer Monitor Program is run by the ARRL under an agreement with the FCC. Formally enlisted volunteers <b>monitor the bands for rule violations</b>, aiming to encourage hams to <b>self-regulate</b> and comply. To find a station holding a repeater on, monitors compare beam headings on the repeater's input from different locations.</p>"],"hf":["HF operating","<p>Call <b>CQ</b> a few times, then \"this is\" and your call sign a few times, then listen. Use the <b>NATO phonetic alphabet</b> (Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta). For a <b>long-path</b> contact, point the beam 180 degrees from the short-path heading. An <b>azimuthal projection map</b>, centered on your location, shows true bearings and distances.</p><p>Logs aren't required, but help if the FCC asks about your station. In contests, normal FCC identification rules still apply. Signal reports early in a contact let each station adjust to conditions.</p>"],"rtty":["RTTY","<p>RTTY sent by AFSK through an SSB transmitter normally uses <b>lower sideband</b>, with a shift of <b>170 Hz</b> between mark and space. If a tuned-in RTTY signal won't decode, check for reversed mark and space, the wrong baud rate, or the wrong sideband.</p><p>Most HF digital activity on 20 meters sits between <b>14.070 and 14.100 MHz</b>.</p>"],"winlink":["Winlink and mesh networks","<p><b>Winlink</b> sends and receives email over amateur radio, on HF and VHF, including by packet radio. Its stations are gateways (Remote Message Servers); you reach one by transmitting a connect request on its published frequency. <b>VARA</b> and <b>PACTOR</b> are protocols it uses, and PACTOR links just two stations. Interference shows up as retries, pauses and failed connections.</p><p><b>AREDN</b> mesh networks carry high-speed data for emergencies and community events.</p>"],"ft8":["FT8 and its relatives","<p>FT8, FT4, JT65 and JT9 are sent with <b>upper sideband</b>. Stations take turns in 15-second time slots, so FT8 needs your computer clock right to within about <b>one second</b>. To answer a CQ, pick a clear spot and transmit in the <b>other</b> time slot. On 20 meters FT8 lives around <b>14.074 MHz</b>.</p>"]},"CON":{"G2A01":"sideband","G2A02":"sideband","G2A03":"sideband","G2A04":"sideband","G2A05":"sideband","G2A06":"sideband","G2A07":"sideband","G2A08":"phoneops","G2A09":"sideband","G2A10":"phoneops","G2A11":"phoneops","G2A12":"phoneops","G2B01":"frequency","G2B02":"emergency","G2B03":"frequency","G2B04":"frequency","G2B05":"frequency","G2B06":"frequency","G2B07":"frequency","G2B08":"frequency","G2B09":"emergency","G2B10":"emergency","G2B11":"emergency","G2C01":"cw","G2C02":"qsignals","G2C03":"cw","G2C04":"qsignals","G2C05":"cw","G2C06":"cw","G2C07":"cw","G2C08":"cw","G2C09":"qsignals","G2C10":"qsignals","G2C11":"qsignals","G2D01":"monitor","G2D02":"monitor","G2D03":"monitor","G2D04":"hf","G2D05":"hf","G2D06":"hf","G2D07":"hf","G2D08":"hf","G2D09":"hf","G2D10":"hf","G2D11":"hf","G2E01":"rtty","G2E02":"winlink","G2E03":"winlink","G2E04":"ft8","G2E05":"ft8","G2E06":"rtty","G2E07":"ft8","G2E08":"rtty","G2E09":"winlink","G2E10":"winlink","G2E11":"winlink","G2E12":"winlink","G2E13":"winlink","G2E14":"rtty","G2E15":"ft8"}},{"id":"g3","code":"G3","name":"Radio wave propagation","exam":3,"G":{"G3A":"The Sun, storms and the indices","G3B":"MUF, LUF, hops and paths","G3C":"Ionospheric regions, scatter, NVIS"},"Q":[["G3A01","How does a higher sunspot number affect HF propagation?",["Higher sunspot numbers generally indicate a greater probability of good propagation at higher frequencies","Lower sunspot numbers generally indicate greater probability of sporadic E propagation","A zero sunspot number indicates that radio propagation is not possible on any band","A zero sunspot number indicates undisturbed conditions"],0,"Higher sunspot numbers mean a better chance of good propagation on the higher frequencies."],["G3A02","What effect does a sudden ionospheric disturbance have on the daytime ionospheric propagation?",["It enhances propagation on all HF frequencies","It disrupts signals on lower frequencies more than those on higher frequencies","It disrupts communications via satellite more than direct communications","None, because only areas on the night side of the Earth are affected"],1,"A sudden ionospheric disturbance disrupts lower frequencies more than higher ones."],["G3A03","Approximately how long does it take the increased ultraviolet and X-ray radiation from a solar flare to affect radio propagation on Earth?",["28 days","1 to 2 hours","8 minutes","20 to 40 hours"],2,"About 8 minutes: flare radiation travels at the speed of light."],["G3A04","Which of the following are the least reliable bands for long-distance communications during periods of low solar activity?",["80 meters and 160 meters","60 meters and 40 meters","30 meters and 20 meters","15 meters, 12 meters, and 10 meters"],3,"15, 12 and 10 meters are the least reliable for long distance when solar activity is low."],["G3A05","What is the solar flux index?",["A measure of the highest frequency that is useful for ionospheric propagation between two points on Earth","A count of sunspots that is adjusted for solar emissions","Another name for the American sunspot number","A measure of solar radiation with a wavelength of 10.7 centimeters"],3,"The solar flux index measures solar radiation at a wavelength of 10.7 centimeters."],["G3A06","What is a geomagnetic storm?",["A sudden drop in the solar flux index","A thunderstorm that affects radio propagation","Ripples in the geomagnetic force","A temporary disturbance in Earth’s geomagnetic field"],3,"A geomagnetic storm is a temporary disturbance in Earth's magnetic field."],["G3A07","At what point in the solar cycle does the 20-meter band usually support worldwide propagation during daylight hours?",["At the summer solstice","Only at the maximum point","Only at the minimum point","At any point"],3,"20 meters usually supports worldwide daytime propagation at any point in the solar cycle."],["G3A08","How can a geomagnetic storm affect HF propagation?",["Improve high-latitude HF propagation","Degrade ground wave propagation","Improve ground wave propagation","Degrade high-latitude HF propagation"],3,"A geomagnetic storm degrades HF propagation at high latitudes."],["G3A09","How can high geomagnetic activity benefit radio communications?",["Creates auroras that can reflect VHF signals","Increases signal strength for HF signals passing through the polar regions","Improve HF long path propagation","Reduce long delayed echoes"],0,"High geomagnetic activity creates auroras, which can reflect VHF signals."],["G3A10","What causes HF propagation conditions to vary periodically in a 26- to 28-day cycle?",["Long term oscillations in the upper atmosphere","Cyclic variation in Earth’s radiation belts","Rotation of the Sun’s surface layers around its axis","The position of the Moon in its orbit"],2,"The 26- to 28-day cycle comes from the Sun's rotation."],["G3A11","How long does it take a coronal mass ejection to affect radio propagation on Earth?",["28 days","14 days","4 to 8 minutes","15 hours to several days"],3,"A coronal mass ejection takes 15 hours to several days to affect propagation on Earth."],["G3A12","What does the K-index measure?",["The relative position of sunspots on the surface of the Sun","The short-term stability of Earth’s geomagnetic field","The short-term stability of the Sun’s magnetic field","The solar radio flux at Boulder, Colorado"],1,"The K-index measures the short-term stability of Earth's geomagnetic field."],["G3A13","What does the A-index measure?",["The relative position of sunspots on the surface of the Sun","The amount of polarization of the Sun’s electric field","The long-term stability of Earth’s geomagnetic field","The solar radio flux at Boulder, Colorado"],2,"The A-index measures the long-term stability of Earth's geomagnetic field."],["G3A14","How is long distance radio communication usually affected by the charged particles that reach Earth from solar coronal holes?",["HF communication is improved","HF communication is disturbed","VHF/UHF ducting is improved","VHF/UHF ducting is disturbed"],1,"Charged particles from coronal holes usually disturb HF communication."],["G3B01","What is a characteristic of skywave signals arriving at your location by both short-path and long-path propagation?",["Periodic fading approximately every 10 seconds","Signal strength increased by 3 dB","The signal might be cancelled causing severe attenuation","A slightly delayed echo might be heard"],3,"When a signal arrives by both short and long path, you may hear a slightly delayed echo."],["G3B02","What factors affect the MUF?",["Path distance and location","Time of day and season","Solar radiation and ionospheric disturbances","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: path distance and location, time of day and season, and solar radiation and disturbances affect the MUF."],["G3B03","Which frequency will have the least attenuation for long-distance skip propagation?",["Just below the MUF","Just above the LUF","Just below the critical frequency","Just above the critical frequency"],0,"The least attenuation for long-distance skip is just below the MUF."],["G3B04","Which of the following is a way to determine current propagation on a desired band from your station?",["Use a network of automated receiving stations on the internet to see where your transmissions are being received","Check the A-index","Send a series of dots and listen for echoes","All these choices are correct"],0,"Use a network of automated receiving stations on the internet to see where your signal is heard."],["G3B05","How does the ionosphere affect radio waves with frequencies below the MUF and above the LUF?",["They are refracted back to Earth","They pass through the ionosphere","They are amplified by interaction with the ionosphere","They are refracted and trapped in the ionosphere to circle Earth"],0,"Frequencies between the LUF and the MUF are refracted back to Earth."],["G3B06","What usually happens to radio waves with frequencies below the LUF?",["They are refracted back to Earth","They pass through the ionosphere","They are attenuated before reaching the destination","They are refracted and trapped in the ionosphere to circle Earth"],2,"Below the LUF, signals are absorbed before they reach their destination."],["G3B07","What does LUF stand for?",["The Lowest Usable Frequency for communications between two specific points","The Lowest Usable Frequency for communications to any point outside a 100-mile radius","The Lowest Usable Frequency during a 24-hour period","The Lowest Usable Frequency during the past 60 minutes"],0,"LUF is the Lowest Usable Frequency for communications between two specific points."],["G3B08","What does MUF stand for?",["The Minimum Usable Frequency for communications between two points","The Maximum Usable Frequency for communications between two points","The Minimum Usable Frequency during a 24-hour period","The Maximum Usable Frequency during a 24-hour period"],1,"MUF is the Maximum Usable Frequency for communications between two points."],["G3B09","What is the approximate maximum distance along the Earth’s surface normally covered in one hop using the F2 region?",["180 miles","1,200 miles","2,500 miles","12,000 miles"],2,"One F2-region hop covers up to about 2,500 miles."],["G3B10","What is the approximate maximum distance along the Earth’s surface normally covered in one hop using the E region?",["180 miles","1,200 miles","2,500 miles","12,000 miles"],1,"One E-region hop covers up to about 1,200 miles."],["G3B11","What happens to HF propagation when the LUF exceeds the MUF?",["Propagation via ordinary skywave communications is not possible over that path","HF communications over the path are enhanced","Double-hop propagation along the path is more common","Propagation over the path on all HF frequencies is enhanced"],0,"When the LUF exceeds the MUF, ordinary skywave over that path is impossible."],["G3B12","Which of the following is typical of the lower HF frequencies during the summer?",["Poor propagation at any time of day","World-wide propagation during daylight hours","Heavy distortion on signals due to photon absorption","High levels of atmospheric noise or static"],3,"In summer the lower HF bands carry high levels of atmospheric noise, or static."],["G3C01","Which ionospheric region is closest to the surface of Earth?",["The D region","The E region","The F1 region","The F2 region"],0,"The D region is closest to Earth's surface."],["G3C02","What is meant by the term “critical frequency” at a given incidence angle?",["The highest frequency which is refracted back to Earth","The lowest frequency which is refracted back to Earth","The frequency at which the signal-to-noise ratio approaches unity","The frequency at which the signal-to-noise ratio is 6 dB"],0,"The critical frequency is the highest frequency refracted back to Earth at a given angle."],["G3C03","Why is skip propagation via the F2 region longer than that via the other ionospheric regions?",["Because it is the densest","Because of the Doppler effect","Because it is the highest","Because of temperature inversions"],2,"F2 skip is longer because the F2 region is the highest."],["G3C04","What does the term “critical angle” mean, as applied to radio wave propagation?",["The long path azimuth of a distant station","The short path azimuth of a distant station","The lowest takeoff angle that will return a radio wave to Earth under specific ionospheric conditions","The highest takeoff angle that will return a radio wave to Earth under specific ionospheric conditions"],3,"The critical angle is the highest takeoff angle that will still return a wave to Earth."],["G3C05","Why is long-distance communication on the 40-, 60-, 80-, and 160-meter bands more difficult during the day?",["The F region absorbs signals at these frequencies during daylight hours","The F region is unstable during daylight hours","The D region absorbs signals at these frequencies during daylight hours","The E region is unstable during daylight hours"],2,"By day the D region absorbs signals on 40, 60, 80 and 160 meters."],["G3C06","What is a characteristic of HF scatter?",["Phone signals have high intelligibility","Signals have a fluttering sound","There are very large, sudden swings in signal strength","Scatter propagation occurs only at night"],1,"HF scatter signals have a fluttering sound."],["G3C07","What makes HF scatter signals often sound distorted?",["The ionospheric region involved is unstable","Ground waves are absorbing much of the signal","The E region is not present","Energy is scattered into the skip zone through several different paths"],3,"Scatter sounds distorted because energy arrives by several different paths into the skip zone."],["G3C08","Why are HF scatter signals in the skip zone usually weak?",["Only a small part of the signal energy is scattered into the skip zone","Signals are scattered from the magnetosphere, which is not a good reflector","Propagation is via ground waves, which absorb most of the signal energy","Propagation is via ducts in the F region, which absorb most of the energy"],0,"Scatter signals are weak because only a small part of the energy is scattered into the skip zone."],["G3C09","What type of propagation allows signals to be heard in the transmitting station’s skip zone?",["Faraday rotation","Scatter","Chordal hop","Short-path"],1,"Scatter lets signals be heard in the transmitting station's skip zone."],["G3C10","What is near vertical incidence skywave (NVIS) propagation?",["Propagation near the MUF","Short distance MF or HF propagation at high elevation angles","Long path HF propagation at sunrise and sunset","Double hop propagation near the LUF"],1,"NVIS is short-distance MF or HF propagation at high elevation angles."],["G3C11","Which ionospheric region is the most absorbent of signals below 10 MHz during daylight hours?",["The F2 region","The F1 region","The E region","The D region"],3,"The D region is the most absorbent of signals below 10 MHz in daylight."]],"P":{"sun":["The Sun drives HF","<p>Ultraviolet light from the Sun ionizes the upper atmosphere, and the ionosphere bends HF signals back to Earth. More <b>sunspots</b> mean more ionization, so higher frequencies work: 15, 12 and 10 meters shine near solar maximum and are the least reliable near minimum. 20 meters supports daytime worldwide contacts at <b>any point</b> in the roughly 11-year cycle.</p><p>The Sun turns about once every <b>27 days</b> as seen from Earth, so active regions, and the conditions they bring, tend to return on a 26- to 28-day cycle.</p>"],"disturb":["Solar disturbances","<p>A <b>solar flare</b>'s ultraviolet and X-rays reach Earth in about <b>8 minutes</b>, at the speed of light. They can cause a <b>sudden ionospheric disturbance</b>: the daytime D region absorbs hard, wiping out lower frequencies more than higher ones.</p><p>Charged particles travel slower. A <b>coronal mass ejection</b> arrives <b>15 hours to several days</b> later, and particles from <b>coronal holes</b> also disturb HF. They cause <b>geomagnetic storms</b>, temporary disturbances of Earth's magnetic field that degrade HF, especially at high latitudes. The upside: <b>auroras</b> that can reflect VHF signals.</p>"],"indices":["Measuring conditions","<p>The <b>solar flux index</b> measures the Sun's radio output at a wavelength of <b>10.7 cm</b>; higher is better for HF. The <b>K-index</b> measures the short-term stability of Earth's geomagnetic field (every three hours), and the <b>A-index</b> the long-term stability (a daily figure). For both, lower is quieter and better.</p><p>To see where your own signal is getting out right now, check a network of automated receiving stations on the internet, such as the Reverse Beacon Network or PSK Reporter.</p>"],"paths":["Hops and paths","<p>One hop off the <b>F2 region</b> covers up to about <b>2,500 miles</b>; one off the lower <b>E region</b>, about <b>1,200 miles</b>. Farther takes multiple hops.</p><p>A signal can arrive both the short way around the Earth and the long way. When both arrive, the long-path one comes slightly later, so you may hear an echo.</p>"],"muf":["MUF and LUF","<p>Between two points, the <b>MUF</b> (Maximum Usable Frequency) is the highest frequency the ionosphere will return, and the <b>LUF</b> (Lowest Usable Frequency) the lowest that gets through without being absorbed. Between them, signals are refracted back to Earth. Above the MUF they pass into space; below the LUF they're absorbed before arriving.</p><p>The MUF changes with path distance and location, time of day, season, solar radiation and disturbances. The strongest long-distance signals are <b>just below the MUF</b>. If the LUF rises above the MUF, ordinary skywave over that path is impossible.</p>"],"layers":["The ionosphere's regions","<p>From the ground up: the <b>D</b> region (lowest), then <b>E</b>, then <b>F1</b> and <b>F2</b> (highest). The D region forms in daylight, largely disappears at night, and <b>absorbs</b> signals below about 10 MHz, which is why 160, 80, 60 and 40 meters are short-range by day and open up at night. F2 is the highest, so it gives the longest skip.</p><p>In summer, thunderstorms fill the lower HF bands with static.</p>"],"critical":["Angles and NVIS","<p>The <b>critical frequency</b> is the highest frequency refracted back to Earth at a given angle. Ionosondes measure it with signals sent straight up; at lower angles higher frequencies return, which is why the MUF over a long path is well above it. The <b>critical angle</b> is the highest takeoff angle that still returns a signal to Earth.</p><p><b>NVIS</b> (Near Vertical Incidence Skywave) sends MF or HF signals nearly straight up so they come down close by, covering a region a few hundred miles across with no skip zone.</p>"],"scatter":["Scatter","<p>Between the end of ground wave and the first skywave hop lies the <b>skip zone</b>. Stations there can sometimes still be heard by <b>scatter</b>: a small part of the signal is scattered into the zone by several paths at once. So scatter signals are <b>weak</b>, <b>flutter</b> and often sound distorted.</p>"]},"CON":{"G3A01":"sun","G3A02":"disturb","G3A03":"disturb","G3A04":"sun","G3A05":"indices","G3A06":"disturb","G3A07":"sun","G3A08":"disturb","G3A09":"disturb","G3A10":"sun","G3A11":"disturb","G3A12":"indices","G3A13":"indices","G3A14":"disturb","G3B01":"paths","G3B02":"muf","G3B03":"muf","G3B04":"indices","G3B05":"muf","G3B06":"muf","G3B07":"muf","G3B08":"muf","G3B09":"paths","G3B10":"paths","G3B11":"muf","G3B12":"layers","G3C01":"layers","G3C02":"critical","G3C03":"layers","G3C04":"critical","G3C05":"layers","G3C06":"scatter","G3C07":"scatter","G3C08":"scatter","G3C09":"scatter","G3C10":"critical","G3C11":"layers"}},{"id":"g4","code":"G4","name":"Amateur radio practices","exam":5,"G":{"G4A":"Station controls and amplifiers","G4B":"Test equipment","G4C":"Interference, grounding and bonding","G4D":"Speech processors, S meters, band edges","G4E":"Mobile, portable and solar power"},"Q":[["G4A01","What is the purpose of the notch filter found on many HF transceivers?",["To restrict the transmitter voice bandwidth","To reduce interference from carriers in the receiver passband","To eliminate receiver interference from impulse noise sources","To remove interfering splatter generated by signals on adjacent frequencies"],1,"A notch filter reduces interference from carriers in the receiver passband."],["G4A02","What is the benefit of using the opposite or “reverse” sideband when receiving CW?",["Interference from impulse noise will be eliminated","More stations can be accommodated within a given signal passband","It may be possible to reduce or eliminate interference from other signals","Accidental out-of-band operation can be prevented"],2,"Receiving CW on the opposite sideband may reduce or eliminate interference from other signals."],["G4A03","How does a noise blanker work?",["By temporarily increasing received bandwidth","By redirecting noise pulses into a filter capacitor","By reducing receiver gain during a noise pulse","By clipping noise peaks"],2,"A noise blanker reduces receiver gain during each noise pulse."],["G4A04","What is the effect on plate current of the correct setting of a vacuum-tube RF power amplifier’s TUNE control?",["A pronounced peak","A pronounced dip","No change will be observed","A slow, rhythmic oscillation"],1,"When a tube amplifier's TUNE control is set correctly, plate current shows a pronounced dip."],["G4A05","Why is automatic level control (ALC) used with an RF power amplifier?",["To balance the transmitter audio frequency response","To reduce harmonic radiation","To prevent excessive drive","To increase overall efficiency"],2,"ALC prevents excessive drive to an RF power amplifier."],["G4A06","What is the purpose of an antenna tuner?",["Reduce the SWR in the feed line to the antenna","Reduce the power dissipation in the feedline to the antenna","Increase power transfer from the transmitter to the feed line","All these choices are correct"],2,"An antenna tuner increases power transfer from the transmitter to the feed line."],["G4A07","What happens as a receiver’s noise reduction control level is increased?",["Received signals may become distorted","Received frequency may become unstable","CW signals may become severely attenuated","Received frequency may shift several kHz"],0,"Turning noise reduction up too far can distort received signals."],["G4A08","What is the correct adjustment for the LOAD or COUPLING control of a vacuum tube RF power amplifier?",["Minimum SWR on the antenna","Minimum plate current without exceeding maximum allowable grid current","Highest plate voltage while minimizing grid current","Desired power output without exceeding maximum allowable plate current"],3,"Set a tube amplifier's LOAD control for the desired output without exceeding maximum plate current."],["G4A09","What is the purpose of delaying RF output after activating a transmitter’s keying line to an external amplifier?",["To prevent key clicks on CW","To prevent transient overmodulation","To allow time for the amplifier to switch the antenna between the transceiver and the amplifier output","To allow time for the amplifier power supply to reach operating level"],2,"The delay gives the external amplifier time to switch the antenna over before RF arrives."],["G4A10","What is the function of an electronic keyer?",["Automatic transmit/receive switching","Automatic generation of dots and dashes for CW operation","To allow time for switching the antenna from the receiver to the transmitter","Computer interface for PSK and RTTY operation"],1,"An electronic keyer automatically generates dots and dashes for CW."],["G4A11","Why should the ALC system be inactive when transmitting AFSK data signals?",["ALC will invert the modulation of the AFSK mode","The ALC action distorts the signal","When using digital modes, too much ALC activity can cause the transmitter to overheat","All these choices are correct"],1,"ALC should be inactive for AFSK data because ALC action distorts the signal."],["G4A12","Which of the following is a common use of the dual-VFO feature on a transceiver?",["To allow transmitting on two frequencies at once","To permit full duplex operation -- that is, transmitting and receiving at the same time","To transmit on one frequency and listen on another","To improve frequency accuracy by allowing variable frequency output (VFO) operation"],2,"Dual VFOs let you transmit on one frequency and listen on another."],["G4A13","What is the purpose of using a receive attenuator?",["To prevent receiver overload from strong incoming signals","To reduce the transmitter power when driving a linear amplifier","To reduce power consumption when operating from batteries","To reduce excessive audio level on strong signals"],0,"A receive attenuator prevents receiver overload from strong incoming signals."],["G4B01","What item of test equipment contains horizontal and vertical channel amplifiers?",["An ohmmeter","A signal generator","An ammeter","An oscilloscope"],3,"An oscilloscope has horizontal and vertical channel amplifiers."],["G4B02","Which of the following is an advantage of an oscilloscope versus a digital voltmeter?",["An oscilloscope uses less power","Complex impedances can be easily measured","Greater precision","Complex waveforms can be measured"],3,"An oscilloscope can show complex waveforms; a digital voltmeter can't."],["G4B03","Which of the following is the best instrument to use for checking the keying waveform of a CW transmitter?",["An oscilloscope","A field strength meter","A sidetone monitor","A wavemeter"],0,"An oscilloscope is best for checking a CW transmitter's keying waveform."],["G4B04","What signal source is connected to the vertical input of an oscilloscope when checking the RF envelope pattern of a transmitted signal?",["The local oscillator of the transmitter","An external RF oscillator","The transmitter balanced mixer output","The attenuated RF output of the transmitter"],3,"Connect the transmitter's attenuated RF output to the oscilloscope's vertical input."],["G4B05","Why do voltmeters have high input impedance?",["It improves the frequency response","It allows for higher voltages to be safely measured","It improves the resolution of the readings","It decreases the loading on circuits being measured"],3,"Voltmeters have high input impedance to decrease loading on the circuit being measured."],["G4B06","What is an advantage of a digital multimeter as compared to an analog multimeter?",["Better for measuring computer circuits","Less prone to overload","Higher precision","Faster response"],2,"A digital multimeter offers higher precision than an analog one."],["G4B07","What signals are used to conduct a two-tone test?",["Two audio signals of the same frequency shifted 90 degrees","Two non-harmonically related audio signals","Two swept frequency tones","Two audio frequency range square wave signals of equal amplitude"],1,"A two-tone test uses two non-harmonically related audio signals."],["G4B08","What transmitter performance parameter does a two-tone test analyze?",["Linearity","Percentage of suppression of the carrier and undesired sideband for SSB","Percentage of frequency modulation","Percentage of carrier phase shift"],0,"A two-tone test analyzes a transmitter's linearity."],["G4B09","When is an analog multimeter preferred to a digital multimeter?",["When testing logic circuits","When high precision is desired","When measuring the frequency of an oscillator","When adjusting circuits for maximum or minimum values"],3,"An analog meter is better when adjusting a circuit for a maximum or minimum."],["G4B10","Which of the following can be determined with a directional wattmeter?",["Standing wave ratio","Antenna front-to-back ratio","RF interference","Radio wave propagation"],0,"A directional wattmeter can determine standing wave ratio."],["G4B11","Which of the following must be connected to an antenna analyzer when it is being used for SWR measurements?",["Receiver","Transmitter","Antenna and feed line","All these choices are correct"],2,"For SWR measurements, connect the antenna analyzer to the antenna and feed line."],["G4B12","What effect can strong signals from nearby transmitters have on an antenna analyzer?",["Desensitization which can cause intermodulation products which interfere with impedance readings","Received power that interferes with SWR readings","Generation of harmonics which interfere with frequency readings","All these choices are correct"],1,"Strong signals from nearby transmitters put received power into the analyzer, upsetting its SWR readings."],["G4B13","Which of the following can be measured with an antenna analyzer?",["Front-to-back ratio of an antenna","Power output from a transmitter","Impedance of coaxial cable","Gain of a directional antenna"],2,"An antenna analyzer can measure the impedance of coaxial cable."],["G4C01","Which of the following might be useful in reducing RF interference to audio frequency circuits?",["Bypass inductor","Bypass capacitor","Forward-biased diode","Reverse-biased diode"],1,"A bypass capacitor can help reduce RF interference to audio circuits."],["G4C02","Which of the following could be a cause of interference covering a wide range of frequencies?",["Not using a balun or line isolator to feed balanced antennas","Lack of rectification of the transmitter’s signal in power conductors","Arcing at a poor electrical connection","Using a balun to feed an unbalanced antenna"],2,"Arcing at a poor electrical connection can cause interference across a wide range of frequencies."],["G4C03","What sound is heard from an audio device experiencing RF interference from a single sideband phone transmitter?",["A steady hum whenever the transmitter is on the air","On-and-off humming or clicking","Distorted speech","Clearly audible speech"],2,"RF interference from an SSB phone transmitter sounds like distorted speech in audio gear."],["G4C04","What sound is heard from an audio device experiencing RF interference from a CW transmitter?",["On-and-off humming or clicking","A CW signal at a nearly pure audio frequency","A chirpy CW signal","Severely distorted audio"],0,"RF interference from a CW transmitter sounds like on-and-off humming or clicking."],["G4C05","What is a possible cause of high voltages that produce RF burns?",["Flat braid rather than round wire has been used for the ground wire","Insulated wire has been used for the ground wire","The ground rod is resonant","The ground wire has high impedance on that frequency"],3,"A ground wire with high impedance at the operating frequency can produce high voltages that cause RF burns."],["G4C06","What is a possible effect of a resonant ground connection?",["Overheating of ground straps","Corrosion of the ground rod","High RF voltages on the enclosures of station equipment","A ground loop"],2,"A resonant ground connection can put high RF voltages on station equipment enclosures."],["G4C07","Why should soldered joints not be used in lightning protection ground connections?",["A soldered joint will likely be destroyed by the heat of a lightning strike","Solder flux will prevent a low conductivity connection","Solder has too high a dielectric constant to provide adequate lightning protection","All these choices are correct"],0,"Don't solder lightning ground connections: a strike's heat would likely destroy the joint."],["G4C08","Which of the following would reduce RF interference caused by common-mode current on an audio cable?",["Place a ferrite choke on the cable","Connect the center conductor to the shield of all cables to short circuit the RFI signal","Ground the center conductor of the audio cable causing the interference","Add an additional insulating jacket to the cable"],0,"A ferrite choke on the cable reduces RF interference from common-mode current."],["G4C09","How can the effects of ground loops be minimized?",["Connect all ground conductors in series","Connect the AC neutral conductor to the ground wire","Avoid using lock washers and star washers when making ground connections","Bond equipment enclosures together"],3,"Bond equipment enclosures together to minimize ground loops."],["G4C10","What could be a symptom caused by a ground loop in your station’s audio connections?",["You receive reports of “hum” on your station’s transmitted signal","The SWR reading for one or more antennas is suddenly very high","An item of station equipment starts to draw excessive amounts of current","You receive reports of harmonic interference from your station"],0,"A ground loop in your station's audio connections can cause reports of hum on your transmitted signal."],["G4C11","What technique helps to minimize RF “hot spots” in an amateur station?",["Building all equipment in a metal enclosure","Using surge suppressor power outlets","Bonding all equipment enclosures together","Placing low-pass filters on all feed lines"],2,"Bonding all equipment enclosures together helps minimize RF hot spots."],["G4C12","Why must all metal enclosures of station equipment be grounded?",["It prevents a blown fuse in the event of an internal short circuit","It prevents signal overload","It ensures that the neutral wire is grounded","It ensures that hazardous voltages cannot appear on the chassis"],3,"Metal enclosures must be grounded so hazardous voltages can't appear on the chassis."],["G4D01","What is the purpose of a speech processor in a transceiver?",["Increase the apparent loudness of transmitted voice signals","Increase transmitter bass response for more natural-sounding SSB signals","Prevent distortion of voice signals","Decrease high-frequency voice output to prevent out-of-band operation"],0,"A speech processor increases the apparent loudness of transmitted voice."],["G4D02","How does a speech processor affect a single sideband phone signal?",["It increases peak power","It increases average power","It reduces harmonic distortion","It reduces intermodulation distortion"],1,"A speech processor increases an SSB signal's average power."],["G4D03","What is the effect of an incorrectly adjusted speech processor?",["Distorted speech","Excess intermodulation products","Excessive background noise","All these choices are correct"],3,"A misadjusted speech processor causes distorted speech, intermodulation products and excess background noise."],["G4D04","What does an S meter measure?",["Carrier suppression","Impedance","Received signal strength","Transmitter power output"],2,"An S meter measures received signal strength."],["G4D05","How does a signal that reads 20 dB over S9 compare to one that reads S9 on a receiver, assuming a properly calibrated S meter?",["It is 10 times less powerful","It is 20 times less powerful","It is 20 times more powerful","It is 100 times more powerful"],3,"20 dB over S9 is 100 times more powerful than S9."],["G4D06","How much change in signal strength is typically represented by one S unit?",["6 dB","12 dB","15 dB","18 dB"],0,"One S unit is typically 6 dB."],["G4D07","How much must the power output of a transmitter be raised to change the S meter reading on a distant receiver from S8 to S9?",["Approximately 1.5 times","Approximately 2 times","Approximately 4 times","Approximately 8 times"],2,"To go from S8 to S9, raise power about 4 times."],["G4D08","What frequency range is occupied by a 3 kHz LSB signal when the displayed carrier frequency is set to 7.178 MHz?",["7.178 MHz to 7.181 MHz","7.178 MHz to 7.184 MHz","7.175 MHz to 7.178 MHz","7.1765 MHz to 7.1795 MHz"],2,"A 3 kHz LSB signal at 7.178 MHz occupies 7.175 to 7.178 MHz."],["G4D09","What frequency range is occupied by a 3 kHz USB signal with the displayed carrier frequency set to 14.347 MHz?",["14.347 MHz to 14.647 MHz","14.347 MHz to 14.350 MHz","14.344 MHz to 14.347 MHz","14.3455 MHz to 14.3485 MHz"],1,"A 3 kHz USB signal at 14.347 MHz occupies 14.347 to 14.350 MHz."],["G4D10","How close to the lower edge of a band’s phone segment should your displayed carrier frequency be when using 3 kHz wide LSB?",["At least 3 kHz above the edge of the segment","At least 3 kHz below the edge of the segment","At least 1 kHz below the edge of the segment","At least 1 kHz above the edge of the segment"],0,"On LSB, keep the displayed frequency at least 3 kHz above the lower edge of the phone segment."],["G4D11","How close to the upper edge of a band’s phone segment should your displayed carrier frequency be when using 3 kHz wide USB?",["At least 3 kHz above the edge of the band","At least 3 kHz below the edge of the band","At least 1 kHz above the edge of the segment","At least 1 kHz below the edge of the segment"],1,"On USB, keep the displayed frequency at least 3 kHz below the upper edge of the phone segment."],["G4E01","What is the purpose of a capacitance hat on a mobile antenna?",["To increase the power handling capacity of a whip antenna","To reduce radiation resistance","To electrically lengthen a physically short antenna","To lower the radiation angle"],2,"A capacitance hat electrically lengthens a physically short antenna."],["G4E02","What is the purpose of a corona ball on an HF mobile antenna?",["To narrow the operating bandwidth of the antenna","To increase the “Q” of the antenna","To reduce the chance of damage if the antenna should strike an object","To reduce RF voltage discharge from the tip of the antenna while transmitting"],3,"A corona ball reduces RF voltage discharge from the tip of the antenna."],["G4E03","Which of the following direct, fused power connections would be the best for a 100-watt HF mobile installation?",["To the battery using heavy-gauge wire","To the alternator or generator using heavy-gauge wire","To the battery using insulated heavy duty balanced transmission line","To the alternator or generator using insulated heavy duty balanced transmission line"],0,"For a 100-watt mobile, connect directly to the battery with heavy-gauge, fused wire."],["G4E04","Why should DC power for a 100-watt HF transceiver not be supplied by a vehicle’s auxiliary power socket?",["The socket is not wired with an RF-shielded power cable","The socket’s wiring may be inadequate for the current drawn by the transceiver","The DC polarity of the socket is reversed from the polarity of modern HF transceivers","Drawing more than 50 watts from this socket could cause the engine to overheat"],1,"An accessory power socket's wiring may not handle a 100-watt transceiver's current."],["G4E05","Which of the following most limits an HF mobile installation?",["“Picket fencing”","The wire gauge of the DC power line to the transceiver","Efficiency of the electrically short antenna","FCC rules limiting mobile output power on the 75-meter band"],2,"The efficiency of the electrically short antenna most limits an HF mobile station."],["G4E06","What is one disadvantage of using a shortened mobile antenna as opposed to a full-size antenna?",["Short antennas are more likely to cause distortion of transmitted signals","Q of the antenna will be very low","Operating bandwidth may be very limited","Harmonic radiation may increase"],2,"A shortened antenna's operating bandwidth may be very limited."],["G4E07","Which of the following may cause receive interference to an HF transceiver installed in a vehicle?",["The battery charging system","The fuel delivery system","The control computers","All these choices are correct"],3,"A vehicle's charging system, fuel delivery and control computers can all cause receive interference."],["G4E08","In what configuration are the individual cells in a solar panel connected together?",["Series-parallel","Shunt","Bypass","Full-wave bridge"],0,"Solar cells are connected in series-parallel."],["G4E09","What is the approximate open-circuit voltage from a fully illuminated silicon photovoltaic cell?",["0.02 VDC","0.5 VDC","0.2 VDC","1.38 VDC"],1,"A fully lit silicon solar cell gives about 0.5 volt with no load."],["G4E10","Why should a series diode be connected between a solar panel and a storage battery that is being charged by the panel?",["To prevent overload by regulating the charging voltage","To prevent discharge of the battery through the panel during times of low or no illumination","To limit the current flowing from the panel to a safe value","To prevent damage to the battery due to excessive voltage at high illumination levels"],1,"A series diode keeps the battery from discharging through the panel in the dark."],["G4E11","What precaution should be taken when connecting a solar panel to a lithium iron phosphate battery?",["Ground the solar panel outer metal framework","Ensure the battery is placed terminals-up","A series resistor must be in place","The solar panel must have a charge controller"],3,"A lithium iron phosphate battery charged from a solar panel needs a charge controller."]],"P":{"receiver":["Receiver controls","<p>A <b>notch filter</b> removes a steady carrier (a whistle) inside the receiver passband. A <b>noise blanker</b> cuts the receiver's gain for the instant of each noise pulse. <b>Noise reduction</b> set too high distorts signals. A <b>receive attenuator</b> keeps very strong signals from overloading the receiver.</p><p>On CW, switching to the <b>opposite sideband</b> can move an interfering signal out of the way. <b>Dual VFOs</b> let you transmit on one frequency and listen on another (\"split\").</p>"],"transmitter":["Transmitters, amplifiers and tuners","<p><b>ALC</b> (automatic level control) prevents excessive drive. For AFSK data, keep ALC inactive by setting audio below the point where it acts, since ALC action distorts the signal.</p><p>A vacuum-tube amplifier is tuned for a <b>dip</b> in plate current with the TUNE control, then the LOAD control is set for the desired output without exceeding the maximum plate current. A transceiver delays RF after keying an external amplifier so the amplifier's relay can switch the antenna first.</p><p>An <b>antenna tuner</b> matches the transmitter to the feed line, increasing power transfer; it doesn't change the SWR on the feed line itself. An <b>electronic keyer</b> forms dots and dashes automatically.</p>"],"scope":["Oscilloscopes and the two-tone test","<p>An <b>oscilloscope</b> draws a signal's waveform, using horizontal and vertical channel amplifiers, so it can show complex waveforms a meter can't, such as a CW keying waveform or a transmitter's RF envelope (feed the attenuated RF output to the vertical input).</p><p>A <b>two-tone test</b> feeds two non-harmonically related audio tones into an SSB transmitter to check its <b>linearity</b>.</p>"],"meters":["Meters","<p>Voltmeters have high input impedance so they <b>don't load</b> the circuit they measure. A digital multimeter gives higher precision; an analog meter's needle is better for adjusting a circuit to a maximum or minimum. A <b>directional wattmeter</b> reads forward and reflected power, from which you get SWR.</p>"],"analyzer":["Antenna analyzers","<p>An antenna analyzer is a small, low-power signal source and meter. Connect it to the <b>antenna and feed line</b> (no transmitter or receiver) to read SWR and impedance, including a coax cable's impedance. Strong signals from nearby transmitters get into it and upset its SWR readings.</p>"],"rfi":["Interference to electronics","<p>RF getting into audio gear sounds like the mode causing it: from SSB, <b>distorted speech</b>; from CW, <b>on-and-off humming or clicking</b>. A <b>bypass capacitor</b> shunts RF away from audio circuits, and a <b>ferrite choke</b> on a cable stops common-mode current. <b>Arcing</b> at a poor electrical connection makes interference across a wide range of frequencies.</p>"],"grounding":["Grounding and bonding","<p>Ground equipment enclosures so <b>hazardous voltages can't appear on the chassis</b>. <b>Bond</b> enclosures together to minimize ground loops (which show up as hum on your signal) and RF hot spots.</p><p>A ground wire about a quarter wavelength long (or an odd multiple) is resonant and has <b>high impedance</b> at the equipment end. A ground lead with high impedance at the operating frequency can put high RF voltages on equipment, enough to cause RF burns. For lightning grounds, don't use solder: a strike's heat would melt the joint.</p>"],"processor":["Speech processors","<p>A speech processor raises the <b>average power</b> of an SSB signal, making it sound louder without raising peak power. Set it too high and you get distorted speech, extra intermodulation products and more background noise.</p>"],"smeter":["S meters and decibels","<p>An S meter reads <b>received signal strength</b>. One S unit is about <b>6 dB</b>, four times the power, so going from S8 to S9 at the far end takes about four times the transmitter power. Above S9 the meter reads in dB: <b>20 dB over S9</b> is 100 times the power of S9.</p>"],"edges":["Staying inside the band","<p>An SSB signal extends about 3 kHz to one side of the displayed (carrier) frequency: <b>below it on LSB, above it on USB</b>. So on LSB, keep the display at least <b>3 kHz above</b> the bottom edge of the phone segment; on USB, at least <b>3 kHz below</b> the top edge.</p>"],"mobile":["HF mobile","<p>The <b>electrically short antenna</b> is what limits an HF mobile station most: it's inefficient and has a narrow bandwidth. A <b>capacitance hat</b> electrically lengthens it, and a <b>corona ball</b> on the tip reduces RF voltage discharge.</p><p>Wire a 100-watt radio directly to the <b>battery</b> with heavy-gauge wire and fuses; the accessory socket's wiring may not be able to carry the current. Charging systems, fuel pumps and control computers can all cause receive interference.</p>"],"solar":["Solar power","<p>A silicon solar cell makes about <b>0.5 volt</b>, so panels connect cells in <b>series-parallel</b> for useful voltage and current. A <b>series diode</b> stops the battery discharging back through the panel in the dark, and a lithium iron phosphate battery needs a proper <b>charge controller</b>.</p>"]},"CON":{"G4A01":"receiver","G4A02":"receiver","G4A03":"receiver","G4A04":"transmitter","G4A05":"transmitter","G4A06":"transmitter","G4A07":"receiver","G4A08":"transmitter","G4A09":"transmitter","G4A10":"transmitter","G4A11":"transmitter","G4A12":"receiver","G4A13":"receiver","G4B01":"scope","G4B02":"scope","G4B03":"scope","G4B04":"scope","G4B05":"meters","G4B06":"meters","G4B07":"scope","G4B08":"scope","G4B09":"meters","G4B10":"meters","G4B11":"analyzer","G4B12":"analyzer","G4B13":"analyzer","G4C01":"rfi","G4C02":"rfi","G4C03":"rfi","G4C04":"rfi","G4C05":"grounding","G4C06":"grounding","G4C07":"grounding","G4C08":"rfi","G4C09":"grounding","G4C10":"grounding","G4C11":"grounding","G4C12":"grounding","G4D01":"processor","G4D02":"processor","G4D03":"processor","G4D04":"smeter","G4D05":"smeter","G4D06":"smeter","G4D07":"smeter","G4D08":"edges","G4D09":"edges","G4D10":"edges","G4D11":"edges","G4E01":"mobile","G4E02":"mobile","G4E03":"mobile","G4E04":"mobile","G4E05":"mobile","G4E06":"mobile","G4E07":"mobile","G4E08":"solar","G4E09":"solar","G4E10":"solar","G4E11":"solar"}},{"id":"g5","code":"G5","name":"Electrical principles","exam":3,"G":{"G5A":"Reactance, impedance, resonance","G5B":"Decibels, power, RMS and PEP","G5C":"Series and parallel parts, transformers"},"Q":[["G5A01","What happens when inductive and capacitive reactance are equal in a series LC circuit?",["Resonance causes impedance to be very high","Impedance is equal to the geometric mean of the inductance and capacitance","Resonance causes impedance to be very low","Impedance is equal to the arithmetic mean of the inductance and capacitance"],2,"At resonance in a series LC circuit, impedance is very low."],["G5A02","What is reactance?",["Opposition to the flow of direct current caused by resistance","Opposition to the flow of alternating current caused by capacitance or inductance","Reinforcement of the flow of direct current caused by resistance","Reinforcement of the flow of alternating current caused by capacitance or inductance"],1,"Reactance is opposition to alternating current caused by capacitance or inductance."],["G5A03","Which of the following is opposition to the flow of alternating current in an inductor?",["Conductance","Reluctance","Admittance","Reactance"],3,"Opposition to AC in an inductor is reactance."],["G5A04","Which of the following is opposition to the flow of alternating current in a capacitor?",["Conductance","Reluctance","Reactance","Admittance"],2,"Opposition to AC in a capacitor is reactance."],["G5A05","How does an inductor react to AC?",["As the frequency of the applied AC increases, the reactance decreases","As the amplitude of the applied AC increases, the reactance increases","As the amplitude of the applied AC increases, the reactance decreases","As the frequency of the applied AC increases, the reactance increases"],3,"An inductor's reactance increases as frequency increases."],["G5A06","How does a capacitor react to AC?",["As the frequency of the applied AC increases, the reactance decreases","As the frequency of the applied AC increases, the reactance increases","As the amplitude of the applied AC increases, the reactance increases","As the amplitude of the applied AC increases, the reactance decreases"],0,"A capacitor's reactance decreases as frequency increases."],["G5A07","What is the term for the inverse of impedance?",["Conductance","Susceptance","Reluctance","Admittance"],3,"The inverse of impedance is admittance."],["G5A08","What is impedance?",["The ratio of current to voltage","The product of current and voltage","The ratio of voltage to current","The product of current and reactance"],2,"Impedance is the ratio of voltage to current."],["G5A09","What unit is used to measure reactance?",["Farad","Ohm","Ampere","Siemens"],1,"Reactance is measured in ohms."],["G5A10","Which of the following devices can be used for impedance matching at radio frequencies?",["A transformer","A Pi-network","A length of transmission line","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: transformers, pi-networks and lengths of transmission line can match impedances at RF."],["G5A11","What letter is used to represent reactance?",["Z","X","B","Y"],1,"Reactance is represented by the letter X."],["G5A12","What occurs in an LC circuit at resonance?",["Current and voltage are equal","Resistance is cancelled","The circuit radiates all its energy in the form of radio waves","Inductive reactance and capacitive reactance cancel"],3,"At resonance, inductive and capacitive reactance cancel."],["G5B01","What dB change represents a factor of two increase or decrease in power?",["Approximately 2 dB","Approximately 3 dB","Approximately 6 dB","Approximately 9 dB"],1,"A factor of two in power is about 3 dB."],["G5B02","How does the total current relate to the individual currents in a circuit of parallel resistors?",["It equals the average of the branch currents","It decreases as more parallel branches are added to the circuit","It equals the sum of the currents through each branch","It is the sum of the reciprocal of each individual voltage drop"],2,"In a parallel circuit, the total current is the sum of the currents through each branch."],["G5B03","How many watts of electrical power are consumed if 400 VDC is supplied to an 800-ohm load?",["0.5 watts","200 watts","400 watts","3200 watts"],1,"400 VDC across 800 ohms consumes 200 watts."],["G5B04","How many watts of electrical power are consumed by a 12 VDC light bulb that draws 0.2 amperes?",["2.4 watts","24 watts","6 watts","60 watts"],0,"A 12 VDC bulb drawing 0.2 A consumes 2.4 watts."],["G5B05","How many watts are consumed when a current of 7.0 milliamperes flows through a 1,250-ohm resistance?",["Approximately 61 milliwatts","Approximately 61 watts","Approximately 11 milliwatts","Approximately 11 watts"],0,"7.0 mA through 1,250 ohms consumes about 61 milliwatts."],["G5B06","What is the PEP produced by 200 volts peak-to-peak across a 50-ohm dummy load?",["1.4 watts","100 watts","353.5 watts","400 watts"],1,"200 volts peak-to-peak across 50 ohms is 100 watts PEP."],["G5B07","What value of an AC signal produces the same power dissipation in a resistor as a DC voltage of the same value?",["The peak-to-peak value","The peak value","The RMS value","The reciprocal of the RMS value"],2,"The RMS value of an AC signal heats a resistor the same as an equal DC voltage."],["G5B08","What is the peak-to-peak voltage of a sine wave with an RMS voltage of 120 volts?",["84.8 volts","169.7 volts","240.0 volts","339.4 volts"],3,"A 120-volt RMS sine wave is 339.4 volts peak-to-peak."],["G5B09","What is the RMS voltage of a sine wave with a value of 17 volts peak?",["8.5 volts","12 volts","24 volts","34 volts"],1,"17 volts peak is 12 volts RMS."],["G5B10","What percentage of power loss is equivalent to a loss of 1 dB?",["10.9 percent","12.2 percent","20.6 percent","25.9 percent"],2,"A 1 dB loss is about a 20.6 percent power loss."],["G5B11","What is the ratio of PEP to average power for an unmodulated carrier?",["0.707","1.00","1.414","2.00"],1,"For an unmodulated carrier, PEP to average power is 1.00."],["G5B12","What is the RMS voltage across a 50-ohm dummy load dissipating 1200 watts?",["173 volts","245 volts","346 volts","692 volts"],1,"A 50-ohm load dissipating 1200 watts has about 245 volts RMS across it."],["G5B13","What is the output PEP of an unmodulated carrier if the average power is 1060 watts?",["530 watts","1060 watts","1500 watts","2120 watts"],1,"An unmodulated carrier with 1060 watts average power has 1060 watts PEP."],["G5B14","What is the output PEP of 500 volts peak-to-peak across a 50-ohm load?",["8.75 watts","625 watts","2500 watts","5000 watts"],1,"500 volts peak-to-peak across 50 ohms is 625 watts PEP."],["G5C01","What causes a voltage to appear across the secondary winding of a transformer when an AC voltage source is connected across its primary winding?",["Capacitive coupling","Displacement current coupling","Mutual inductance","Mutual capacitance"],2,"Mutual inductance makes a voltage appear across the secondary."],["G5C02","What is the output voltage if an input signal is applied to the secondary winding of a 4:1 voltage step-down transformer instead of the primary winding?",["The input voltage is multiplied by 4","The input voltage is divided by 4","Additional resistance must be added in series with the primary to prevent overload","Additional resistance must be added in parallel with the secondary to prevent overload"],0,"Feeding a 4:1 step-down transformer backwards multiplies the input voltage by 4."],["G5C03","What is the total resistance of a 10-, a 20-, and a 50-ohm resistor connected in parallel?",["5.9 ohms","0.17 ohms","17 ohms","80 ohms"],0,"10, 20 and 50 ohms in parallel total about 5.9 ohms."],["G5C04","What is the approximate total resistance of a 100- and a 200-ohm resistor in parallel?",["300 ohms","150 ohms","75 ohms","67 ohms"],3,"100 and 200 ohms in parallel total about 67 ohms."],["G5C05","Why is the primary winding wire of a voltage step-up transformer usually a larger size than that of the secondary winding?",["To improve the coupling between the primary and secondary","To accommodate the higher current of the primary","To prevent parasitic oscillations due to resistive losses in the primary","To ensure that the volume of the primary winding is equal to the volume of the secondary winding"],1,"A step-up transformer's primary carries the higher current, so it uses heavier wire."],["G5C06","What is the voltage output of a transformer with a 500-turn primary and a 1500-turn secondary when 120 VAC is applied to the primary?",["360 volts","120 volts","40 volts","25.5 volts"],0,"A 500-turn primary and 1500-turn secondary turn 120 VAC into 360 volts."],["G5C07","What transformer turns ratio matches an antenna’s 600-ohm feed point impedance to a 50-ohm coaxial cable?",["3.5 to 1","12 to 1","24 to 1","144 to 1"],0,"Matching 600 ohms to 50 ohms takes a turns ratio of about 3.5 to 1."],["G5C08","What is the equivalent capacitance of two 5.0-nanofarad capacitors and one 750-picofarad capacitor connected in parallel?",["576.9 nanofarads","1,733 picofarads","3,583 picofarads","10.750 nanofarads"],3,"Two 5.0 nF and one 750 pF capacitor in parallel make 10.750 nF."],["G5C09","What is the capacitance of three 100-microfarad capacitors connected in series?",["0.33 microfarads","3.0 microfarads","33.3 microfarads","300 microfarads"],2,"Three 100 µF capacitors in series make 33.3 µF."],["G5C10","What is the inductance of three 10-millihenry inductors connected in parallel?",["0.30 henries","3.3 henries","3.3 millihenries","30 millihenries"],2,"Three 10 mH inductors in parallel make 3.3 mH."],["G5C11","What is the inductance of a circuit with a 20-millihenry inductor connected in series with a 50-millihenry inductor?",["7 millihenries","14.3 millihenries","70 millihenries","1,000 millihenries"],2,"20 mH and 50 mH in series make 70 mH."],["G5C12","What is the capacitance of a 20-microfarad capacitor connected in series with a 50-microfarad capacitor?",["0.07 microfarads","14.3 microfarads","70 microfarads","1,000 microfarads"],1,"20 µF and 50 µF in series make 14.3 µF."],["G5C13","Which of the following components should be added to a capacitor to increase the capacitance?",["An inductor in series","An inductor in parallel","A capacitor in parallel","A capacitor in series"],2,"To increase capacitance, add a capacitor in parallel."],["G5C14","Which of the following components should be added to an inductor to increase the inductance?",["A capacitor in series","A capacitor in parallel","An inductor in parallel","An inductor in series"],3,"To increase inductance, add an inductor in series."]],"P":{"resonance":["Resonance and matching","<p>At <b>resonance</b>, inductive and capacitive reactance are equal and cancel. A <b>series</b> LC circuit then has very <b>low</b> impedance (only resistance is left); a parallel one has very high impedance.</p><p>Impedances are matched at RF with transformers, pi-networks, or lengths of transmission line.</p>"],"reactance":["Reactance and impedance","<p><b>Reactance</b> (symbol <b>X</b>, measured in <b>ohms</b>) is the opposition to AC caused by inductance or capacitance. An inductor's reactance <b>rises</b> with frequency; a capacitor's <b>falls</b> with frequency. Changing the amplitude of the AC doesn't change either reactance.</p><p><b>Impedance</b> (Z) is the ratio of voltage to current. Its inverse is <b>admittance</b>.</p>"],"decibel":["Decibels","<p>Decibels compare power ratios. About <b>3 dB</b> is twice (or half) the power, <b>10 dB</b> ten times. A <b>1 dB</b> loss leaves about 79% of the power: a 20.6% loss.</p>"],"series":["Series and parallel","<p><b>Resistors and inductors</b> add in series. In parallel, use 1/(1/a + 1/b + ...), or for two, (a &times; b)/(a + b); for equal parts, divide one by how many there are.</p><p><b>Capacitors</b> work the other way round: they add in <b>parallel</b> and use the reciprocal formula in <b>series</b>.</p><p>So to increase capacitance, add a capacitor in parallel; to increase inductance, add an inductor in series. In a parallel circuit the total current is the <b>sum</b> of the branch currents.</p>"],"power":["Power","<p>Power in watts: <b>P = E &times; I</b>, or <b>P = E&sup2; / R</b>, or <b>P = I&sup2; &times; R</b>. Watch the units: milliamps are thousandths of an amp.</p>"],"rms":["RMS, peak and PEP","<p>For a sine wave: <b>peak = RMS &times; 1.414</b>, <b>RMS = peak &times; 0.707</b>, and <b>peak-to-peak = 2 &times; peak</b>. The <b>RMS</b> value is the one that heats a resistor like the same DC voltage.</p><p><b>PEP</b> from a peak-to-peak voltage: halve it to get peak, multiply by 0.707 for RMS, then P = E&sup2;/R. For an unmodulated carrier, PEP equals average power (ratio 1.00). And E = &radic;(P &times; R).</p>"],"transformer":["Transformers","<p>A transformer works by <b>mutual inductance</b>. Voltage follows the <b>turns ratio</b>: a 500-turn primary and 1500-turn secondary triples the voltage. Fed backwards, a 4:1 step-down becomes a 1:4 step-up: the voltage is multiplied by 4.</p><p>Impedance follows the <b>square</b> of the turns ratio, so the turns ratio is the <b>square root</b> of the impedance ratio. A step-up transformer's primary carries more current, so it uses heavier wire.</p>"]},"CON":{"G5A01":"resonance","G5A02":"reactance","G5A03":"reactance","G5A04":"reactance","G5A05":"reactance","G5A06":"reactance","G5A07":"reactance","G5A08":"reactance","G5A09":"reactance","G5A10":"resonance","G5A11":"reactance","G5A12":"resonance","G5B01":"decibel","G5B02":"series","G5B03":"power","G5B04":"power","G5B05":"power","G5B06":"rms","G5B07":"rms","G5B08":"rms","G5B09":"rms","G5B10":"decibel","G5B11":"rms","G5B12":"rms","G5B13":"rms","G5B14":"rms","G5C01":"transformer","G5C02":"transformer","G5C03":"series","G5C04":"series","G5C05":"transformer","G5C06":"transformer","G5C07":"transformer","G5C08":"series","G5C09":"series","G5C10":"series","G5C11":"series","G5C12":"series","G5C13":"series","G5C14":"series"}},{"id":"g6","code":"G6","name":"Circuit components","exam":2,"G":{"G6A":"Batteries, diodes, transistors, tubes","G6B":"ICs, ferrites and connectors"},"Q":[["G6A01","What is the minimum allowable discharge voltage for maximum life of a standard 12-volt lead-acid battery?",["6 volts","8.5 volts","10.5 volts","12 volts"],2,"Don't discharge a 12-volt lead-acid battery below about 10.5 volts."],["G6A02","What is an advantage of batteries with low internal resistance?",["Long life","High discharge current","High voltage","Rapid recharge"],1,"Low internal resistance lets a battery deliver high discharge current."],["G6A03","What is the approximate forward threshold voltage of a germanium diode?",["0.1 volt","0.3 volts","0.7 volts","1.0 volts"],1,"A germanium diode starts conducting at about 0.3 volt."],["G6A04","Which of the following is characteristic of an electrolytic capacitor?",["Tight tolerance","Much less leakage than any other type","High capacitance for a given volume","Inexpensive RF capacitor"],2,"Electrolytic capacitors give high capacitance for their size."],["G6A05","What is the approximate forward threshold voltage of a silicon junction diode?",["0.1 volt","0.3 volts","0.7 volts","1.0 volts"],2,"A silicon junction diode starts conducting at about 0.7 volt."],["G6A06","Why should wire-wound resistors not be used in RF circuits?",["The resistor’s tolerance value would not be adequate","The resistor’s inductance could make circuit performance unpredictable","The resistor could overheat","The resistor’s internal capacitance would detune the circuit"],1,"Wire-wound resistors have inductance that makes RF circuits unpredictable."],["G6A07","What are the operating points for a bipolar transistor used as a switch?",["Saturation and cutoff","The active region (between cutoff and saturation)","Peak and valley current points","Enhancement and depletion modes"],0,"A bipolar transistor used as a switch operates at saturation and cutoff."],["G6A08","Which of the following is characteristic of low voltage ceramic capacitors?",["Tight tolerance","High stability","High capacitance for given volume","Comparatively low cost"],3,"Low-voltage ceramic capacitors are comparatively low in cost."],["G6A09","Which of the following describes MOSFET construction?",["The gate is formed by a back-biased junction","The gate is separated from the channel by a thin insulating layer","The source is separated from the drain by a thin insulating layer","The source is formed by depositing metal on silicon"],1,"In a MOSFET, the gate is separated from the channel by a thin insulating layer."],["G6A10","Which element of a vacuum tube regulates the flow of electrons between cathode and plate?",["Control grid","Suppressor grid","Screen grid","Trigger electrode"],0,"The control grid regulates the flow of electrons between cathode and plate."],["G6A11","What happens when an inductor is operated above its self-resonant frequency?",["Its reactance increases","Harmonics are generated","It becomes capacitive","Catastrophic failure is likely"],2,"Above its self-resonant frequency, an inductor becomes capacitive."],["G6A12","What is the primary purpose of a screen grid in a vacuum tube?",["To reduce grid-to-plate capacitance","To increase efficiency","To increase the control grid resistance","To decrease plate resistance"],0,"A screen grid's main purpose is to reduce grid-to-plate capacitance."],["G6B01","What determines the performance of a ferrite core at different frequencies?",["Its conductivity","Its thickness","The composition, or “mix,” of materials used","The ratio of outer diameter to inner diameter"],2,"A ferrite core's performance at different frequencies depends on its composition, or mix."],["G6B02","What is meant by the term MMIC?",["Multi-Mode Integrated Circuit","Monolithic Microwave Integrated Circuit","Metal Monolayer Integrated Circuit","Mode Modulated Integrated Circuit"],1,"MMIC means Monolithic Microwave Integrated Circuit."],["G6B03","Which of the following is an advantage of CMOS integrated circuits compared to TTL integrated circuits?",["Low power consumption","High power handling capability","Better suited for RF amplification","Better suited for power supply regulation"],0,"CMOS integrated circuits use much less power than TTL."],["G6B04","What is a typical upper frequency limit for low SWR operation of 50-ohm BNC connectors?",["50 MHz","500 MHz","4 GHz","40 GHz"],2,"50-ohm BNC connectors work with low SWR up to about 4 GHz."],["G6B05","What is an advantage of using a ferrite core toroidal inductor?",["Large values of inductance may be obtained","The magnetic properties of the core may be optimized for a specific range of frequencies","Most of the magnetic field is contained in the core","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: ferrite toroids give large inductance, can be optimized for a frequency range, and keep most of the field in the core."],["G6B06","What kind of device is an integrated circuit operational amplifier?",["Digital","MMIC","Programmable Logic","Analog"],3,"An op amp is an analog device."],["G6B07","Which of the following describes a type N connector?",["A moisture-resistant RF connector useful to 10 GHz","A small bayonet connector used for data circuits","A low noise figure VHF connector","A nickel plated version of the PL-259"],0,"A type N connector is a moisture-resistant RF connector useful to 10 GHz."],["G6B08","How is an LED biased when emitting light?",["In the tunnel-effect region","At the Zener voltage","Reverse biased","Forward biased"],3,"An LED is forward biased when it gives off light."],["G6B10","How does a ferrite bead or core reduce common-mode RF current on the shield of a coaxial cable?",["By creating an impedance in the current’s path","It converts common-mode current to differential mode current","By creating an out-of-phase current to cancel the common-mode current","Ferrites expel magnetic fields"],0,"A ferrite bead or core reduces common-mode current by creating an impedance in its path."],["G6B11","What is an SMA connector?",["A type-S to type-M adaptor","A small threaded connector suitable for signals up to several GHz","A connector designed for serial multiple access signals","A type of push-on connector intended for high-voltage applications"],1,"An SMA connector is a small threaded connector for signals up to several GHz."],["G6B12","Which of these connector types is commonly used for low frequency or dc signal connections to a transceiver?",["PL-259","BNC","RCA Phono","Type N"],2,"RCA phono connectors are common for low-frequency or DC connections to a transceiver."]],"P":{"batteries":["Batteries","<p>Don't discharge a 12-volt lead-acid battery below about <b>10.5 volts</b> if you want it to last. A battery with low internal resistance can deliver <b>high current</b>.</p>"],"diodes":["Diodes and LEDs","<p>A diode starts conducting at its forward threshold voltage: about <b>0.3 volt</b> for germanium and <b>0.7 volt</b> for silicon. An LED gives light when it's <b>forward biased</b>.</p>"],"passives":["Resistors, capacitors and inductors","<p><b>Electrolytic</b> capacitors pack high capacitance into a small volume; low-voltage <b>ceramic</b> capacitors are cheap. <b>Wire-wound</b> resistors are coils of wire, so their inductance makes them unpredictable at RF.</p><p>Every inductor has some capacitance between its turns. Above its <b>self-resonant frequency</b>, that capacitance wins and the inductor behaves like a capacitor.</p>"],"transistors":["Transistors","<p>Used as a switch, a bipolar transistor is either fully on (<b>saturation</b>) or fully off (<b>cutoff</b>). In a <b>MOSFET</b>, a thin insulating layer separates the gate from the channel, so the gate draws almost no current.</p>"],"tubes":["Vacuum tubes","<p>The <b>control grid</b> sits between cathode and plate and regulates the flow of electrons. The <b>screen grid</b> reduces the capacitance between control grid and plate, which reduces the feedback that can make the tube oscillate.</p>"],"ferrite":["Ferrite cores","<p>A ferrite's performance at different frequencies depends on its <b>mix</b> of materials. Ferrite toroids give large inductance, keep the magnetic field inside the core, and can be chosen for a frequency range. A ferrite bead or choke on a coax cable adds <b>impedance</b> to common-mode current on the shield.</p>"],"ics":["Integrated circuits","<p>An <b>MMIC</b> is a Monolithic Microwave Integrated Circuit, a microwave circuit, most often a complete RF amplifier, on one chip. <b>CMOS</b> logic uses far less power than TTL. An <b>op amp</b> is an analog device.</p>"],"connectors":["Connectors","<p><b>BNC</b>: bayonet, good to about 4 GHz. <b>Type N</b>: moisture-resistant, good to about 10 GHz. <b>SMA</b>: small and threaded, for signals up to several GHz. <b>RCA phono</b>: low-frequency and DC connections, like audio and keying lines. The PL-259 (UHF connector) is the common HF connector.</p>"]},"CON":{"G6A01":"batteries","G6A02":"batteries","G6A03":"diodes","G6A04":"passives","G6A05":"diodes","G6A06":"passives","G6A07":"transistors","G6A08":"passives","G6A09":"transistors","G6A10":"tubes","G6A11":"passives","G6A12":"tubes","G6B01":"ferrite","G6B02":"ics","G6B03":"ics","G6B04":"connectors","G6B05":"ferrite","G6B06":"ics","G6B07":"connectors","G6B08":"diodes","G6B10":"ferrite","G6B11":"connectors","G6B12":"connectors"}},{"id":"g7","code":"G7","name":"Practical circuits","exam":3,"G":{"G7A":"Power supplies, schematic symbols","G7B":"Amplifiers, oscillators, digital logic","G7C":"Transceivers, filters, DSP and SDR"},"Q":[["G7A01","What is the function of a power supply bleeder resistor?",["It acts as a fuse for excess voltage","It discharges the filter capacitors when power is removed","It removes shock hazards from the induction coils","It eliminates ground loop current"],1,"A bleeder resistor discharges the filter capacitors when power is removed."],["G7A02","Which of the following components are used in a power supply filter network?",["Diodes","Transformers and transducers","Capacitors and inductors","All these choices are correct"],2,"Power supply filters use capacitors and inductors."],["G7A03","Which type of rectifier circuit uses two diodes and a center-tapped transformer?",["Full-wave","Full-wave bridge","Half-wave","Synchronous"],0,"A full-wave rectifier can use two diodes and a center-tapped transformer."],["G7A04","What is characteristic of a half-wave rectifier in a power supply?",["Only one diode is required","The ripple frequency is twice that of a full-wave rectifier","More current can be drawn from the half-wave rectifier","The output voltage is two times the peak input voltage"],0,"A half-wave rectifier needs only one diode."],["G7A05","What portion of the AC cycle is converted to DC by a half-wave rectifier?",["90 degrees","180 degrees","270 degrees","360 degrees"],1,"A half-wave rectifier converts 180 degrees of each AC cycle."],["G7A06","What portion of the AC cycle is converted to DC by a full-wave rectifier?",["90 degrees","180 degrees","270 degrees","360 degrees"],3,"A full-wave rectifier converts all 360 degrees of the AC cycle."],["G7A07","What is the output waveform of an unfiltered full-wave rectifier connected to a resistive load?",["A series of DC pulses at twice the frequency of the AC input","A series of DC pulses at the same frequency as the AC input","A sine wave at half the frequency of the AC input","A steady DC voltage"],0,"Unfiltered full-wave output is a series of DC pulses at twice the AC frequency."],["G7A08","Which of the following is characteristic of a switchmode power supply as compared to a linear power supply?",["Faster switching time makes higher output voltage possible","Fewer circuit components are required","High-frequency operation allows the use of smaller components","Inherently more stable"],2,"Switchmode supplies run at high frequency, which allows smaller components."],["G7A09","Which symbol in figure G7-1 represents a field effect transistor?",["Symbol 2","Symbol 5","Symbol 1","Symbol 4"],2,"Symbol 1 in figure G7-1 is a field effect transistor.","G7-1"],["G7A10","Which symbol in figure G7-1 represents a Zener diode?",["Symbol 4","Symbol 1","Symbol 11","Symbol 5"],3,"Symbol 5 in figure G7-1 is a Zener diode.","G7-1"],["G7A11","Which symbol in figure G7-1 represents an NPN junction transistor?",["Symbol 1","Symbol 2","Symbol 7","Symbol 11"],1,"Symbol 2 in figure G7-1 is an NPN junction transistor.","G7-1"],["G7A12","Which symbol in Figure G7-1 represents a solid core transformer?",["Symbol 4","Symbol 7","Symbol 6","Symbol 1"],2,"Symbol 6 in figure G7-1 is a solid core transformer.","G7-1"],["G7A13","Which symbol in Figure G7-1 represents a tapped inductor?",["Symbol 7","Symbol 11","Symbol 6","Symbol 1"],0,"Symbol 7 in figure G7-1 is a tapped inductor.","G7-1"],["G7B01","What is the purpose of neutralizing an amplifier?",["To limit the modulation index","To eliminate self-oscillations","To cut off the final amplifier during standby periods","To keep the carrier on frequency"],1,"Neutralizing an amplifier eliminates self-oscillation."],["G7B02","Which of these classes of amplifiers has the highest efficiency?",["Class A","Class B","Class AB","Class C"],3,"Class C amplifiers have the highest efficiency."],["G7B03","Which of the following describes the function of a two-input AND gate?",["Output is high when either or both inputs are low","Output is high only when both inputs are high","Output is low when either or both inputs are high","Output is low only when both inputs are high"],1,"A two-input AND gate's output is high only when both inputs are high."],["G7B04","In a Class A amplifier, what percentage of the time does the amplifying device conduct?",["100%","More than 50% but less than 100%","50%","Less than 50%"],0,"In a Class A amplifier, the device conducts 100% of the time."],["G7B05","How many states does a 3-bit binary counter have?",["3","6","8","16"],2,"A 3-bit binary counter has 8 states."],["G7B06","What is a shift register?",["A clocked array of circuits that passes data in steps along the array","An array of operational amplifiers used for tri-state arithmetic operations","A digital mixer","An analog mixer"],0,"A shift register is a clocked array of circuits that passes data along it in steps."],["G7B07","Which of the following are basic components of a sine wave oscillator?",["An amplifier and a divider","A frequency multiplier and a mixer","A circulator and a filter operating in a feed-forward loop","A filter and an amplifier operating in a feedback loop"],3,"A sine-wave oscillator is a filter and an amplifier in a feedback loop."],["G7B08","How is the efficiency of an RF power amplifier determined?",["Divide the DC input power by the DC output power","Divide the RF output power by the DC input power","Multiply the RF input power by the reciprocal of the RF output power","Add the RF input power to the DC output power"],1,"Amplifier efficiency is RF output power divided by DC input power."],["G7B09","What determines the frequency of an LC oscillator?",["The number of stages in the counter","The number of stages in the divider","The inductance and capacitance in the tank circuit","The time delay of the lag circuit"],2,"An LC oscillator's frequency is set by the inductance and capacitance of its tank circuit."],["G7B10","Which of the following describes a linear amplifier?",["Any RF power amplifier used in conjunction with an amateur transceiver","An amplifier in which the output preserves the input waveform","A Class C high efficiency amplifier","An amplifier used as a frequency multiplier"],1,"A linear amplifier's output preserves the input waveform."],["G7B11","For which of the following modes is a Class C power stage appropriate for amplifying a modulated signal?",["SSB","FM","AM","All these choices are correct"],1,"A Class C stage is suitable for amplifying FM."],["G7C01","What circuit is used to select one of the sidebands from a balanced modulator?",["Carrier oscillator","Filter","IF amplifier","RF amplifier"],1,"A filter selects one sideband from a balanced modulator's output."],["G7C02","What output is produced by a balanced modulator?",["Frequency modulated RF","Audio with equalized frequency response","Audio extracted from the modulation signal","Double-sideband modulated RF"],3,"A balanced modulator produces double-sideband modulated RF."],["G7C03","What is one reason to use an impedance matching transformer at a transmitter output?",["To minimize transmitter power output","To present the desired impedance to the transmitter and feed line","To reduce power supply ripple","To minimize radiation resistance"],1,"An impedance matching transformer presents the desired impedance to the transmitter and feed line."],["G7C04","How is a product detector used?",["Used in test gear to detect spurious mixing products","Used in a transmitter to perform frequency multiplication","Used in an FM receiver to filter out unwanted sidebands","Used in a single sideband receiver to extract the modulated signal"],3,"A product detector extracts the modulating signal in a single sideband receiver."],["G7C05","Which of the following is characteristic of a direct digital synthesizer (DDS)?",["Extremely narrow tuning range","Relatively high-power output","Pure sine wave output","Variable output frequency with the stability of a crystal oscillator"],3,"A DDS gives a variable output frequency with the stability of a crystal oscillator."],["G7C06","Which of the following is an advantage of a digital signal processing (DSP) filter compared to an analog filter?",["A wide range of filter bandwidths and shapes can be created","Fewer digital components are required","Mixing products are greatly reduced","The DSP filter is much more effective at VHF frequencies"],0,"DSP filters can be made in a wide range of bandwidths and shapes."],["G7C07","What term specifies a filter’s attenuation inside its passband?",["Insertion loss","Return loss","Q","Ultimate rejection"],0,"A filter's attenuation inside its passband is its insertion loss."],["G7C08","Which parameter affects receiver sensitivity?",["Input amplifier gain","Demodulator stage bandwidth","Input amplifier noise figure","All these choices are correct"],3,"Receiver sensitivity depends on input amplifier gain, demodulator bandwidth and input amplifier noise figure."],["G7C09","What is the phase difference between the I and Q RF signals that software-defined radio (SDR) equipment uses for modulation and demodulation?",["Zero","90 degrees","180 degrees","45 degrees"],1,"An SDR's I and Q signals are 90 degrees apart."],["G7C10","What is an advantage of using I-Q modulation with software-defined radios (SDRs)?",["The need for high resolution analog-to-digital converters is eliminated","All types of modulation can be created with appropriate processing","Minimum detectible signal level is reduced","Automatic conversion of the signal from digital to analog"],1,"With I-Q modulation, any type of modulation can be created with the right processing."],["G7C11","Which of these functions is performed by software in a software-defined radio (SDR)?",["Filtering","Detection","Modulation","All these choices are correct"],3,"An SDR performs filtering, detection and modulation in software."],["G7C12","What is the frequency above which a low-pass filter’s output power is less than half the input power?",["Notch frequency","Neper frequency","Cutoff frequency","Rolloff frequency"],2,"Above a low-pass filter's cutoff frequency, output power is less than half the input."],["G7C13","What term specifies a filter’s maximum ability to reject signals outside its passband?",["Notch depth","Rolloff","Insertion loss","Ultimate rejection"],3,"A filter's maximum ability to reject signals outside its passband is its ultimate rejection."],["G7C14","The bandwidth of a band-pass filter is measured between what two frequencies?",["Upper and lower half-power","Cutoff and rolloff","Pole and zero","Image and harmonic"],0,"A band-pass filter's bandwidth is measured between its upper and lower half-power frequencies."]],"P":{"supply":["Power supplies","<p>A <b>half-wave</b> rectifier uses one diode and converts <b>180 degrees</b> of each AC cycle; a <b>full-wave</b> rectifier converts all <b>360 degrees</b>, either with two diodes and a center-tapped transformer or with four in a bridge. Unfiltered, full-wave output is a series of DC pulses at <b>twice</b> the AC frequency.</p><p>A filter of <b>capacitors and inductors</b> smooths the pulses. A <b>bleeder resistor</b> discharges the filter capacitors when power is off, for safety. <b>Switchmode</b> supplies run at high frequency, so their transformers and filter parts can be much smaller.</p>"],"symbols":["Figure G7-1","<p>Figure G7-1 is an oscillator followed by an amplifier. The parts the pool asks about: <b>1</b> field effect transistor (a bar for the channel, with an arrow on the gate), <b>2</b> NPN junction transistor (arrow pointing out on the emitter), <b>5</b> Zener diode (a diode whose bar has bent ends), <b>6</b> solid core transformer (two coils with straight lines between them), and <b>7</b> tapped inductor (a coil with a connection partway along).</p><p>The others: 3 diode, 4 varactor diode, 8 and 10 capacitors, 9 resistor, 11 potentiometer.</p>"],"amps":["Amplifiers","<p>Classes, by how much of each cycle the device conducts: <b>Class A</b> all the time (100%, most linear, least efficient), <b>Class B</b> half, <b>AB</b> more than half but less than all of the cycle, <b>Class C</b> less than half (most efficient, but distorts amplitude).</p><p>A <b>linear amplifier</b> preserves the input waveform, which SSB and AM need. Class C suits <b>FM</b>, whose amplitude is constant. Efficiency is <b>RF output power divided by DC input power</b>. <b>Neutralizing</b> an amplifier cancels internal feedback to eliminate self-oscillation.</p>"],"digital":["Digital logic","<p>A two-input <b>AND</b> gate's output is high only when both inputs are high. A counter with <b>n</b> bits has <b>2<sup>n</sup></b> states, so 3 bits give 8. A <b>shift register</b> is a clocked chain of circuits that passes data along one step at a time.</p>"],"osc":["Oscillators and synthesizers","<p>A sine-wave oscillator is a <b>filter and an amplifier in a feedback loop</b>. In an LC oscillator, the <b>inductance and capacitance</b> of the tank circuit set the frequency.</p><p>A <b>direct digital synthesizer</b> (DDS) builds its output digitally from a crystal clock, so it can tune widely with the stability of a crystal.</p>"],"tx":["Inside a transceiver","<p>A <b>balanced modulator</b> produces double-sideband RF with the carrier suppressed; a <b>filter</b> then selects one sideband to make SSB. In the receiver, a <b>product detector</b> recovers the audio from an SSB signal. An impedance matching transformer at the transmitter output presents the right impedance to both the transmitter and the feed line.</p>"],"filters":["Filters and sensitivity","<p>A filter's <b>insertion loss</b> is its attenuation inside the passband; its <b>ultimate rejection</b> is the most it can reject outside it. The <b>cutoff frequency</b> is where output falls to half power, and a band-pass filter's bandwidth is measured between its upper and lower <b>half-power</b> points. <b>DSP</b> filters can be made in a wide range of bandwidths and shapes.</p><p>Receiver sensitivity depends on the input amplifier's gain and noise figure and on the bandwidth.</p>"],"sdr":["Software-defined radio","<p>An SDR does its filtering, detection and modulation in <b>software</b>. It works with two versions of the signal, <b>I</b> and <b>Q</b>, <b>90 degrees</b> apart, from which any type of modulation can be created or decoded.</p>"]},"CON":{"G7A01":"supply","G7A02":"supply","G7A03":"supply","G7A04":"supply","G7A05":"supply","G7A06":"supply","G7A07":"supply","G7A08":"supply","G7A09":"symbols","G7A10":"symbols","G7A11":"symbols","G7A12":"symbols","G7A13":"symbols","G7B01":"amps","G7B02":"amps","G7B03":"digital","G7B04":"amps","G7B05":"digital","G7B06":"digital","G7B07":"osc","G7B08":"amps","G7B09":"osc","G7B10":"amps","G7B11":"amps","G7C01":"tx","G7C02":"tx","G7C03":"tx","G7C04":"tx","G7C05":"osc","G7C06":"filters","G7C07":"filters","G7C08":"filters","G7C09":"sdr","G7C10":"sdr","G7C11":"sdr","G7C12":"filters","G7C13":"filters","G7C14":"filters"}},{"id":"g8","code":"G8","name":"Signals and emissions","exam":3,"G":{"G8A":"Modulation, link budgets","G8B":"Mixers, bandwidth, intermodulation","G8C":"Digital modes"},"Q":[["G8A01","How is direct binary FSK modulation generated?",["By keying an FM transmitter with a sub-audible tone","By changing an oscillator’s frequency directly with a digital control signal","By using a transceiver’s computer data interface protocol to change frequencies","By reconfiguring the CW keying input to act as a tone generator"],1,"Direct binary FSK changes an oscillator's frequency directly with a digital control signal."],["G8A02","What is the name of the process that changes the phase angle of an RF signal to convey information?",["Phase convolution","Phase modulation","Phase transformation","Phase inversion"],1,"Changing the phase angle of an RF signal to carry information is phase modulation."],["G8A03","What is the name of the process that changes the instantaneous frequency of an RF wave to convey information?",["Frequency convolution","Frequency transformation","Frequency conversion","Frequency modulation"],3,"Changing the instantaneous frequency of an RF wave to carry information is frequency modulation."],["G8A04","What emission is produced by a reactance modulator connected to a transmitter RF amplifier stage?",["Multiplex modulation","Phase modulation","Amplitude modulation","Pulse modulation"],1,"A reactance modulator on a transmitter's RF amplifier stage produces phase modulation."],["G8A05","What type of modulation varies the instantaneous power level of the RF signal?",["Power modulation","Phase modulation","Frequency modulation","Amplitude modulation"],3,"Amplitude modulation varies the instantaneous power level of the RF signal."],["G8A06","Which of the following is characteristic of QPSK31?",["It is sideband sensitive","Its encoding provides error correction","Its bandwidth is approximately the same as BPSK31","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: QPSK31 is sideband sensitive, has error correction, and is about as wide as BPSK31."],["G8A07","Which of the following phone emissions uses the narrowest bandwidth?",["Single sideband","Vestigial sideband","Phase modulation","Frequency modulation"],0,"Single sideband is the narrowest phone emission."],["G8A08","Which of the following is an effect of overmodulation?",["Insufficient audio","Insufficient bandwidth","Frequency drift","Excessive bandwidth"],3,"Overmodulation causes excessive bandwidth."],["G8A09","What type of modulation is used by FT8?",["8-tone frequency shift keying","Vestigial sideband","Amplitude compressed AM","8-bit direct sequence spread spectrum"],0,"FT8 uses 8-tone frequency shift keying."],["G8A10","What is meant by the term “flat-topping,” when referring to an amplitude-modulated phone signal?",["Signal distortion caused by insufficient collector current","The transmitter’s automatic level control (ALC) is properly adjusted","Signal distortion caused by excessive drive or speech levels","The transmitter’s carrier is properly suppressed"],2,"Flat-topping is distortion caused by excessive drive or speech levels."],["G8A11","What is the modulation envelope of an AM signal?",["The waveform created by connecting the peak values of the modulated signal","The carrier frequency that contains the signal","Spurious signals that envelop nearby frequencies","The bandwidth of the modulated signal"],0,"An AM signal's modulation envelope is the waveform made by connecting the peaks of the modulated signal."],["G8A12","What is QPSK modulation?",["Modulation using quasi-parallel to serial conversion to reduce bandwidth","Modulation using quadra-pole sideband keying to generate spread spectrum signals","Modulation using Fast Fourier Transforms to generate frequencies at the first, second, third, and fourth harmonics of the carrier frequency to improve noise immunity","Modulation in which digital data is transmitted using 0-, 90-, 180- and 270-degrees phase shift to represent pairs of bits"],3,"QPSK sends digital data using 0, 90, 180 and 270 degree phase shifts, each representing a pair of bits."],["G8A13","What is a link budget?",["The financial costs associated with operating a radio link","The sum of antenna gains minus system losses","The sum of transmit power and antenna gains minus system losses as seen at the receiver","The difference between transmit power and receiver sensitivity"],2,"A link budget is transmit power plus antenna gains minus system losses, as seen at the receiver."],["G8A14","What is link margin?",["The opposite of fade margin","The difference between received power level and minimum required signal level at the input to the receiver","Transmit power minus receiver sensitivity","Receiver sensitivity plus 3 dB"],1,"Link margin is the difference between received power and the minimum signal level the receiver needs."],["G8B01","Which mixer input is varied or tuned to convert signals of different frequencies to an intermediate frequency (IF)?",["Image frequency","Local oscillator","RF input","Beat frequency oscillator"],1,"The local oscillator is tuned to convert different signals to the IF."],["G8B02","What is the term for interference from a signal at twice the IF frequency from the desired signal?",["Quadrature response","Image response","Mixer interference","Intermediate interference"],1,"Interference from a signal twice the IF away from the desired signal is image response."],["G8B03","What is another term for the mixing of two RF signals?",["Heterodyning","Synthesizing","Frequency inversion","Phase inversion"],0,"Mixing two RF signals is also called heterodyning."],["G8B04","What is the stage in a VHF FM transmitter that generates a harmonic of a lower frequency signal to reach the desired operating frequency?",["Mixer","Reactance modulator","Balanced converter","Multiplier"],3,"A multiplier generates a harmonic of a lower frequency to reach the operating frequency."],["G8B05","Which intermodulation products are closest to the original signal frequencies?",["Second harmonics","Even-order","Odd-order","Intercept point"],2,"Odd-order intermodulation products are closest to the original frequencies."],["G8B06","What is the total bandwidth of an FM phone transmission having 5 kHz deviation and 3 kHz modulating frequency?",["3 kHz","5 kHz","8 kHz","16 kHz"],3,"5 kHz deviation with 3 kHz audio gives an FM bandwidth of about 16 kHz."],["G8B07","What is the frequency deviation for a 12.21 MHz reactance modulated oscillator in a 5 kHz deviation, 146.52 MHz FM phone transmitter?",["101.75 Hz","416.7 Hz","5 kHz","60 kHz"],1,"A 12.21 MHz oscillator in a 146.52 MHz, 5 kHz deviation transmitter needs about 416.7 Hz of deviation."],["G8B08","Why is it important to know the duty cycle of the mode you are using when transmitting?",["To aid in tuning your transmitter","Some modes have high duty cycles that could exceed the transmitter’s average power rating","To allow time for the other station to break in during a transmission","To prevent overmodulation"],1,"Some modes have high duty cycles that could exceed a transmitter's average power rating."],["G8B09","Why is it good to match receiver bandwidth to the bandwidth of the operating mode?",["It is required by FCC rules","It minimizes power consumption in the receiver","It improves impedance matching of the antenna","It results in the best signal-to-noise ratio"],3,"Matching receiver bandwidth to the mode gives the best signal-to-noise ratio."],["G8B10","What is the relationship between transmitted symbol rate and bandwidth?",["Symbol rate and bandwidth are not related","Higher symbol rates require wider bandwidth","Lower symbol rates require wider bandwidth","Bandwidth is half the symbol rate"],1,"Higher symbol rates require wider bandwidth."],["G8B11","What combination of a mixer’s Local Oscillator (LO) and RF input frequencies is found in the output?",["The ratio","The average","The sum and difference","The arithmetic product"],2,"A mixer's output contains the sum and difference of the LO and RF frequencies."],["G8B12","What process combines two signals in a non-linear circuit to produce unwanted spurious outputs?",["Intermodulation","Heterodyning","Detection","Rolloff"],0,"Intermodulation combines two signals in a non-linear circuit to produce unwanted outputs."],["G8B13","Which of the following is an odd-order intermodulation product of frequencies F1 and F2?",["5F1-3F2","3F1-F2","2F1-F2","All these choices are correct"],2,"2F1 − F2 is an odd-order intermodulation product."],["G8C02","Which digital mode is used as a low-power beacon for assessing HF propagation?",["WSPR","MFSK16","PSK31","SSB-SC"],0,"WSPR is a low-power beacon mode for assessing HF propagation."],["G8C03","What part of a packet radio frame contains the routing and handling information?",["Directory","Preamble","Header","Trailer"],2,"A packet's header contains its routing and handling information."],["G8C04","Which of the following describes Baudot code?",["A 7-bit code with start, stop, and parity bits","A code using error detection and correction","A 5-bit code with additional start and stop bits","A code using SELCAL and LISTEN"],2,"Baudot is a 5-bit code with additional start and stop bits."],["G8C05","In an ARQ mode, what is meant by a NAK response to a transmitted packet?",["Request retransmission of the packet","Packet was received without error","Receiving station connected and ready for transmissions","Entire file received correctly"],0,"In an ARQ mode, a NAK asks for the packet to be sent again."],["G8C06","What action results from a failure to exchange information due to excessive transmission attempts when using an ARQ mode?",["The checksum overflows","The connection is dropped","Packets will be routed incorrectly","Encoding reverts to the default character set"],1,"Too many failed transmission attempts in an ARQ mode drop the connection."],["G8C07","Which of the following narrow-band digital modes can receive signals with very low signal-to-noise ratios?",["MSK144","FT8","AMTOR","MFSK32"],1,"FT8 can receive signals with very low signal-to-noise ratios."],["G8C08","Which of the following statements is true about PSK31?",["Upper case letters are sent with more power","Upper case letters use longer Varicode bit sequences and thus slow down transmission","Error correction is used to ensure accurate message reception","Higher power is needed as compared to RTTY for similar error rates"],1,"In PSK31, upper-case letters use longer Varicode sequences, which slows transmission."],["G8C09","Which is true of mesh network microwave nodes?",["Having more nodes increases signal strengths","If one node fails, a packet may still reach its target station via an alternate node","Links between two nodes in a network may have different frequencies and bandwidths","More nodes reduce overall microwave out of band interference"],1,"In a mesh network, if one node fails a packet may still reach its target through another node."],["G8C10","How does forward error correction (FEC) allow the receiver to correct data errors?",["By controlling transmitter output power for optimum signal strength","By using the Varicode character set","By transmitting redundant information with the data","By using a parity bit with each character"],2,"Forward error correction sends redundant information with the data so the receiver can fix errors."],["G8C11","How are the two separate frequencies of a Frequency Shift Keyed (FSK) signal identified?",["Dot and dash","On and off","High and low","Mark and space"],3,"The two frequencies of an FSK signal are called mark and space."],["G8C12","Which type of code is used for sending characters in a PSK31 signal?",["Varicode","Viterbi","Volumetric","Binary"],0,"PSK31 sends characters in Varicode."],["G8C13","What is indicated on a waterfall display by one or more vertical lines on either side of a data mode or RTTY signal?",["Long path propagation","Backscatter propagation","Insufficient modulation","Overmodulation"],3,"Vertical lines on either side of a data or RTTY signal on a waterfall mean overmodulation."],["G8C14","Which of the following describes a waterfall display?",["Frequency is horizontal, signal strength is vertical, time is intensity","Frequency is vertical, signal strength is intensity, time is horizontal","Frequency is horizontal, signal strength is intensity, time is vertical","Frequency is vertical, signal strength is horizontal, time is intensity"],2,"On a waterfall, frequency runs across, signal strength is brightness, and time runs down."],["G8C15","What does an FT8 signal report of +3 mean?",["The signal is 3 times the noise level of an equivalent SSB signal","The signal is S3 (weak signals)","The signal-to-noise ratio is equivalent to +3dB in a 2.5 kHz bandwidth","The signal is 3 dB over S9"],2,"An FT8 report of +3 means a signal-to-noise ratio of +3 dB in a 2.5 kHz bandwidth."],["G8C16","Which of the following provide digital voice modes?",["WSPR, MFSK16, and EasyPAL","FT8, FT4, and FST4","Winlink, PACTOR II, and PACTOR III","DMR, D-STAR, and SystemFusion"],3,"DMR, D-STAR and System Fusion are digital voice modes."]],"P":{"modulation":["Kinds of modulation","<p>Information rides on a carrier by changing its <b>amplitude</b> (AM, which varies the instantaneous power), its <b>frequency</b> (FM) or its <b>phase</b> (PM). A <b>reactance modulator</b> on an RF amplifier stage produces phase modulation. <b>Direct FSK</b> shifts an oscillator's frequency with a digital signal.</p><p>Of the voice modes, <b>SSB</b> is the narrowest. The <b>modulation envelope</b> is the outline traced by the signal's peaks. Overdriving an AM or SSB transmitter flattens those peaks (<b>flat-topping</b>) and <b>overmodulation</b> spreads the signal over excessive bandwidth.</p>"],"psk":["PSK and QPSK","<p><b>QPSK</b> sends pairs of bits using four phase shifts: 0, 90, 180 and 270 degrees. <b>QPSK31</b> takes about the same bandwidth as BPSK31, adds error correction, and is sideband sensitive.</p><p><b>PSK31</b> sends characters in <b>Varicode</b>, where common lower-case letters get short codes, so upper case takes longer to send.</p>"],"digital":["FT8, WSPR and the waterfall","<p><b>FT8</b> uses 8-tone frequency shift keying and decodes signals far below the noise. Its reports are signal-to-noise ratio in a 2.5 kHz bandwidth, so +3 means 3 dB above the noise there. <b>WSPR</b> is a low-power beacon mode for checking propagation.</p><p>A <b>waterfall</b> shows frequency across, time down, and signal strength as brightness. Extra vertical lines beside a data or RTTY signal mean <b>overmodulation</b>. The two frequencies of FSK are called <b>mark and space</b>. DMR, D-STAR and System Fusion are digital <b>voice</b> modes.</p>"],"link":["Link budgets","<p>A <b>link budget</b> adds up transmit power and antenna gains and subtracts system losses, to find the signal level at the receiver. The <b>link margin</b> is how far that received level is above the minimum the receiver needs.</p>"],"mixers":["Mixers and multipliers","<p>Mixing two RF signals is <b>heterodyning</b>; the output contains their <b>sum and difference</b>. A receiver tunes its <b>local oscillator</b> to bring each signal to the intermediate frequency (IF). A signal twice the IF away from the one you want can reach the IF too: that's <b>image response</b>.</p><p>VHF FM transmitters often reach their operating frequency with a <b>multiplier</b>, which generates a harmonic of a lower frequency.</p>"],"imd":["Intermodulation","<p>When two signals meet in a non-linear circuit, they create unwanted new frequencies: <b>intermodulation</b>. The order of a product is the sum of the multipliers, so 2F1 &minus; F2 is third order. <b>Odd-order</b> products fall closest to the original signals, which is why they cause the most trouble.</p>"],"bandwidth":["Bandwidth and duty cycle","<p>An FM signal's bandwidth is about <b>2 &times; (deviation + highest audio frequency)</b>: 5 kHz deviation with 3 kHz audio takes 16 kHz. A multiplier multiplies the deviation too, so an oscillator feeding a &times;12 multiplier needs only 1/12 of the final deviation.</p><p>Higher symbol rates need wider bandwidth. Matching receiver bandwidth to the mode gives the best signal-to-noise ratio. Modes with a high <b>duty cycle</b>, like FM and many data modes, can exceed a transmitter's average power rating.</p>"],"packet":["Packets and error control","<p>A packet's <b>header</b> carries its routing and handling information. <b>Baudot</b> (used for RTTY) is a 5-bit code with start and stop bits.</p><p><b>ARQ</b> modes check each packet: a <b>NAK</b> asks for it again, and too many failed attempts drop the connection. <b>Forward error correction</b> sends redundant information so the receiver can fix errors itself. In a <b>mesh network</b>, a packet can still get through by another node if one fails.</p>"]},"CON":{"G8A01":"modulation","G8A02":"modulation","G8A03":"modulation","G8A04":"modulation","G8A05":"modulation","G8A06":"psk","G8A07":"modulation","G8A08":"modulation","G8A09":"digital","G8A10":"modulation","G8A11":"modulation","G8A12":"psk","G8A13":"link","G8A14":"link","G8B01":"mixers","G8B02":"mixers","G8B03":"mixers","G8B04":"mixers","G8B05":"imd","G8B06":"bandwidth","G8B07":"bandwidth","G8B08":"bandwidth","G8B09":"bandwidth","G8B10":"bandwidth","G8B11":"mixers","G8B12":"imd","G8B13":"imd","G8C02":"digital","G8C03":"packet","G8C04":"packet","G8C05":"packet","G8C06":"packet","G8C07":"digital","G8C08":"psk","G8C09":"packet","G8C10":"packet","G8C11":"digital","G8C12":"psk","G8C13":"digital","G8C14":"digital","G8C15":"digital","G8C16":"digital"}},{"id":"g9","code":"G9","name":"Antennas and feed lines","exam":4,"G":{"G9A":"Feed lines and SWR","G9B":"Dipoles and verticals","G9C":"Yagis and directional antennas","G9D":"Specialized antennas"},"Q":[["G9A01","Which of the following factors determine the characteristic impedance of a parallel conductor feed line?",["The distance between the centers of the conductors and the radius of the conductors","The distance between the centers of the conductors and the length of the line","The radius of the conductors and the frequency of the signal","The frequency of the signal and the length of the line"],0,"A parallel-conductor line's characteristic impedance is set by the conductor spacing and radius."],["G9A02","What is the relationship between high standing wave ratio (SWR) and transmission line loss?",["There is no relationship between transmission line loss and SWR","High SWR increases loss in a lossy transmission line","High SWR makes it difficult to measure transmission line loss","High SWR reduces the relative effect of transmission line loss"],1,"High SWR increases loss in a lossy transmission line."],["G9A03","What is the nominal characteristic impedance of “window line” transmission line?",["50 ohms","75 ohms","100 ohms","450 ohms"],3,"Window line is nominally 450 ohms."],["G9A04","What causes reflected power at an antenna’s feed point?",["Operating an antenna at its resonant frequency","Using more transmitter power than the antenna can handle","A difference between feed line impedance and antenna feed point impedance","Feeding the antenna with unbalanced feed line"],2,"Reflected power comes from a mismatch between the feed line's impedance and the antenna's feed point impedance."],["G9A05","How does the attenuation of coaxial cable change with increasing frequency?",["Attenuation is independent of frequency","Attenuation increases","Attenuation decreases","Attenuation follows Marconi’s Law of Attenuation"],1,"Coax attenuation increases with frequency."],["G9A06","In what units is RF feed line loss usually expressed?",["Ohms per 1,000 feet","Decibels per 1,000 feet","Ohms per 100 feet","Decibels per 100 feet"],3,"Feed line loss is usually expressed in decibels per 100 feet."],["G9A07","What must be done to prevent standing waves on a feed line connected to an antenna?",["The antenna feed point must be at DC ground potential","The feed line must be an odd number of electrical quarter wavelengths long","The feed line must be an even number of physical half wavelengths long","The antenna feed point impedance must be matched to the characteristic impedance of the feed line"],3,"To prevent standing waves, match the antenna's feed point impedance to the feed line's characteristic impedance."],["G9A08","If the SWR on an antenna feed line is 5:1, and a matching network at the transmitter end of the feed line is adjusted to present a 1:1 SWR to the transmitter, what is the resulting SWR on the feed line?",["1:1","5:1","Between 1:1 and 5:1 depending on the characteristic impedance of the line","Between 1:1 and 5:1 depending on the reflected power at the transmitter"],1,"The SWR on the feed line stays 5:1; the tuner only changes what the transmitter sees."],["G9A09","What standing wave ratio results from connecting a 50-ohm feed line to a 200-ohm resistive load?",["4:1","1:4","2:1","1:2"],0,"A 50-ohm line to a 200-ohm resistive load gives an SWR of 4:1."],["G9A10","What standing wave ratio results from connecting a 50-ohm feed line to a 10-ohm resistive load?",["2:1","1:2","1:5","5:1"],3,"A 50-ohm line to a 10-ohm resistive load gives an SWR of 5:1."],["G9A11","What is the effect of transmission line loss on SWR measured at the input to the line?",["Higher loss reduces SWR measured at the input to the line","Higher loss increases SWR measured at the input to the line","Higher loss increases the accuracy of SWR measured at the input to the line","Transmission line loss does not affect the SWR measurement"],0,"Higher line loss reduces the SWR measured at the input to the line."],["G9B01","What is a characteristic of a random-wire HF antenna connected directly to the transmitter?",["It must be longer than 1 wavelength","Station equipment may carry significant RF current","It produces only vertically polarized radiation","It is more effective on the lower HF bands than on the higher bands"],1,"With a random wire fed directly from the transmitter, station equipment may carry significant RF current."],["G9B02","Which of the following is a common way to adjust the feed point impedance of an elevated quarter-wave ground-plane vertical antenna to be approximately 50 ohms?",["Slope the radials upward","Slope the radials downward","Lengthen the radials beyond one wavelength","Coil the radials"],1,"Sloping the radials downward brings a ground-plane vertical's impedance close to 50 ohms."],["G9B03","Which of the following best describes the radiation pattern of a quarter-wave ground-plane vertical antenna?",["Bi-directional in azimuth","Isotropic","Hemispherical","Omnidirectional in azimuth"],3,"A quarter-wave ground-plane vertical is omnidirectional in azimuth."],["G9B04","What is the radiation pattern of a dipole antenna in free space in a plane containing the conductor?",["It is a figure-eight at right angles to the antenna","It is a figure-eight off both ends of the antenna","It is a circle (equal radiation in all directions)","It has a pair of lobes on one side of the antenna and a single lobe on the other side"],0,"In free space, a dipole's pattern in the plane of the wire is a figure-eight at right angles to the antenna."],["G9B05","How does antenna height affect the azimuthal radiation pattern of a horizontal dipole HF antenna at elevation angles higher than about 45 degrees?",["If the antenna is too high, the pattern becomes unpredictable","Antenna height has no effect on the pattern","If the antenna is less than 1/2 wavelength high, the azimuthal pattern is almost omnidirectional","If the antenna is less than 1/2 wavelength high, radiation off the ends of the wire is eliminated"],2,"A horizontal dipole less than half a wavelength high radiates almost equally in all directions at high angles."],["G9B06","Where should the radial wires of a ground-mounted vertical antenna system be placed?",["As high as possible above the ground","Parallel to the antenna element","On the surface or buried a few inches below the ground","At the center of the antenna"],2,"A ground-mounted vertical's radials go on the surface or buried a few inches."],["G9B07","How does the feed point impedance of a horizontal 1/2 wave dipole antenna change as the antenna height is reduced to 1/10 wavelength above ground?",["It steadily increases","It steadily decreases","It peaks at about 1/8 wavelength above ground","It is unaffected by the height above ground"],1,"A dipole's feed point impedance steadily decreases as it's lowered toward 1/10 wavelength."],["G9B08","How does the feed point impedance of a 1/2 wave dipole change as the feed point is moved from the center toward the ends?",["It steadily increases","It steadily decreases","It peaks at about 1/8 wavelength from the end","It is unaffected by the location of the feed point"],0,"A dipole's feed point impedance steadily increases as the feed point moves from the center toward the ends."],["G9B09","Which of the following is an advantage of using a horizontally polarized as compared to a vertically polarized HF antenna?",["Lower ground losses","Lower feed point impedance","Shorter radials","Lower radiation resistance"],0,"Horizontally polarized HF antennas have lower ground losses than vertical ones."],["G9B10","What is the approximate length for a 1/2 wave dipole antenna cut for 14.250 MHz?",["8 feet","16 feet","24 feet","33 feet"],3,"A half-wave dipole for 14.250 MHz is about 33 feet long."],["G9B11","What is the approximate length for a 1/2 wave dipole antenna cut for 3.550 MHz?",["42 feet","84 feet","132 feet","263 feet"],2,"A half-wave dipole for 3.550 MHz is about 132 feet long."],["G9B12","What is the approximate length for a 1/4 wave monopole antenna cut for 28.5 MHz?",["8 feet","11 feet","16 feet","21 feet"],0,"A quarter-wave monopole for 28.5 MHz is about 8 feet long."],["G9C01","Which of the following would increase the bandwidth of a Yagi antenna?",["Larger-diameter elements","Closer element spacing","Loading coils in series with the element","Tapered-diameter elements"],0,"Larger-diameter elements increase a Yagi's bandwidth."],["G9C02","What is the approximate length of the driven element of a Yagi antenna?",["1/4 wavelength","1/2 wavelength","3/4 wavelength","1 wavelength"],1,"A Yagi's driven element is about half a wavelength long."],["G9C03","How do the lengths of a three-element Yagi reflector and director compare to that of the driven element?",["The reflector is longer, and the director is shorter","The reflector is shorter, and the director is longer","They are all the same length","Relative length depends on the frequency of operation"],0,"In a 3-element Yagi, the reflector is longer and the director shorter than the driven element."],["G9C04","How does antenna gain in dBi compare to gain stated in dBd for the same antenna?",["Gain in dBi is 2.15 dB lower","Gain in dBi is 2.15 dB higher","Gain in dBd is 1.25 dBd lower","Gain in dBd is 1.25 dBd higher"],1,"Gain in dBi is 2.15 dB higher than the same gain stated in dBd."],["G9C05","What is the primary effect of increasing boom length and adding directors to a Yagi antenna?",["Gain increases","Beamwidth increases","Front-to-back ratio decreases","Resonant frequency is lower"],0,"A longer boom with more directors mainly increases gain."],["G9C07","What does “front-to-back ratio” mean in reference to a Yagi antenna?",["The number of directors versus the number of reflectors","The relative position of the driven element with respect to the reflectors and directors","The power radiated in the major lobe compared to that in the opposite direction","The ratio of forward gain to dipole gain"],2,"Front-to-back ratio compares the power radiated in the main lobe with that in the opposite direction."],["G9C08","What is meant by the “main lobe” of a directive antenna?",["The magnitude of the maximum vertical angle of radiation","The point of maximum current in a radiating antenna element","The maximum voltage standing wave point on a radiating element","The direction of maximum radiated field strength from the antenna"],3,"The main lobe is the direction of maximum radiated field strength."],["G9C09","In free space, how does the gain of two three-element, horizontally polarized Yagi antennas spaced vertically 1/2 wavelength apart typically compare to the gain of a single three-element Yagi?",["Approximately 1.5 dB higher","Approximately 3 dB higher","Approximately 6 dB higher","Approximately 9 dB higher"],1,"Two Yagis stacked half a wavelength apart give about 3 dB more gain than one."],["G9C10","Which of the following can be adjusted to optimize forward gain, front-to-back ratio, or SWR bandwidth of a Yagi antenna?",["The physical length of the boom","The number of elements on the boom","The spacing of each element along the boom","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: boom length, number of elements and element spacing can be adjusted to optimize a Yagi."],["G9C11","What is a beta or hairpin match?",["A shorted transmission line stub placed at the feed point of a Yagi antenna to provide impedance matching","A 1/4 wavelength section of 75-ohm coax in series with the feed point of a Yagi to provide impedance matching","A series capacitor selected to cancel the inductive reactance of a folded dipole antenna","A section of 300-ohm twin-lead transmission line used to match a folded dipole antenna"],0,"A beta or hairpin match is a shorted transmission line stub at a Yagi's feed point."],["G9C12","Which of the following is a characteristic of using a gamma match with a Yagi antenna?",["It does not require the driven element to be insulated from the boom","It does not require any inductors or capacitors","It is useful for matching multiband antennas","All these choices are correct"],0,"A gamma match doesn't require the driven element to be insulated from the boom."],["G9D01","Which of the following antenna types will be most effective as a near vertical incidence skywave (NVIS) antenna for short-skip communications on 40 meters during the day?",["A horizontal dipole placed between 1/10 and 1/4 wavelength above the ground","A vertical antenna placed between 1/4 and 1/2 wavelength above the ground","A horizontal dipole placed at approximately 1/2 wavelength above the ground","A vertical dipole placed at approximately 1/2 wavelength above the ground"],0,"For 40-meter NVIS in daytime, use a horizontal dipole 1/10 to 1/4 wavelength above ground."],["G9D02","What is the feed point impedance of an end-fed half-wave antenna?",["Very low","Approximately 50 ohms","Approximately 300 ohms","Very high"],3,"An end-fed half-wave antenna's feed point impedance is very high."],["G9D03","In which direction is the maximum radiation from a VHF/UHF “halo” antenna?",["Broadside to the plane of the halo","Opposite the feed point","Omnidirectional in the plane of the halo","On the same side as the feed point"],2,"A halo radiates most strongly in all directions in the plane of the halo."],["G9D04","What is the primary function of antenna traps?",["To enable multiband operation","To notch spurious frequencies","To provide balanced feed point impedance","To prevent out-of-band operation"],0,"Antenna traps enable multiband operation."],["G9D05","What is an advantage of vertically stacking horizontally polarized Yagi antennas?",["It allows quick selection of vertical or horizontal polarization","It allows simultaneous vertical and horizontal polarization","It narrows the main lobe in azimuth","It narrows the main lobe in elevation"],3,"Vertically stacking horizontally polarized Yagis narrows the main lobe in elevation."],["G9D06","Which of the following is an advantage of a log-periodic antenna?",["Wide bandwidth","Higher gain per element than a Yagi antenna","Harmonic suppression","Polarization diversity"],0,"A log-periodic antenna's advantage is wide bandwidth."],["G9D07","Which of the following describes a log-periodic antenna?",["Element length and spacing vary logarithmically along the boom","Impedance varies periodically as a function of frequency","Gain varies logarithmically as a function of frequency","SWR varies periodically as a function of boom length"],0,"In a log-periodic antenna, element length and spacing vary logarithmically along the boom."],["G9D08","How does a “screwdriver” mobile antenna adjust its feed point impedance?",["By varying its body capacitance","By varying the base loading inductance","By extending and retracting the whip","By deploying a capacitance hat"],1,"A screwdriver antenna adjusts by varying its base loading inductance."],["G9D09","What is the primary use of a Beverage antenna?",["Directional receiving for MF and low HF bands","Directional transmitting for low HF bands","Portable direction finding at higher HF frequencies","Portable direction finding at lower HF frequencies"],0,"A Beverage antenna is for directional receiving on MF and the low HF bands."],["G9D10","In which direction or directions does an electrically small loop (less than 1/10 wavelength in circumference) have nulls in its radiation pattern?",["In the plane of the loop","Broadside to the loop","Broadside and in the plane of the loop","Electrically small loops are omnidirectional"],1,"An electrically small loop has its nulls broadside to the loop."],["G9D11","Which of the following is a disadvantage of multiband antennas?",["They present low impedance on all design frequencies","They must be used with an antenna tuner","They must be fed with open wire line","They have poor harmonic rejection"],3,"Multiband antennas have poor harmonic rejection."],["G9D12","What is the common name of a dipole with a single central support?",["Inverted V","Inverted L","Sloper","Lazy H"],0,"A dipole with a single central support is an inverted V."]],"P":{"feedline":["Feed lines","<p>A parallel-conductor line's characteristic impedance is set by the <b>spacing between its conductors and their radius</b>. <b>Window line</b> is nominally <b>450 ohms</b>. Coax loss <b>rises with frequency</b> and is quoted in <b>dB per 100 feet</b>.</p><p>High SWR <b>increases</b> the loss in a lossy line. Loss also hides SWR: reflected power is weakened on its way back, so the SWR measured at the transmitter end reads <b>lower</b> than at the antenna.</p>"],"swr":["SWR and matching","<p>Power reflects at the feed point when the antenna's impedance <b>differs from the feed line's</b>; matching them prevents standing waves. For a resistive load, SWR is the larger impedance divided by the smaller: 200 ohms on 50-ohm line is 4:1, and 10 ohms is 5:1.</p><p>A tuner at the transmitter makes the transmitter see 1:1, but the SWR on the feed line itself is <b>unchanged</b>.</p>"],"wire":["Dipoles","<p>A half-wave dipole is about <b>468 &divide; frequency in MHz</b> feet long: about 33 feet at 14.25 MHz, 132 feet at 3.55 MHz. In free space its pattern is a <b>figure-eight at right angles to the wire</b>. Below half a wavelength high, it radiates nearly equally in all directions at high angles.</p><p>Below about 1/4 wavelength, its feed point impedance <b>falls</b> as it's lowered toward 1/10 wavelength. At any height, it <b>rises</b> as the feed point moves from the center toward the ends. Horizontal antennas have <b>lower ground losses</b> than verticals. A random wire connected straight to the transmitter can leave <b>RF current on the station equipment</b>.</p>"],"verticals":["Vertical antennas","<p>A quarter-wave ground-plane vertical radiates <b>equally in all directions</b> around it (omnidirectional in azimuth). <b>Sloping its radials downward</b> raises its feed point impedance to about 50 ohms. A quarter-wave monopole is about <b>234 &divide; MHz</b> feet: about 8 feet at 28.5 MHz.</p><p>A ground-mounted vertical's radials go <b>on the surface or a few inches under it</b>.</p>"],"yagi":["Yagis","<p>A Yagi's driven element is about <b>half a wavelength</b>. The <b>reflector is longer</b> and the <b>directors shorter</b>. A longer boom with more directors gives <b>more gain</b>; element length and spacing trade off gain, front-to-back ratio and SWR bandwidth; larger-diameter elements widen the bandwidth.</p><p>The <b>main lobe</b> is the direction of strongest radiation; <b>front-to-back ratio</b> compares the power in the main lobe with the power in the opposite direction. Gain in <b>dBi</b> (over an isotropic antenna) is <b>2.15 dB higher</b> than the same gain in dBd (over a dipole).</p>"],"stacking":["Stacking","<p>Two Yagis stacked vertically, about half a wavelength apart, give about <b>3 dB</b> more gain than one. Stacking horizontally polarized Yagis vertically <b>narrows the main lobe in elevation</b>.</p>"],"yagimatch":["Matching a Yagi","<p>A <b>beta or hairpin match</b> is a shorted stub of transmission line across the feed point. A <b>gamma match</b> taps the element off-center. Since the element's center is at zero RF voltage, the element can be mounted directly on the boom without insulation.</p>"],"special":["Special-purpose antennas","<p>For <b>NVIS</b> on 40 meters, a horizontal dipole only <b>1/10 to 1/4 wavelength</b> high sends signals nearly straight up. An <b>end-fed half-wave</b> has a <b>very high</b> feed point impedance. A <b>halo</b> radiates roughly equally in all directions in its own plane. A <b>screwdriver</b> mobile antenna tunes by changing its base loading inductance.</p><p>A <b>Beverage</b> is a long, low wire for <b>directional receiving</b> on MF and the low HF bands. An electrically small loop has its nulls <b>broadside</b> to the loop. A dipole hung from one central support is an <b>inverted V</b>.</p>"],"multiband":["Multiband antennas","<p><b>Traps</b> (tuned circuits in the elements) let one antenna work on several bands. A <b>log-periodic</b> antenna, whose element lengths and spacing change logarithmically along the boom, covers a <b>wide bandwidth</b>. The downside of multiband antennas: <b>poor harmonic rejection</b>, since a harmonic may land on another band they're built for.</p>"]},"CON":{"G9A01":"feedline","G9A02":"feedline","G9A03":"feedline","G9A04":"swr","G9A05":"feedline","G9A06":"feedline","G9A07":"swr","G9A08":"swr","G9A09":"swr","G9A10":"swr","G9A11":"feedline","G9B01":"wire","G9B02":"verticals","G9B03":"verticals","G9B04":"wire","G9B05":"wire","G9B06":"verticals","G9B07":"wire","G9B08":"wire","G9B09":"wire","G9B10":"wire","G9B11":"wire","G9B12":"verticals","G9C01":"yagi","G9C02":"yagi","G9C03":"yagi","G9C04":"yagi","G9C05":"yagi","G9C07":"yagi","G9C08":"yagi","G9C09":"stacking","G9C10":"yagi","G9C11":"yagimatch","G9C12":"yagimatch","G9D01":"special","G9D02":"special","G9D03":"special","G9D04":"multiband","G9D05":"stacking","G9D06":"multiband","G9D07":"multiband","G9D08":"special","G9D09":"special","G9D10":"special","G9D11":"multiband","G9D12":"special"}},{"id":"g0","code":"G0","name":"Electrical and RF safety","exam":2,"G":{"G0A":"RF exposure","G0B":"Electrical, lightning and tower safety"},"Q":[["G0A01","What is one way that RF energy can affect human body tissue?",["It heats body tissue","It causes radiation poisoning","It causes the blood count to reach a dangerously low level","It cools body tissue"],0,"RF energy can heat body tissue."],["G0A02","Which of the following is used to determine RF exposure from a transmitted signal?",["Its duty cycle","Its frequency","Its power density","All these choices are correct"],3,"Duty cycle, frequency and power density are all used to determine RF exposure."],["G0A03","How can you determine that your station complies with FCC RF exposure regulations?",["By calculation based on FCC OET Bulletin 65","By calculation based on computer modeling","By measurement of field strength using calibrated equipment","All these choices are correct"],3,"All of them: you can check compliance by calculation from OET Bulletin 65, by computer modeling, or by measurement."],["G0A04","What does “time averaging” mean when evaluating RF radiation exposure?",["The average amount of power developed by the transmitter over a specific 24-hour period","The average time it takes RF radiation to have any long-term effect on the body","The total time of the exposure","The total RF exposure averaged over a certain period"],3,"Time averaging means the total RF exposure averaged over a certain period."],["G0A05","What must you do if an evaluation of your station shows that the RF energy radiated by your station exceeds permissible limits for possible human absorption?",["Take action to prevent human exposure to the excessive RF fields","File an Environmental Impact Statement (EIS-97) with the FCC","Secure written permission from your neighbors to operate above the controlled MPE limits","All these choices are correct"],0,"If your station exceeds the limits, take action to prevent human exposure to the excessive fields."],["G0A06","What must you do if your station fails to meet the FCC RF exposure exemption criteria?",["Perform an RF exposure evaluation in accordance with FCC OET Bulletin 65","Contact the FCC for permission to transmit","Perform an RF exposure evaluation in accordance with World Meteorological Organization guidelines","Use an FCC-approved band-pass filter"],0,"If your station fails the exemption criteria, perform an RF exposure evaluation using OET Bulletin 65."],["G0A07","What is the effect of modulation duty cycle on RF exposure?",["A lower duty cycle permits greater power levels to be transmitted","A higher duty cycle permits greater power levels to be transmitted","Low duty cycle transmitters are exempt from RF exposure evaluation requirements","High duty cycle transmitters are exempt from RF exposure requirements"],0,"A lower duty cycle permits greater power for the same exposure."],["G0A08","Which of the following steps must an amateur operator take to ensure compliance with RF safety regulations?",["Post a copy of FCC Part 97.13 in the station","Notify neighbors within a 100-foot radius of the antenna of the existence of the station and power levels","Perform a routine RF exposure evaluation and prevent access to any identified high exposure areas","All these choices are correct"],2,"Perform a routine RF exposure evaluation and prevent access to any high-exposure areas."],["G0A09","What type of instrument can be used to accurately measure an RF field strength?",["A receiver with digital signal processing (DSP) noise reduction","A calibrated field strength meter with a calibrated antenna","An SWR meter with a peak-reading function","An oscilloscope with a high-stability crystal marker generator"],1,"A calibrated field strength meter with a calibrated antenna can accurately measure RF field strength."],["G0A10","What should be done if evaluation shows that a neighbor might experience more than the allowable limit of RF exposure from the main lobe of a directional antenna?",["Change to a non-polarized antenna with higher gain","Use an antenna with a higher front-to-back ratio","Take precautions to ensure that the antenna cannot be pointed in their direction when they are present","All these choices are correct"],2,"If a neighbor could be over the limit in your beam's main lobe, make sure it can't point their way when they're there."],["G0A11","What precaution should be taken if you install an indoor transmitting antenna?",["Locate the antenna close to your operating position to minimize feed-line radiation","Position the antenna along the edge of a wall to reduce parasitic radiation","Make sure that MPE limits are not exceeded in occupied areas","Make sure the antenna is properly shielded"],2,"With an indoor transmitting antenna, make sure exposure limits aren't exceeded in occupied areas."],["G0A12","What stations are subject to the FCC rules on RF exposure?",["All commercial stations; amateur radio stations are exempt","Only stations with antennas lower than one wavelength above the ground","Only stations transmitting more than 500 watts PEP","All stations with a time-averaged transmission of more than one milliwatt"],3,"The RF exposure rules cover all stations with a time-averaged transmission of more than one milliwatt."],["G0B01","Which wire or wires in a four-conductor 240 VAC circuit should be attached to fuses or circuit breakers?",["Only the hot wires","Only the neutral wire","Only the ground wire","All wires"],0,"In a four-conductor 240 VAC circuit, fuse or break only the hot wires."],["G0B02","According to the National Electrical Code, what is the minimum wire size that may be used safely for wiring with a 20-ampere circuit breaker?",["AWG number 20","AWG number 16","AWG number 12","AWG number 8"],2,"A 20-amp circuit needs at least AWG 12 wire."],["G0B03","Which size of fuse or circuit breaker would be appropriate to use with a circuit that uses AWG number 14 wiring?",["30 amperes","25 amperes","20 amperes","15 amperes"],3,"AWG 14 wiring calls for a 15-amp fuse or breaker."],["G0B04","Where should the station’s lightning protection ground system be located?",["As close to the station equipment as possible","Outside the building","Next to the closest power pole","Parallel to the water supply line"],1,"The lightning protection ground system belongs outside the building."],["G0B05","Which of the following conditions will cause a ground fault circuit interrupter (GFCI) to disconnect AC power?",["Current flowing from one or more of the hot wires to the neutral wire","Current flowing from one or more of the hot wires directly to ground","Overvoltage on the hot wires","All these choices are correct"],1,"A GFCI trips when current flows from a hot wire directly to ground."],["G0B06","Which of the following is covered by the National Electrical Code?",["Acceptable bandwidth limits","Acceptable modulation limits","Electrical safety of the station","RF exposure limits of the human body"],2,"The National Electrical Code covers the electrical safety of the station."],["G0B07","Which of these choices should be observed when climbing a tower using a safety harness?",["Always hold on to the tower with one hand","Confirm that the harness is rated for the weight of the climber and that it is within its allowable service life","Ensure that all heavy tools are securely fastened to the harness","All these choices are correct"],1,"Confirm the harness is rated for the climber's weight and within its allowable service life."],["G0B08","What should be done before climbing a tower that supports electrically powered devices?",["Notify the electric company that a person will be working on the tower","Make sure all circuits that supply power to the tower are locked out and tagged","Unground the base of the tower","All these choices are correct"],1,"Before climbing a tower with powered devices, lock out and tag all circuits that supply power to it."],["G0B09","Which of the following is true of an emergency generator installation?",["The generator should be operated in a well-ventilated area","The generator must be insulated from ground","Fuel should be stored near the generator for rapid refueling in case of an emergency","All these choices are correct"],0,"Run an emergency generator outdoors, in a well-ventilated area, never indoors."],["G0B10","Which of the following is a danger from lead-tin solder?",["Lead can contaminate food if hands are not washed carefully after handling the solder","High voltages can cause lead-tin solder to disintegrate suddenly","Tin in the solder can “cold flow,” causing shorts in the circuit","RF energy can convert the lead into a poisonous gas"],0,"Lead from solder can contaminate food if you don't wash your hands after handling it."],["G0B11","Which of the following is required for lightning protection ground rods?",["They must be bonded to all buried water and gas lines","Bends in ground wires must be made as close as possible to a right angle","Lightning grounds must be connected to all ungrounded wiring","They must be bonded together with all other grounds"],3,"Lightning protection ground rods must be bonded together with all other grounds."],["G0B12","What is the purpose of a power supply interlock?",["To prevent unauthorized changes to the circuit that would void the manufacturer’s warranty","To shut down the unit if it becomes too hot","To ensure that dangerous voltages are removed if the cabinet is opened","To shut off the power supply if too much voltage is produced"],2,"A power supply interlock removes dangerous voltages when the cabinet is opened."],["G0B13","Where should lightning arrestors be located?",["Where the feed lines enter the building","On the antenna, opposite the feed point","In series with each ground lead","At the closest power pole ground electrode"],0,"Lightning arrestors belong where the feed lines enter the building."]],"P":{"rfexposure":["How RF affects the body","<p>RF energy's main known effect on the body at amateur frequencies is <b>heating</b> tissue. Exposure depends on the signal's <b>power density, frequency and duty cycle</b>, and limits are <b>time-averaged</b>: total exposure averaged over a set period. So a <b>lower duty cycle</b> allows more power for the same exposure.</p>"],"evaluation":["Evaluating your station","<p>The FCC's RF exposure rules apply to <b>every station</b> with a time-averaged output of more than one milliwatt. If your station doesn't meet the exemption criteria, you must <b>evaluate</b> it using FCC <b>OET Bulletin 65</b>: by calculation, computer modeling, or measurement with a <b>calibrated field strength meter and calibrated antenna</b>.</p><p>If the evaluation shows limits could be exceeded where people can go, you must <b>prevent exposure</b>: keep people out of those areas, or make sure a beam can't point at a neighbor when they're there. With an indoor antenna, make sure exposure limits aren't exceeded in occupied rooms.</p>"],"electrical":["Electrical safety","<p>The <b>National Electrical Code</b> covers the electrical safety of your station. Fuse only the <b>hot</b> wires. Wire sizes: a 20-amp breaker needs at least <b>AWG 12</b>; AWG 14 wire gets a <b>15-amp</b> fuse or breaker. A <b>GFCI</b> trips when current flows from a hot wire directly to ground. A power supply <b>interlock</b> removes dangerous voltages when the cabinet is opened.</p><p>Run a generator only <b>outdoors</b> in a well-ventilated area, never indoors or in a garage (carbon monoxide). Lead solder can contaminate food, so wash your hands after handling it.</p>"],"lightning":["Lightning protection","<p>Put the lightning ground system <b>outside the building</b>, with lightning arrestors <b>where the feed lines enter</b>. All ground rods must be <b>bonded together</b> with all the other grounds, so everything stays at the same potential during a strike.</p>"],"tower":["Tower safety","<p>Before climbing, make sure the safety harness is <b>rated for your weight</b> and within its service life. If the tower carries powered equipment, <b>lock out and tag</b> every circuit that feeds it.</p>"]},"CON":{"G0A01":"rfexposure","G0A02":"rfexposure","G0A03":"evaluation","G0A04":"rfexposure","G0A05":"evaluation","G0A06":"evaluation","G0A07":"rfexposure","G0A08":"evaluation","G0A09":"evaluation","G0A10":"evaluation","G0A11":"evaluation","G0A12":"evaluation","G0B01":"electrical","G0B02":"electrical","G0B03":"electrical","G0B04":"lightning","G0B05":"electrical","G0B06":"electrical","G0B07":"tower","G0B08":"tower","G0B09":"electrical","G0B10":"electrical","G0B11":"lightning","G0B12":"electrical","G0B13":"lightning"}},{"id":"math","code":"Math","name":"Electronics math","exam":0,"G":{"G5B":"Decibels, power, RMS and PEP","G5C":"Series, parallel and transformers","X":"Math questions elsewhere in the pool"},"Q":[["G5B01","What dB change represents a factor of two increase or decrease in power?",["Approximately 2 dB","Approximately 3 dB","Approximately 6 dB","Approximately 9 dB"],1,"Twice or half the power is about 3 dB."],["G5B10","What percentage of power loss is equivalent to a loss of 1 dB?",["10.9 percent","12.2 percent","20.6 percent","25.9 percent"],2,"1 dB down leaves 10^(−0.1) ≈ 0.794 of the power: a 20.6% loss."],["G5B03","How many watts of electrical power are consumed if 400 VDC is supplied to an 800-ohm load?",["0.5 watts","200 watts","400 watts","3200 watts"],1,"P = E²/R = 400² ÷ 800 = 160,000 ÷ 800 = 200 W."],["G5B04","How many watts of electrical power are consumed by a 12 VDC light bulb that draws 0.2 amperes?",["2.4 watts","24 watts","6 watts","60 watts"],0,"P = E × I = 12 × 0.2 = 2.4 W."],["G5B05","How many watts are consumed when a current of 7.0 milliamperes flows through a 1,250-ohm resistance?",["Approximately 61 milliwatts","Approximately 61 watts","Approximately 11 milliwatts","Approximately 11 watts"],0,"P = I² × R = 0.007² × 1,250 = 0.061 W, about 61 mW."],["G5B06","What is the PEP produced by 200 volts peak-to-peak across a 50-ohm dummy load?",["1.4 watts","100 watts","353.5 watts","400 watts"],1,"200 V p-p → 100 V peak → 70.7 V RMS; 70.7² ÷ 50 = 100 W PEP."],["G5B08","What is the peak-to-peak voltage of a sine wave with an RMS voltage of 120 volts?",["84.8 volts","169.7 volts","240.0 volts","339.4 volts"],3,"120 V RMS × 1.414 = 169.7 V peak; × 2 = 339.4 V peak-to-peak."],["G5B09","What is the RMS voltage of a sine wave with a value of 17 volts peak?",["8.5 volts","12 volts","24 volts","34 volts"],1,"17 V peak × 0.707 = 12 V RMS."],["G5B12","What is the RMS voltage across a 50-ohm dummy load dissipating 1200 watts?",["173 volts","245 volts","346 volts","692 volts"],1,"E = √(P × R) = √(1,200 × 50) = √60,000 ≈ 245 V RMS."],["G5B13","What is the output PEP of an unmodulated carrier if the average power is 1060 watts?",["530 watts","1060 watts","1500 watts","2120 watts"],1,"An unmodulated carrier's PEP equals its average power: 1,060 W."],["G5B14","What is the output PEP of 500 volts peak-to-peak across a 50-ohm load?",["8.75 watts","625 watts","2500 watts","5000 watts"],1,"500 V p-p → 250 V peak → 176.8 V RMS; 176.8² ÷ 50 = 625 W PEP."],["G5C03","What is the total resistance of a 10-, a 20-, and a 50-ohm resistor connected in parallel?",["5.9 ohms","0.17 ohms","17 ohms","80 ohms"],0,"1/R = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/50 = 0.17, so R ≈ 5.9 Ω."],["G5C04","What is the approximate total resistance of a 100- and a 200-ohm resistor in parallel?",["300 ohms","150 ohms","75 ohms","67 ohms"],3,"(100 × 200) ÷ (100 + 200) = 20,000 ÷ 300 ≈ 67 Ω."],["G5C08","What is the equivalent capacitance of two 5.0-nanofarad capacitors and one 750-picofarad capacitor connected in parallel?",["576.9 nanofarads","1,733 picofarads","3,583 picofarads","10.750 nanofarads"],3,"Parallel capacitors add: 5.0 + 5.0 + 0.75 nF = 10.75 nF."],["G5C09","What is the capacitance of three 100-microfarad capacitors connected in series?",["0.33 microfarads","3.0 microfarads","33.3 microfarads","300 microfarads"],2,"Equal capacitors in series: 100 µF ÷ 3 ≈ 33.3 µF."],["G5C10","What is the inductance of three 10-millihenry inductors connected in parallel?",["0.30 henries","3.3 henries","3.3 millihenries","30 millihenries"],2,"Equal inductors in parallel: 10 mH ÷ 3 ≈ 3.3 mH."],["G5C11","What is the inductance of a circuit with a 20-millihenry inductor connected in series with a 50-millihenry inductor?",["7 millihenries","14.3 millihenries","70 millihenries","1,000 millihenries"],2,"Inductors in series add: 20 + 50 = 70 mH."],["G5C12","What is the capacitance of a 20-microfarad capacitor connected in series with a 50-microfarad capacitor?",["0.07 microfarads","14.3 microfarads","70 microfarads","1,000 microfarads"],1,"Capacitors in series: (20 × 50) ÷ (20 + 50) = 1,000 ÷ 70 ≈ 14.3 µF."],["G5C02","What is the output voltage if an input signal is applied to the secondary winding of a 4:1 voltage step-down transformer instead of the primary winding?",["The input voltage is multiplied by 4","The input voltage is divided by 4","Additional resistance must be added in series with the primary to prevent overload","Additional resistance must be added in parallel with the secondary to prevent overload"],0,"Fed backwards, a 4:1 step-down steps up by 4: the input voltage is multiplied by 4."],["G5C06","What is the voltage output of a transformer with a 500-turn primary and a 1500-turn secondary when 120 VAC is applied to the primary?",["360 volts","120 volts","40 volts","25.5 volts"],0,"Turns ratio 1,500 ÷ 500 = 3, so 120 V × 3 = 360 V."],["G5C07","What transformer turns ratio matches an antenna’s 600-ohm feed point impedance to a 50-ohm coaxial cable?",["3.5 to 1","12 to 1","24 to 1","144 to 1"],0,"Impedance ratio 600 ÷ 50 = 12; turns ratio = √12 ≈ 3.5:1."],["G4D05","How does a signal that reads 20 dB over S9 compare to one that reads S9 on a receiver, assuming a properly calibrated S meter?",["It is 10 times less powerful","It is 20 times less powerful","It is 20 times more powerful","It is 100 times more powerful"],3,"20 dB is 10 dB twice: ×10 × 10 = 100 times the power."],["G4D07","How much must the power output of a transmitter be raised to change the S meter reading on a distant receiver from S8 to S9?",["Approximately 1.5 times","Approximately 2 times","Approximately 4 times","Approximately 8 times"],2,"One S unit is 6 dB, which is 4 times the power."],["G4D08","What frequency range is occupied by a 3 kHz LSB signal when the displayed carrier frequency is set to 7.178 MHz?",["7.178 MHz to 7.181 MHz","7.178 MHz to 7.184 MHz","7.175 MHz to 7.178 MHz","7.1765 MHz to 7.1795 MHz"],2,"LSB extends below the display: 7.178 − 0.003 = 7.175 MHz, so 7.175 to 7.178 MHz."],["G4D09","What frequency range is occupied by a 3 kHz USB signal with the displayed carrier frequency set to 14.347 MHz?",["14.347 MHz to 14.647 MHz","14.347 MHz to 14.350 MHz","14.344 MHz to 14.347 MHz","14.3455 MHz to 14.3485 MHz"],1,"USB extends above the display: 14.347 + 0.003 = 14.350 MHz, so 14.347 to 14.350 MHz."],["G7B05","How many states does a 3-bit binary counter have?",["3","6","8","16"],2,"A 3-bit counter has 2³ = 8 states."],["G8B06","What is the total bandwidth of an FM phone transmission having 5 kHz deviation and 3 kHz modulating frequency?",["3 kHz","5 kHz","8 kHz","16 kHz"],3,"2 × (5 + 3) = 16 kHz."],["G8B07","What is the frequency deviation for a 12.21 MHz reactance modulated oscillator in a 5 kHz deviation, 146.52 MHz FM phone transmitter?",["101.75 Hz","416.7 Hz","5 kHz","60 kHz"],1,"146.52 ÷ 12.21 = 12; 5 kHz ÷ 12 ≈ 416.7 Hz."],["G8B13","Which of the following is an odd-order intermodulation product of frequencies F1 and F2?",["5F1-3F2","3F1-F2","2F1-F2","All these choices are correct"],2,"2F1 − F2: order 2 + 1 = 3, odd. The others are 4th and 8th order."],["G9A09","What standing wave ratio results from connecting a 50-ohm feed line to a 200-ohm resistive load?",["4:1","1:4","2:1","1:2"],0,"SWR = 200 ÷ 50 = 4:1."],["G9A10","What standing wave ratio results from connecting a 50-ohm feed line to a 10-ohm resistive load?",["2:1","1:2","1:5","5:1"],3,"SWR = 50 ÷ 10 = 5:1."],["G9B10","What is the approximate length for a 1/2 wave dipole antenna cut for 14.250 MHz?",["8 feet","16 feet","24 feet","33 feet"],3,"468 ÷ 14.25 ≈ 32.8 ft, about 33 feet."],["G9B11","What is the approximate length for a 1/2 wave dipole antenna cut for 3.550 MHz?",["42 feet","84 feet","132 feet","263 feet"],2,"468 ÷ 3.55 ≈ 131.8 ft, about 132 feet."],["G9B12","What is the approximate length for a 1/4 wave monopole antenna cut for 28.5 MHz?",["8 feet","11 feet","16 feet","21 feet"],0,"234 ÷ 28.5 ≈ 8.2 ft, about 8 feet."]],"P":{"decibels":["Decibels","<p>Decibels compare powers. About <b>3 dB</b> is twice the power, <b>6 dB</b> four times, <b>10 dB</b> ten times, <b>20 dB</b> a hundred times. Going down, the same numbers are losses: <b>1 dB</b> down leaves about 79% (a 20.6% loss). One S unit is 6 dB.</p>"],"power":["Power","<p><b>P = E &times; I</b>, <b>P = E&sup2; / R</b>, <b>P = I&sup2; &times; R</b>, and from those <b>E = &radic;(P &times; R)</b>. Convert milliamps to amps first (divide by 1,000).</p>"],"rms":["RMS, peak and PEP","<p>For a sine wave: <b>peak = RMS &times; 1.414</b>, <b>RMS = peak &times; 0.707</b>, <b>peak-to-peak = 2 &times; peak</b>. For PEP from peak-to-peak volts: halve, multiply by 0.707, then P = E&sup2;/R. An unmodulated carrier's PEP equals its average power.</p>"],"combine":["Series and parallel","<p><b>Resistors and inductors</b> (inductors assumed not coupled): add in series; in parallel use 1/(1/a + 1/b + ...), or (a &times; b)/(a + b) for two, or divide by the count for equal values. <b>Capacitors</b> are the reverse: add in parallel, use the reciprocal formula in series. Put all values in the same unit first.</p>"],"transformer":["Transformers","<p>Voltage follows the <b>turns ratio</b>. Impedance follows the turns ratio <b>squared</b>, so the turns ratio is the <b>square root</b> of the impedance ratio. Fed backwards, a step-down becomes a step-up.</p>"],"band":["Signals and band edges","<p>An SSB signal spans about 3 kHz: <b>below</b> the displayed frequency on LSB, <b>above</b> it on USB. FM bandwidth &asymp; <b>2 &times; (deviation + audio frequency)</b>, and a frequency multiplier multiplies deviation too. A binary counter with n bits has 2<sup>n</sup> states. An intermod product's order is the <b>sum of its multipliers, ignoring signs</b> (2F1 &minus; F2 is 2 + 1 = 3rd order).</p>"],"antenna":["SWR and antenna lengths","<p>For a resistive load, <b>SWR = larger impedance &divide; smaller</b>. Half-wave dipole: <b>468 &divide; MHz</b> feet. Quarter-wave: <b>234 &divide; MHz</b> feet.</p>"]},"CON":{"G5B01":"decibels","G5B10":"decibels","G5B03":"power","G5B04":"power","G5B05":"power","G5B06":"rms","G5B08":"rms","G5B09":"rms","G5B12":"power","G5B13":"rms","G5B14":"rms","G5C03":"combine","G5C04":"combine","G5C08":"combine","G5C09":"combine","G5C10":"combine","G5C11":"combine","G5C12":"combine","G5C02":"transformer","G5C06":"transformer","G5C07":"transformer","G4D05":"decibels","G4D07":"decibels","G4D08":"band","G4D09":"band","G7B05":"band","G8B06":"band","G8B07":"band","G8B13":"band","G9A09":"antenna","G9A10":"antenna","G9B10":"antenna","G9B11":"antenna","G9B12":"antenna"},"F":{"G5B01":["3 dB = &times;2 power",["<b>3 dB</b> = a doubling (or halving) of power","<b>10 &times; log<sub>10</sub>(2)</b> &asymp; 3.01 dB"]],"G5B10":["remaining = 10<sup>&minus;dB/10</sup>",["<b>dB</b> = the loss in decibels, here 1","<b>remaining</b> = fraction of power left, 0.794","<b>loss</b> = 1 &minus; 0.794 = 0.206, or 20.6%"]],"G5B03":["P = E&sup2; &divide; R",["<b>P</b> = power in watts","<b>E</b> = 400 volts","<b>R</b> = 800 ohms"]],"G5B04":["P = E &times; I",["<b>P</b> = power in watts","<b>E</b> = 12 volts","<b>I</b> = 0.2 amperes"]],"G5B05":["P = I&sup2; &times; R",["<b>P</b> = power in watts","<b>I</b> = 0.007 amperes (7.0 mA &divide; 1,000)","<b>R</b> = 1,250 ohms"]],"G5B06":["PEP = (E<sub>p-p</sub> &divide; 2 &times; 0.707)&sup2; &divide; R",["<b>E<sub>p-p</sub></b> = 200 volts peak-to-peak","<b>&divide; 2</b> = 100 V peak; <b>&times; 0.707</b> = 70.7 V RMS","<b>R</b> = 50 ohms"]],"G5B08":["E<sub>p-p</sub> = E<sub>RMS</sub> &times; 1.414 &times; 2",["<b>E<sub>RMS</sub></b> = 120 volts","<b>1.414</b> converts RMS to peak","<b>2</b> converts peak to peak-to-peak"]],"G5B09":["E<sub>RMS</sub> = E<sub>peak</sub> &times; 0.707",["<b>E<sub>peak</sub></b> = 17 volts","<b>0.707</b> converts peak to RMS"]],"G5B12":["E = &radic;(P &times; R)",["<b>E</b> = RMS volts","<b>P</b> = 1,200 watts","<b>R</b> = 50 ohms"]],"G5B13":["PEP = P<sub>avg</sub> (unmodulated)",["<b>PEP</b> = peak envelope power","<b>P<sub>avg</sub></b> = 1,060 watts; with no modulation the envelope is flat, so the ratio is 1.00"]],"G5B14":["PEP = (E<sub>p-p</sub> &divide; 2 &times; 0.707)&sup2; &divide; R",["<b>E<sub>p-p</sub></b> = 500 volts peak-to-peak","<b>&divide; 2</b> = 250 V peak; <b>&times; 0.707</b> = 176.8 V RMS","<b>R</b> = 50 ohms"]],"G5C03":["1/R = 1/R<sub>1</sub> + 1/R<sub>2</sub> + 1/R<sub>3</sub>",["<b>R<sub>1</sub>, R<sub>2</sub>, R<sub>3</sub></b> = 10, 20 and 50 ohms","<b>R</b> = 1 &divide; 0.17 &asymp; 5.9 ohms"]],"G5C04":["R = (R<sub>1</sub> &times; R<sub>2</sub>) &divide; (R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub>)",["<b>R<sub>1</sub></b> = 100 ohms","<b>R<sub>2</sub></b> = 200 ohms"]],"G5C08":["C = C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>3</sub>",["<b>C<sub>1</sub>, C<sub>2</sub></b> = 5.0 nF each","<b>C<sub>3</sub></b> = 750 pF = 0.75 nF (1,000 pF = 1 nF)"]],"G5C09":["C = C<sub>each</sub> &divide; n (series, equal values)",["<b>C<sub>each</sub></b> = 100 &micro;F","<b>n</b> = 3 capacitors"]],"G5C10":["L = L<sub>each</sub> &divide; n (parallel, equal values)",["<b>L<sub>each</sub></b> = 10 mH","<b>n</b> = 3 inductors"]],"G5C11":["L = L<sub>1</sub> + L<sub>2</sub>",["<b>L<sub>1</sub></b> = 20 mH","<b>L<sub>2</sub></b> = 50 mH"]],"G5C12":["C = (C<sub>1</sub> &times; C<sub>2</sub>) &divide; (C<sub>1</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>)",["<b>C<sub>1</sub></b> = 20 &micro;F","<b>C<sub>2</sub></b> = 50 &micro;F"]],"G5C02":["E<sub>out</sub> = E<sub>in</sub> &times; (N<sub>out</sub> &divide; N<sub>in</sub>)",["<b>N<sub>out</sub> &divide; N<sub>in</sub></b> = 4 &divide; 1 when fed into the winding with fewer turns","<b>E<sub>out</sub></b> = 4 &times; E<sub>in</sub>"]],"G5C06":["E<sub>s</sub> = E<sub>p</sub> &times; (N<sub>s</sub> &divide; N<sub>p</sub>)",["<b>E<sub>p</sub></b> = 120 VAC","<b>N<sub>p</sub></b> = 500 turns","<b>N<sub>s</sub></b> = 1,500 turns"]],"G5C07":["N<sub>1</sub> &divide; N<sub>2</sub> = &radic;(Z<sub>1</sub> &divide; Z<sub>2</sub>)",["<b>Z<sub>1</sub></b> = 600 ohms","<b>Z<sub>2</sub></b> = 50 ohms"]],"G4D05":["power ratio = 10<sup>dB/10</sup>",["<b>dB</b> = 20","<b>10<sup>2</sup></b> = 100"]],"G4D07":["power ratio = 10<sup>dB/10</sup>",["<b>dB</b> = 6, one S unit","<b>10<sup>0.6</sup></b> &asymp; 4"]],"G4D08":["LSB: (f &minus; 3 kHz) to f",["<b>f</b> = 7.178 MHz displayed","<b>3 kHz</b> = 0.003 MHz signal width"]],"G4D09":["USB: f to (f + 3 kHz)",["<b>f</b> = 14.347 MHz displayed","<b>3 kHz</b> = 0.003 MHz signal width"]],"G7B05":["states = 2<sup>n</sup>",["<b>n</b> = 3 bits","<b>2 &times; 2 &times; 2</b> = 8, counting 000 to 111"]],"G8B06":["BW &asymp; 2 &times; (D + M)",["<b>D</b> = 5 kHz deviation","<b>M</b> = 3 kHz highest modulating frequency"]],"G8B07":["D<sub>osc</sub> = D<sub>out</sub> &divide; (f<sub>out</sub> &divide; f<sub>osc</sub>)",["<b>f<sub>out</sub> &divide; f<sub>osc</sub></b> = 146.52 &divide; 12.21 = 12, the multiplication","<b>D<sub>out</sub></b> = 5 kHz = 5,000 Hz"]],"G8B13":["order = |a| + |b| for aF1 &plusmn; bF2",["<b>2F1 &minus; F2</b>: 2 + 1 = 3, odd","<b>3F1 &minus; F2</b>: 4; <b>5F1 &minus; 3F2</b>: 8, both even"]],"G9A09":["SWR = Z<sub>larger</sub> &divide; Z<sub>smaller</sub>",["<b>Z<sub>larger</sub></b> = 200 ohms load","<b>Z<sub>smaller</sub></b> = 50 ohms line"]],"G9A10":["SWR = Z<sub>larger</sub> &divide; Z<sub>smaller</sub>",["<b>Z<sub>larger</sub></b> = 50 ohms line","<b>Z<sub>smaller</sub></b> = 10 ohms load"]],"G9B10":["length (feet) = 468 &divide; f (MHz)",["<b>f</b> = 14.250 MHz","<b>468</b> = half a free-space wavelength (492 &divide; MHz), shortened about 5% for a real wire"]],"G9B11":["length (feet) = 468 &divide; f (MHz)",["<b>f</b> = 3.550 MHz"]],"G9B12":["length (feet) = 234 &divide; f (MHz)",["<b>f</b> = 28.5 MHz","<b>234</b> = half of 468, for a quarter wave"]]},"cheat":"<p><b>Power:</b> P = E &times; I = E&sup2; &divide; R = I&sup2; &times; R. E = &radic;(P &times; R).</p>\n<p><b>Sine waves:</b> peak = RMS &times; 1.414; RMS = peak &times; 0.707; peak-to-peak = 2 &times; peak.</p>\n<p><b>Decibels:</b> 3 dB = &times;2, 6 dB = &times;4, 10 dB = &times;10, 20 dB = &times;100. 1 dB loss = 20.6%. One S unit = 6 dB.</p>\n<p><b>Series and parallel:</b> R and L add in series; C adds in parallel. Two in parallel (R, L) or series (C): (a &times; b) &divide; (a + b).</p>\n<p><b>Transformers:</b> voltage ratio = turns ratio; turns ratio = &radic;(impedance ratio).</p>\n<p><b>FM:</b> bandwidth &asymp; 2 &times; (deviation + audio). <b>SWR:</b> larger Z &divide; smaller Z. <b>Dipole:</b> 468 &divide; MHz feet; <b>quarter wave:</b> 234 &divide; MHz feet.</p>"}];
window.EXPLAIN={"G1A01":"On 80, 40, 20 and 15 meters, parts of the band are reserved for Amateur Extra (and grandfathered Advanced) licensees, so a General can't transmit everywhere there.\n\nThe other HF bands, 160, 60, 30, 17, 12 and 10 meters, give a General the same privileges as an Extra. Memory hook: 80-40-20-15, the four classic DX bands, are the ones with Extra-only slices.","G1A02":"The 30-meter band, 10.100 to 10.150 MHz, is only 50 kHz wide and amateurs share it as secondary users. To keep signals narrow, the rules allow only CW and data there.\n\nPhone works on 160, 17 and 12 meters for Generals. Of the HF bands, 30 meters is the one to remember as no voice, no images.","G1A03":"Image modes such as slow-scan television (SSTV) take voice-like bandwidth, and 30 meters allows only CW and data.\n\nThe other bands listed allow images in their phone segments. The same answer as the phone question: of the bands listed, 30 meters is the one with no phone and no images.","G1A05":"On 40 meters, General CW and data run 7.025 to 7.125 MHz and General phone runs 7.175 to 7.300 MHz. The gap between, 7.125 to 7.175 MHz, is for Extra and Advanced licensees only.\n\nThe other two answers are fine for a General: all of 10 meters is General territory, and 21.275 to 21.300 MHz is inside General phone on 15 meters (21.275 to 21.450).","G1A06":"Some bands are shared, and the FCC ranks the services. The primary service has first claim. A secondary service may use the band only as long as it doesn't cause harmful interference to the primary users, and it must put up with any interference they cause.\n\nThere are no logging, emergency-only or time-of-day rules attached to secondary status.","G1A07":"CW is allowed on any frequency your license covers, with only rare exceptions. Generals have full privileges on 10 meters, 28.000 to 29.700 MHz, so CW is allowed anywhere in the band.\n\n28.000 to 28.300 MHz is the RTTY and data segment, which limits data modes, not CW. The other ranges aren't CW limits at all.","G1A08":"The FCC set aside slices of four HF bands for Extra licensees: the bottom 25 kHz of the CW/data segments on 80, 40, 20 and 15 meters, plus the lowest part of the phone segments on 80, 20 and 15 meters (on 40 meters the lower phone segment is shared with Advanced licensees).\n\nOn 160, 60, 30, 17, 12 and 10 meters, Generals and Extras have the same privileges. The same four bands as the question about where Generals can't transmit.","G1A09":"Convert kHz to MHz by moving the decimal point: 21300 kHz is 21.300 MHz. That falls in the 15-meter band (21.000 to 21.450 MHz), inside General phone privileges.\n\nThe other answers are on other bands: 14250 kHz is 20 meters, 18155 kHz is 17 meters, and 24900 kHz is 12 meters.","G1A10":"The rules keep repeaters out of the busy lower part of 10 meters. A repeater may receive and retransmit on 10 meters only in 29.5 to 29.7 MHz.\n\nThat top slice is where 10-meter FM repeaters live, typically with outputs near 29.62 to 29.68 MHz.","G1A11":"On 80, 40, 20 and 15 meters the bottom of each phone segment is reserved for Extra (and Advanced) licensees, and Generals get the top. For example, 20-meter phone is 14.150 to 14.350 MHz for Extras, but 14.225 to 14.350 MHz for Generals.\n\nThe pattern holds on every band: higher privileges reach further down.","G1B01":"Antenna structures over 200 feet above ground can be a hazard to aircraft, so they must be reported to the FAA and registered with the FCC. Structures near a public-use airport may need this at lower heights too, depending on their height and distance.\n\nAway from airports, most amateur towers fall well under 200 feet and need neither.","G1B02":"The rule limits a beacon to one channel per band from one location, so a single site doesn't crowd a band with several beacons.\n\nThere's no national beacon coordinating body to answer to, and no requirement to publish the frequency, so the other choices don't apply.","G1B03":"The FCC defines a beacon station as one transmitting for the observation of propagation and reception. Hearing a distant beacon tells you a path is open, and how well.\n\nRepeater identification and bulletins are different station types and purposes.","G1B04":"Amateur stations may not retransmit programs or signals from non-amateur stations, with a few exceptions. One is propagation and weather forecast information meant for the public and coming from US government stations.\n\nUnidentified tests, automatic retransmission by any station, and encrypted messages are all prohibited.","G1B05":"Amateur radio is mostly two-way, but the rules allow some one-way transmissions, including brief adjustments, brief calls to start a contact, telecommand, telemetry, emergency communications, information bulletins, and Morse code practice.\n\nW1AW's code practice sessions are the classic example. Unidentified tests and regular for-sale ads are not allowed.","G1B06":"The FCC's PRB-1 ruling says state and local governments may regulate antennas for things like safety and zoning, but they can't preclude amateur communications. Their rules must reasonably accommodate them and use the minimum practicable regulation.\n\nIt's not a blanket FCC override, and there's no fixed height that triggers local control. Private deed restrictions (HOAs) are a separate matter.","G1B07":"The rules forbid messages encoded to hide their meaning. Abbreviations, Q signals and prosigns like CQ, 73 and QTH are standard and widely understood, so they're allowed.\n\nThe test is intent: shorthand to save time is fine, shorthand to hide what you're saying is not.","G1B08":"International contacts are allowed by default. A country can block them only by notifying the International Telecommunication Union (ITU) that it objects to such communications.\n\nThird-party agreements matter only for passing messages on behalf of non-hams, and UN or IARU membership doesn't matter.","G1B09":"The 10-meter beacon segment, 28.200 to 28.300 MHz, is the only HF range where a beacon may run under automatic control. Others are allowed on some VHF and UHF segments.\n\nNeither low power nor Morse code earns an exception.","G1B10":"The rules cap beacon transmitter power at 100 W. That's plenty for others to hear the beacon when a band opens, without turning it into a nuisance.\n\nThe question writes it as PEP output, the way the rules measure transmitter power.","G1B11":"The rules can't cover every situation, so they require operating by good engineering and good amateur practice. The FCC, which enforces the rules, is the one that determines what that means.\n\nIt isn't left to each control operator, and the IEEE and ITU have no say over US amateur operation.","G1C01":"30 meters runs 10.100 to 10.150 MHz, and every station there is limited to 200 W PEP output, because amateurs share the band as secondary users.\n\nIt's the main HF exception to the usual 1500 W limit. Spot 10.1 MHz in a question and think 200 W.","G1C02":"The general limit for amateur stations is 1500 W PEP output, and 12 meters (24.890 to 24.990 MHz) has no special restriction.\n\nThe 200 W and effective-radiated-power answers belong to other rules: 200 W on 30 meters, and the ERP limits on 60 meters.","G1C03":"60 meters is shared with government users, so amateurs must keep signals narrow. The rule allows no more than 2.8 kHz of occupied bandwidth, enough for a normal SSB voice signal.\n\nThe pool asks about USB because upper sideband is the voice mode used there.","G1C04":"Power on 60 meters is limited in effective radiated power, which depends on antenna gain. A half-wave dipole is assumed to have a gain of 1, so with any other antenna you must keep the manufacturer's gain data or your own calculation in your station records.\n\nNo log of contacts, traffic or equipment makers is required.","G1C05":"Technicians are limited to 200 W on their part of 10 meters, but that limit doesn't apply to Generals. A General control operator has the standard 1500 W PEP output limit.\n\nThe question checks that you don't carry the Technician limit over.","G1C06":"160 meters has no special power restriction, so the general 1500 W PEP output limit applies.\n\nThe other answers are distractors; 1200 W doesn't appear anywhere in the rules.","G1C07":"Messages encoded to hide their meaning are prohibited, so a digital code must be one anyone can decode. The rule is that its technical characteristics must be publicly documented.\n\nNo type certification, experimental license or rulemaking is needed. That openness is how new modes like FT8 came into use quickly.","G1C11":"FCC power limits are stated as peak envelope power, the power at the highest peak of the signal, measured at the transmitter's output.\n\nRMS values and power at the antenna, after feed-line loss, are not what the rules use.","G1D01":"If your General, Advanced or Amateur Extra license expired and wasn't revoked, VEs give you credit for Element 3 (and Element 4 if it was Extra). You still have to pass the current Element 2 (Technician) exam.\n\nThere's no 5-to-15-year window. An expired Novice license earns no credit, and neither does a Technician license granted on or after March 21, 1987; one granted before that date earns Element 3 credit.","G1D02":"Each exam needs VEs who hold a higher class than the one being tested; the exception is the Extra exam, which Extra VEs give. General VEs can give the Technician exam. The General exam needs Advanced or Extra VEs, and the Extra exam needs Extras.\n\nGenerals can be accredited VEs; they're just limited to Technician sessions.","G1D03":"Your Certificate of Successful Completion of Examination lets you use the new privileges as soon as you pass, before the FCC database updates. That means every General segment, including 30 and 60 meters, plus your Technician privileges.\n\nWhile you wait, you add \"AG\" after your call sign when using General privileges.","G1D04":"Every amateur exam session needs a team of at least three VEs. For a Technician exam, each must hold General, Advanced or Extra.\n\nTwo isn't enough, and a Technician can't serve as a VE.","G1D05":"The rules follow the transmitter. A station in the US is under FCC rules, so its control operator needs a US operator license, wherever that operator is sitting.\n\nNo special remote permit exists, and a foreign license alone doesn't authorize it, apart from reciprocal operating arrangements for non-US citizens.","G1D06":"Between passing the exam and the upgrade showing in the FCC database, you operate on your CSCE. When you use frequencies or privileges that need General, you add \"AG\" after your call sign, as in \"KD9ABC/AG\" (said \"slash AG\" on voice).\n\nOn Technician privileges no suffix is needed, and filing the application doesn't remove the requirement.","G1D07":"VECs such as the ARRL VEC and W5YI-VEC operate under agreements with the FCC, and each VEC accredits its own Volunteer Examiners.\n\nThe Universal Licensing System is a database, and the FCC and its Wireless Bureau don't accredit individual VEs.","G1D08":"Citizenship isn't a VE requirement. What matters is holding an FCC-issued license of the right class, at least General, along with accreditation by a VEC and being 18 or older.\n\nThere's no residency period or ITU region requirement.","G1D09":"Your CSCE proves which exam elements you passed. It counts as credit for 365 days, so you can pass one element now and another within the year.\n\nIt's not tied to how long your current license lasts.","G1D10":"The rules require each VE to be at least 18 years old, along with holding the right license class and being accredited by a VEC.\n\nThere's no minimum age to take an exam; the age limit is only for examiners.","G1D11":"A license that expired more than two years ago can't simply be renewed. To get General back, you show proof of the old license grant to the VEs, which gives credit for Element 3, and pass the current Element 2 (Technician) exam.\n\nThe FCC doesn't reinstate it on request, and a copy of the old license alone isn't enough.","G1D12":"Rules follow the transmitter's location. A station in another country transmits under that country's regulations, so those are the rules you follow, even sitting in the US.\n\nThe FCC's rules govern stations in places it regulates.","G1E01":"Almost anyone may speak or send a message through an amateur station as a third party: non-citizens, people speaking other languages, anyone. The main exception is someone whose amateur license was revoked (or suspended) and not reinstated.\n\nThat keeps someone the FCC removed from the air from operating through others.","G1E02":"A Technician has no transmitting privileges in the 10-meter repeater segment above 29.5 MHz. The repeater may relay a Technician's 2-meter signal there only when its own control operator holds General or higher, since that operator answers for the transmission.\n\nNo special authorization or emergency is needed.","G1E03":"Unattended digital stations may run freely only in certain segments. Elsewhere, an automatically controlled station may transmit only in response to a station under local or remote control, so a person is present at one end, and its transmissions must be no wider than 500 Hz.\n\nIt doesn't take an Extra license, and third-party traffic isn't the issue.","G1E04":"The rules call out several cases needing special care. Stations within one mile of an FCC monitoring facility must protect it from harmful interference. On bands where amateurs are secondary, they must not interfere with primary users. Spread spectrum is spread across a wide range, so it must not cause harmful interference.\n\nAll three choices apply.","G1E05":"Third-party agreements let a ham pass messages on behalf of other people (third parties) to another country, but the content is limited. The messages must relate to amateur radio, be remarks of a personal character, or concern emergencies or disaster relief.\n\nThere's no one-minute limit or third-party logging requirement.","G1E06":"The ITU divides the world into three regions. Region 1 is Europe, Africa and the Middle East; Region 2 is the Americas; Region 3 is Asia and the Pacific. US hams follow Region 2 allocations.\n\nThere is no Region 4.","G1E07":"Amateurs may communicate with other amateur stations, plus a few authorized exceptions in emergencies or with government stations. Wi-Fi devices are unlicensed consumer devices, so contact with them is never allowed.\n\nHams may use Wi-Fi hardware on 2.4 GHz, but only to talk with other amateur stations under amateur rules.","G1E08":"Spread spectrum spreads a signal across a wide range of frequencies, so it can interfere with many users at once. The rules cap it at 10 W PEP output.\n\nThe other limits don't apply: 1500 W is the general limit, and the low milliwatt figure isn't in the rules.","G1E10":"The NCDXF/IARU International Beacon Project runs 18 beacons around the world, taking turns on 14.100, 18.110, 21.150, 24.930 and 28.200 MHz. Hams listen to them to see which paths are open.\n\nTransmitting there would cover up the beacons, so good practice is to avoid those frequencies.","G1E11":"Automatically controlled stations sending RTTY or data may talk with each other anywhere on 6 meters and shorter, and in small listed segments of some HF bands, like 14.095 to 14.0995 MHz.\n\nOutside those places, an automatic station may only answer a station under local or remote control.","G1E12":"Remote control doesn't change the third-party rules. If a third-party message would be allowed with you at the station, it's allowed when you're operating it remotely.\n\nThe usual third-party limits still apply, such as the restrictions for countries with or without agreements.","G2A01":"By long-standing convention, amateurs use upper sideband for SSB voice on 20 meters (14 MHz) and every band above it.\n\nIt's a custom, not a rule, but everyone follows it so receivers are set the same way. Memory hook: high frequencies, upper sideband.","G2A02":"Below 10 MHz, amateurs customarily use lower sideband for SSB voice: 160, 75/80 and 40 meters.\n\nThe one exception below 10 MHz is 60 meters, where USB is used. Memory hook: low frequencies, lower sideband.","G2A03":"When amateurs use SSB on 6 meters, 2 meters and up, they use upper sideband, following the same above-10-MHz custom.\n\nMost VHF and UHF voice is FM, but weak-signal SSB work there is always USB.","G2A04":"17 meters (18 MHz) and 12 meters (24 MHz) follow the usual pattern: upper sideband above 10 MHz.\n\nThey're newer bands without their own traditions, so nothing changes there.","G2A05":"SSB dominates HF voice because it's narrow and efficient, letting more stations fit and putting the transmitter's power into speech.\n\nFM is used mostly on 10 meters and above, and AM is a niche.","G2A06":"AM sends a carrier and two identical sidebands. SSB drops the carrier and one sideband, so it occupies about half the bandwidth and all its power goes into the one sideband that carries speech.\n\nIt isn't high fidelity, and it's no better against static or impulse noise.","G2A07":"A single sideband signal is an AM signal with the carrier and one sideband removed, leaving only the upper or lower sideband.\n\nSSB isn't the only voice mode allowed on 20, 15 and 10 meters or on 160, 75 and 40 meters; AM is legal there too, just less common.","G2A08":"Wait for a pause between transmissions and say your call sign once. The stations will hear you and can invite you in.\n\nQRZ asks who's calling, \"breaker\" is CB slang, and CQ is a general call.","G2A09":"Nothing makes lower sideband better or required on the lower bands. It's a custom dating back to early SSB equipment design, and everyone follows it so they can hear each other.\n\nEither sideband is legal; neither works with an AM detector.","G2A10":"VOX (voice-operated transmit) keys the transmitter when the microphone picks up your voice, so you don't need to hold a push-to-talk switch.\n\nIt doesn't change the signal's sound, bandwidth or power.","G2A11":"DX means distant stations. From the contiguous 48 states, that means stations outside them, including Alaska, Hawaii and other countries.\n\nIf you're inside the lower 48, let the DX answer.","G2A12":"ALC (automatic level control) keeps the transmitter from being overdriven. Too much microphone or audio gain drives ALC hard and distorts the signal, so you adjust the gain until the ALC reading is in its proper range.\n\nThe attenuator affects receiving, and antenna tuning is unrelated.","G2B01":"The rules say no frequency is assigned to any station's exclusive use, and operators must cooperate in sharing. The only priority is for emergency communications.\n\nNets, contests and contacts in progress have no legal claim; courtesy settles it.","G2B02":"A distress call comes first. Stop your contact, answer the station, and learn what's wrong and where, so you can get help to them.\n\nGoing silent or lowering power leaves the station unanswered.","G2B03":"Band conditions shift, and stations that couldn't hear each other suddenly overlap. No one has priority, so good practice is to work it out courteously, often by one side moving.\n\nClaiming priority or just continuing won't fix it.","G2B04":"A CW signal is narrow, so a few hundred hertz of separation keeps you out of the next station's passband.\n\n5 to 50 Hz is far too close, and kilohertz of spacing wastes the band.","G2B05":"An SSB voice signal is about 2.4 to 3 kHz wide, so stations need that much separation to avoid overlapping.\n\nThe CW spacing is too close for voice, and 6 kHz is more than needed.","G2B06":"A frequency can sound clear while one side of a contact is talking. Asking first gives anyone using it a chance to say so.\n\nIdentify when you ask. QSY means change frequency, and V's or \"test\" don't ask anything.","G2B07":"Band plans set aside parts of each band for particular modes and activities, so signals that would interfere are kept apart. Following them is accepted practice.\n\nListening briefly is wise, but two minutes isn't a standard, and there's no rule to give your call three times.","G2B08":"The 6-meter band plan reserves this \"DX window\" so weak signals from distant stations aren't buried by domestic contacts. Stations in the lower 48 use it only to work DX.\n\nIt's a voluntary band plan, not an FCC rule, and isn't for digital or SSTV.","G2B09":"RACES is amateur radio in service of civil defense, and its control operators must hold an FCC-issued amateur license and be enrolled with a civil defense organization.\n\nGovernment officials without amateur licenses can't serve as control operators.","G2B10":"A good net plans for trouble: if the main frequency gets interference or the band fades, everyone knows where to move.\n\nMultiple phonetics and reading the whole roster each session waste time.","G2B11":"The rules allow RACES training drills and tests up to a total of one hour a week without special authorization. With approval from the state's chief emergency planning officer, drills may run up to 72 hours, no more than twice a year.\n\nRemember: one hour, every week.","G2C01":"QSK switches between transmit and receive so fast that you hear the band in the gaps between your dots and dashes. Another station can break in at any moment.\n\nBK is a prosign, and keyers or manual switches aren't what full break-in means.","G2C02":"QRS means send more slowly. When the other station sends it, slow down to a speed they can copy.\n\nQRS: S for slower.","G2C03":"KN invites a specific station or stations to reply and asks others not to break in. Plain K invites anyone.\n\nThe prosign for closing your station is CL, and full break-in is QSK.","G2C04":"Send QRL? before calling CQ on a frequency that seems clear. If someone answers with QRL or C, it's in use, so move on.\n\nIt's the CW version of asking \"Is this frequency in use?\"","G2C05":"The calling station chose a speed it's comfortable with, so don't answer faster than that. If you can't copy that fast, answer slower; most operators will slow down to match.\n\nThere's no standard calling speed.","G2C06":"When two signals are on the same frequency, their beat note drops to zero. On CW you tune so your transmit frequency matches the station you're answering, so both stations stay on one frequency.\n\nIt has nothing to do with sending speed.","G2C07":"An RST report is readability, strength and tone. A C after it, as in 579C, warns that the signal's frequency shifts as it keys, a chirp.\n\nKey clicks are reported separately, and the S meter has nothing to do with it.","G2C08":"AR, sent as one character, means end of message. It closes a formal message such as a radiogram.\n\nSK ends a contact, BK means back to you, and KN invites only the station you're working.","G2C09":"QSL acknowledges receipt. A QSL card is named after it, since it confirms a contact, but the Q signal itself means received and understood.\n\nIt doesn't mean you worked before or that the contact is confirmed.","G2C10":"QRN refers to natural noise like static from lightning. Its cousin QRM is man-made interference from other stations.\n\nMemory hook: QRN, N for nature's noise.","G2C11":"QRV says you're ready. Hams also use it to mean \"I'm on the air,\" as in \"I'll be QRV on 20 meters tonight.\"\n\nSending too fast is QRS territory, and interference is QRM.","G2D01":"Run by the ARRL under an agreement with the FCC, the program trains volunteers to listen for rule violations and help operators fix problems, referring serious cases to the FCC.\n\nExams are given by VEs, and repeater coordination is another group's job.","G2D02":"Volunteer Monitors often send friendly advisories before anything reaches the FCC, encouraging hams to fix problems themselves.\n\nExams, emergency service and repeater coordination are other programs.","G2D03":"A stuck transmitter holding a repeater on can be found by direction finding. Each monitor points a beam at the strongest signal on the repeater's input, and where the headings cross is the transmitter.\n\nThat's triangulation; signal strength comparisons alone can't locate it.","G2D04":"Centered on your station, an azimuthal map shows the direction to point your beam for any place on Earth, and how far it is.\n\nIt distorts land shapes, and satellite orbits have nothing to do with it.","G2D05":"A CQ is a general call for any station. Keep it short, identify, then listen, and repeat as needed.\n\nAn unmodulated carrier is not a call, and QTC refers to messages.","G2D06":"Signals can reach a station the short way around the Earth or the long way. The long path runs the opposite direction, so you turn the antenna 180 degrees.\n\nThe gray line is the twilight band around the Earth at sunrise and sunset, which can favor certain paths. A long-path heading is simply the reverse of the short-path heading.","G2D07":"The NATO (ITU) phonetic alphabet is the international standard: Alpha, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, and so on.\n\nAble, Baker, Charlie, Dog is an old military alphabet, and country and city names aren't standard.","G2D08":"The FCC doesn't require a general station log, but a record of when and how you operated can help if the FCC requests information about your station.\n\nNo log is required for international contacts or third-party traffic, and license renewal needs no proof of operation.","G2D09":"Contests change the pace, not the rules. You must give your call sign at least every 10 minutes and at the end of each contact, as always.\n\nSubmitting a log and sending QSL cards are optional.","G2D10":"QRP is the Q signal for reducing power, and hams use it for operating with low power, usually 5 watts or less on CW.\n\nIt's a popular challenge: making contacts with a few watts.","G2D11":"Knowing how well you're being heard lets each station decide how fast to send, whether to repeat things, or whether to change power or mode.\n\nAward programs and frequency calibration aren't the reason.","G2E01":"When RTTY tones are fed into an SSB transmitter's audio (AFSK), the convention is lower sideband, on every band, so the mark and space tones come out the right way round.\n\nThis is the exception to USB above 10 MHz; FT8 and its relatives use USB.","G2E02":"VARA is a fast, reliable digital mode for HF and VHF that Winlink uses to move email.\n\nIt isn't for moonbounce, direction finding or DX spotting.","G2E03":"These protocols check every block of data and resend what arrives damaged. Interference forces repeats, which shows up as retries, pauses and timeouts, and can stop a connection from forming.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G2E04":"FT8 stations take turns in 15-second slots. The CQ station transmits in one slot and listens in the other, so you answer in the other slot. Pick a clear audio frequency so you aren't on top of others.\n\nTransmitting in the same slot means the caller is sending, not listening.","G2E05":"These modes are sent as audio tones through an SSB transmitter, always on upper sideband, on every band.\n\nRTTY by AFSK is the one that uses LSB.","G2E06":"RTTY switches between two tones, mark and space. On the amateur HF bands they're normally 170 Hz apart.\n\n850 Hz and 425 Hz are used elsewhere, and 85 Hz is too narrow for standard RTTY.","G2E07":"FT8 transmissions start on a fixed 15-second schedule. If your clock is off by more than a second or two, your signals land out of step and decoding fails.\n\nNo special modem or antenna is needed, just a sound card connection and a synced clock.","G2E08":"By band plan, the digital modes cluster just above the CW activity on 20 meters, from about 14.070 to 14.100 MHz, with FT8 near 14.074.\n\nSSTV is near 14.230 MHz, and 14.100 itself is kept clear for beacons.","G2E09":"PACTOR connects exactly two stations, with each acknowledging the other's data. A third station has no way in.\n\nTransmitting a carrier to break it up would be deliberate interference.","G2E10":"Gateways such as Winlink stations listen on published frequencies. You send a connect request to the gateway's call sign there, and it answers and links you in.\n\nEmail or QRL won't connect you.","G2E11":"AREDN (Amateur Radio Emergency Data Network) uses modified Wi-Fi-type hardware on amateur frequencies to build a data network that can carry video, email, chat and files when other networks fail.\n\nIt isn't for FM repeaters, propagation reporting or DX spotting.","G2E12":"Winlink lets hams send and receive email over the air, linking to the internet through gateway stations. It runs on HF and VHF using packet, PACTOR, VARA and other protocols.\n\nAll three choices describe it.","G2E13":"Winlink's RMS stations are gateways between radio users and the Winlink email system.\n\nA TNC is a radio modem used by stations, not another name for a gateway, and RJ-45 is a network connector.","G2E14":"An RTTY signal that sounds tuned in can still fail. If mark and space are swapped (often because the sideband is wrong) or the baud rate doesn't match, the decoder turns out nonsense.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G2E15":"FT8 uses a dial frequency of 14.074 MHz on 20 meters, with signals spread across the next 3 kHz of audio.\n\nIt doesn't spread across the voice or CW segments.","G3A01":"Sunspots go with more ultraviolet radiation, which ionizes the upper atmosphere more strongly. A more strongly ionized ionosphere can bend higher frequencies back to Earth, so bands like 15, 12 and 10 meters open up.\n\nZero sunspots doesn't stop propagation; the lower bands still work.","G3A02":"A solar flare's X-rays sharply increase ionization in the D region on the daylit side of Earth. The D region absorbs lower frequencies most, so signals there can vanish for minutes to hours, while higher frequencies suffer less.\n\nIt affects the daytime side, where the Sun's radiation lands.","G3A03":"Light, ultraviolet and X-rays all travel at the speed of light, and sunlight takes about 8 minutes to cross the 93 million miles to Earth. So a flare's effects arrive at the same moment it's seen.\n\nCharged particles take much longer: a CME takes 15 hours to several days. 28 days is about how long the Sun takes to rotate, not a travel time.","G3A04":"The higher HF bands need a strongly ionized ionosphere to bend their signals back. With few sunspots there isn't enough ionization, so 15, 12 and 10 meters are often closed for long-distance work.\n\nThe lower bands keep working because they need less ionization.","G3A05":"The Sun's radio output at 10.7 cm (2800 MHz) rises and falls with its activity, so it's a handy daily gauge. Higher numbers mean more ionization and better HF conditions.\n\nIt's not a sunspot count or a usable-frequency figure.","G3A06":"When a blast of charged particles from the Sun hits Earth's magnetic field, the field is shaken up for hours or days. That's a geomagnetic storm, and it disturbs the ionosphere.\n\nIt isn't a thunderstorm, and it isn't defined by a drop in the solar flux index.","G3A07":"20 meters needs less ionization than the higher bands, so even near solar minimum the daytime ionosphere usually carries it around the world. That makes it the most dependable long-distance band.\n\nIt doesn't depend on solar maximum, minimum or the solstice.","G3A08":"Storm particles are funneled toward the magnetic poles, where they disrupt the ionosphere most. HF paths crossing high latitudes, such as North America to Europe or Asia, suffer most.\n\nGround wave doesn't use the ionosphere, so storms don't affect it.","G3A09":"The same particles that disrupt HF light up the aurora, and the aurora's ionized curtains can reflect VHF signals. Hams point beams north and make contacts by aurora, with a distinctive raspy sound.\n\nPolar HF paths get worse, not better.","G3A10":"The Sun rotates about once every 27 days as seen from Earth. Active regions rotate away and come back, so conditions they cause tend to repeat about four weeks later.\n\nThe Moon and the radiation belts don't drive it.","G3A11":"A CME is a cloud of charged particles thrown off the Sun. It travels much slower than light, so it reaches Earth anywhere from about 15 hours to a few days later, and then can set off a geomagnetic storm.\n\nThe 8-minute figure is for a flare's light.","G3A12":"The K-index is updated every three hours and runs from 0 (calm) to 9 (severe storm). Low K means a quiet field and better HF.\n\nIt describes Earth's field, not the Sun's. Memory hook: K is quicK, A is a daily Average.","G3A13":"The A-index is a daily figure built from the day's K readings. Low A means the field was stable; high A means a disturbed day.\n\nSolar radio flux is measured by the solar flux index instead.","G3A14":"Coronal holes release fast streams of solar wind. When they reach Earth they can stir up geomagnetic activity, and that degrades HF propagation.\n\nVHF/UHF ducting depends on weather, not solar particles.","G3B01":"The long path is the long way around the Earth, so it takes a fraction of a second longer. Hearing both at once gives a slight echo.\n\nIt isn't a regular fading cycle or a fixed boost in strength.","G3B02":"The MUF depends on how strongly the ionosphere is ionized along the path, and that changes with where the path runs and how long it is, the hour and season, and what the Sun is doing.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G3B03":"Absorption in the lower ionosphere hits lower frequencies harder. So the highest frequency that still gets refracted back, just under the MUF, loses the least on the way.\n\nThat's why DXers use the highest open band.","G3B04":"Systems like the Reverse Beacon Network and PSK Reporter have receivers all over the world reporting what they hear online. Call CQ in CW or a digital mode such as FT8, and within minutes you can see where your signal is being heard.\n\nThe A-index is a general measure, not a test of your own station.","G3B05":"That's the usable window: high enough to avoid being absorbed, low enough to be bent back down by the ionosphere instead of passing through.\n\nThe ionosphere doesn't amplify signals.","G3B06":"Lower frequencies are absorbed more by the D and lower E regions. Below the LUF, too much of the signal is lost on the way to be useful.\n\nThey aren't passing through the ionosphere; that happens above the MUF.","G3B07":"Like the MUF, the LUF applies to a particular path. Below it, absorption makes communication between those two points impractical.\n\nIt isn't a 24-hour or one-hour figure.","G3B08":"The MUF is the highest frequency that the ionosphere will refract back to Earth over a particular path. Above it, signals pass through into space.\n\nMaximum, not minimum, and for a path, not a day.","G3B09":"The F2 region is the highest part of the ionosphere, roughly 150 to 300 miles up, so a signal bent back by it lands as far as about 2,500 miles away.\n\nLonger distances take more hops.","G3B10":"The E region is lower, about 60 miles up, so a hop off it reaches only about half as far as an F2 hop, roughly 1,200 miles.\n\nThat's typical of sporadic-E openings on 10 and 6 meters.","G3B11":"There's no frequency left that is both low enough to be refracted and high enough to escape absorption, so the usable window has closed.\n\nNothing is enhanced; that path is simply dead for skywave.","G3B12":"Summer thunderstorms generate lightning crashes that travel long distances on the lower frequencies, so 160, 80 and 40 meters get noisy.\n\nIt isn't caused by photons, and the lower bands don't give worldwide daytime coverage.","G3C01":"From the bottom up, the ionospheric regions are D, E, F1 and F2. The D region sits about 30 to 55 miles up and exists mostly in daylight.\n\nMemory hook: they go up the alphabet as they go up in height.","G3C02":"Above the critical frequency, a signal sent at that angle passes through the ionosphere into space. At or below it, the signal comes back.\n\nIt has nothing to do with signal-to-noise ratio.","G3C03":"The higher the point where a signal is bent back, the farther away it comes down. F2 is the highest region, so its hops are the longest, up to about 2,500 miles.\n\nDensity helps it refract higher frequencies, but height sets the distance.","G3C04":"Signals sent at steeper angles meet the ionosphere more head-on. Above the critical angle they go through instead of bending back.\n\nIt isn't a beam heading.","G3C05":"Sunlight creates the D region, which absorbs lower frequencies before they reach the higher regions that would bend them back. At night the D region fades and these bands open up for long distance.\n\nThe F region isn't the absorber.","G3C06":"Scattered energy reaches you by many slightly different paths that keep changing, so the signal flutters.\n\nScatter signals are weak and hard to understand, and they can occur at any time.","G3C07":"Each path has a slightly different length, so copies of the signal arrive at slightly different times and interfere with each other.\n\nThe ionosphere doesn't have to be unstable for that to happen.","G3C08":"Most of the signal goes where normal propagation sends it. Only a little is scattered in other directions, and that little is all that reaches the skip zone.\n\nGround wave and the magnetosphere aren't involved.","G3C09":"The skip zone is the gap between where ground wave ends and the first skywave hop lands. Scattered energy can reach into it, faintly.\n\nFaraday rotation and chordal hop are different propagation effects.","G3C10":"Signals sent nearly straight up on a low band, like 80 or 40 meters, come back down close by, covering a region a few hundred miles across with no skip zone. It's popular for regional emergency networks.\n\nIt uses the lower HF bands over short paths, and works only below the critical frequency; it isn't long-path or near-MUF DX propagation.","G3C11":"The D region sits low, where the air is still relatively thick. Its free electrons keep colliding with air molecules, which turns the signal's energy into heat, and its ionization is too weak to bend HF signals back. It forms in daylight and largely fades at night.\n\nThe E and F regions are where refraction happens.","G4A01":"A steady carrier, heard as a whistle or tone, can sit right inside an SSB signal. A notch filter cuts a very narrow slice of the passband at that tone, removing it while leaving the rest of the signal.\n\nIt doesn't help with impulse noise or splatter, which are spread out.","G4A02":"On CW, an interfering signal may fall on one side of the one you want. Switching sidebands moves where it lands relative to your filter, sometimes outside the passband.\n\nIt does nothing about impulse noise and doesn't fit more stations in.","G4A03":"Impulse noise, like ignition or power-line pops, comes in short bursts. A noise blanker detects each pulse and briefly turns the receiver's gain down, so the pop is silenced.\n\nIt doesn't clip peaks or widen the bandwidth.","G4A04":"At resonance, the output tank circuit presents its highest impedance to the tube, so plate current falls to a sharp minimum. You tune for that dip.\n\nOff resonance, plate current rises; there's no peak to look for.","G4A05":"Automatic level control feeds back from the amplifier to reduce the transceiver's output if it starts to overdrive, which would cause distortion and splatter.\n\nIt isn't about audio response, harmonics or efficiency.","G4A06":"A tuner matches the transmitter's 50-ohm output to whatever impedance the feed line presents, so the transmitter can deliver full power.\n\nIt doesn't change the SWR on the feed line between the tuner and the antenna, or the loss there.","G4A07":"DSP noise reduction removes what it judges to be noise. Pushed too hard, it starts removing parts of the signal too, and voices sound watery or garbled.\n\nIt doesn't change the received frequency.","G4A08":"After tuning for the dip, the LOAD (coupling) control adjusts how much power is transferred to the antenna system. Increase it until you get the output you want, keeping the plate current within its rated maximum.\n\nSWR is set by the antenna, not the LOAD control.","G4A09":"When the transceiver keys the amplifier, the amplifier's relay needs a few milliseconds to connect the antenna to its output. Sending RF before the relay closes could damage the contacts or the equipment.\n\nKey clicks and power supplies aren't the reason.","G4A10":"With a paddle and a keyer, holding one side sends a string of evenly timed dots and the other dashes. The keyer handles the timing.\n\nIt doesn't switch the transmitter or interface with a computer.","G4A11":"Digital signals need clean, steady audio tones. When ALC acts, it varies the gain and distorts them, causing splatter and poor decoding. Set the audio level just below where ALC begins to act.\n\nOnly the distortion answer is right.","G4A12":"Split operation is common with DX stations, who listen on a different frequency from the one they transmit on. Two VFOs make it easy: one for receive, one for transmit.\n\nIt doesn't let you transmit on two frequencies or work full duplex.","G4A13":"A very strong signal can overload a receiver's front end, causing distortion and false signals. An attenuator reduces everything coming in, often by 10 or 20 dB, to keep the receiver working cleanly.\n\nIt doesn't affect transmit power.","G4B01":"An oscilloscope draws a graph: the vertical channel shows a signal's voltage and the horizontal channel sweeps across in time. Each has its own amplifier.\n\nMeters and signal generators don't draw waveforms.","G4B02":"A voltmeter gives one number. An oscilloscope shows the shape of a signal over time, revealing distortion, spikes and timing.\n\nIt isn't more precise for a single voltage reading.","G4B03":"The rise and fall of each CW element determines whether the signal has key clicks. An oscilloscope shows those edges directly.\n\nA field strength meter or wavemeter shows a signal's presence (and roughly its frequency), not the shape of its keying.","G4B04":"To see the RF envelope, the scope must display the actual transmitted signal, reduced to a safe level.\n\nThe local oscillator or a mixer output wouldn't show the signal going to the antenna.","G4B05":"A meter draws a little current from whatever it measures. With high input impedance, that current is tiny, so the reading reflects the circuit as it really is.\n\nIt isn't about frequency response or safety.","G4B06":"A digital display shows several digits directly, with no needle to read against a scale, giving finer resolution.\n\nAnalog meters are often better for watching a changing value.","G4B07":"Two audio tones, such as 700 and 1900 Hz, are fed into an SSB transmitter at once. Because they aren't harmonically related, the intermodulation products from distortion fall at distinct frequencies that are easy to see.\n\nSquare waves and swept tones aren't used.","G4B08":"A linear transmitter reproduces its input faithfully. With two tones in, a linear output shows just two clean tones; a nonlinear one creates intermodulation products.\n\nIt doesn't measure carrier suppression or modulation percentage.","G4B09":"Watching a needle swing makes it easy to see when a reading peaks or dips. Digital digits change too fast to follow while you tune.\n\nFor precision, digital is better.","G4B10":"A directional wattmeter measures forward and reflected power separately. From those two readings you can calculate the SWR.\n\nIt doesn't measure front-to-back ratio or propagation.","G4B11":"An analyzer contains its own low-power signal source, so it needs only the antenna system to measure. Disconnect the transmitter and receiver.\n\nIt doesn't measure through them.","G4B12":"An analyzer's own signal is weak, so strong RF picked up by the antenna from a nearby transmitter, such as a broadcast station, can swamp it and give false readings.\n\nThe problem is simply received power adding to the analyzer's reading. Intermodulation and harmonic generation aren't the effect, so \"all these\" is wrong.","G4B13":"Analyzers measure impedance and SWR, so they can check a cable's characteristic impedance or find its length.\n\nThey can't measure front-to-back ratio, gain or transmitter power.","G4C01":"A small capacitor passes RF easily but blocks audio. Placed from an audio line to ground, it shunts stray RF away while leaving the audio alone.\n\nAn inductor would do the opposite.","G4C02":"An arc is a series of tiny sparks, and each spark radiates energy across a very wide band. Loose connections in power lines or appliances are a classic source.\n\nThe balun choices don't cause broadband noise.","G4C03":"The audio device rectifies the SSB signal's envelope, which follows the voice, so you hear a garbled version of the operator's speech.\n\nClean speech would mean an AM-like signal.","G4C04":"Each time the transmitter keys, the audio device picks up a burst of rectified RF, heard as a hum or click following the Morse code.\n\nNo CW tone is produced; the device has no beat oscillator.","G4C05":"If the ground lead is a significant fraction of a wavelength, it acts like an antenna rather than a ground, and RF voltage builds up on equipment connected to it.\n\nBraid versus wire and insulation don't cause it.","G4C06":"A ground wire about a quarter wavelength long transforms the ground's low impedance into a high one at the equipment end, so RF voltages appear on the chassis.\n\nIt doesn't cause corrosion or overheating.","G4C07":"A lightning strike carries enormous current that heats any joint. Solder melts at a low temperature, so the connection could fail exactly when it's needed. Use clamps or welded connections.\n\nOnly the heat answer is right.","G4C08":"Common-mode current flows on the outside of a cable's shield. A ferrite choke adds impedance to that current while leaving the signal inside unaffected.\n\nShorting or grounding the center conductor would kill the audio.","G4C09":"A ground loop forms when equipment is grounded through different paths of different lengths. Bonding all enclosures together keeps them at the same potential.\n\nChaining grounds in series leaves each unit at a different potential, and bonding neutral to ground is done only at the service panel, never by you.","G4C10":"Current flowing in a ground loop adds a small 60 Hz voltage to the audio, which goes out on the air as hum.\n\nSWR, current draw and harmonics aren't affected.","G4C11":"An RF hot spot is a piece of equipment with RF voltage on its case. Bonding everything together keeps all cases at the same RF potential.\n\nSurge suppressors and filters don't address it.","G4C12":"If a wire inside comes loose and touches the case, a ground connection carries the fault current away and trips the breaker, instead of leaving the case live for someone to touch.\n\nIt's a safety measure.","G4D01":"Speech has a few loud peaks and many quiet sounds. A processor raises the quieter parts, so the voice punches through noise better.\n\nIt doesn't improve bass response or prevent distortion.","G4D02":"Peak power is limited by the transmitter. A processor fills in the space below the peaks, so the average power goes up while the peak stays the same.\n\nThat higher average is what makes it sound louder.","G4D03":"Too much processing squashes the voice, creates extra frequencies that splatter, and raises background sounds along with speech.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G4D04":"S units run from S1 to S9, then in decibels over S9.\n\nIt doesn't measure transmitter power or impedance.","G4D05":"Every 10 dB is ten times the power, so 20 dB is 10 times 10, or 100 times.\n\nThe answers in \"times less\" go the wrong direction.","G4D06":"By convention, each S unit is about 6 dB, four times the power.\n\nMany meters aren't calibrated exactly, but 6 dB is the standard.","G4D07":"One S unit is 6 dB, and 6 dB is four times the power. So a 100-watt station would need about 400 watts to move a distant meter up one S unit.\n\nDoubling power gains only 3 dB, half an S unit.","G4D08":"On lower sideband, the signal extends below the displayed carrier frequency. 3 kHz below 7.178 MHz is 7.175 MHz.\n\nThat sits right at the bottom of the General 40-meter phone segment.","G4D09":"On upper sideband, the signal extends above the displayed carrier frequency. 3 kHz above 14.347 MHz is 14.350 MHz.\n\nThat's exactly the top of the 20-meter band.","G4D10":"An LSB signal spreads 3 kHz below the displayed frequency. To keep all of it inside the segment, the display must be at least 3 kHz above the bottom edge.\n\nBelow the edge, part of your signal would be out of bounds.","G4D11":"A USB signal spreads 3 kHz above the displayed frequency. To keep it all inside, the display must be at least 3 kHz below the top edge.\n\nMemory hook: leave 3 kHz of room on the side the signal extends toward: up for USB, down for LSB.","G4E01":"A capacitance hat, a set of spokes or a disk near the top of a mobile whip, adds capacitance that makes the antenna act longer. That lowers its resonant frequency and improves efficiency.\n\nIt doesn't increase power handling or lower the radiation angle.","G4E02":"The tip of a short mobile antenna carries very high RF voltage, which can discharge into the air as corona. A smooth ball's larger radius lowers the electric field at the tip, reducing corona discharge.\n\nIt isn't a bumper.","G4E03":"A 100-watt radio draws about 20 amps on transmit. Heavy wire straight to the battery, fused in both leads, avoids voltage drop.\n\nThe alternator isn't a clean power point, and transmission line isn't power wire.","G4E04":"Accessory sockets are usually fused at 10 to 20 amps and wired with light wire, while a 100-watt radio can draw 20 amps or more on peaks.\n\nPolarity is standard, and the engine won't overheat.","G4E05":"A vehicle can carry only a small fraction of a wavelength of antenna on the lower HF bands. Loading it to resonance costs a lot of power in losses.\n\nNo FCC rule limits mobile power.","G4E06":"A loaded short antenna has high Q, so it stays well matched over only a narrow range of frequencies. Moving a few kHz can require retuning.\n\nQ is high, not low.","G4E07":"Alternators whine, fuel pumps buzz and engine computers radiate digital noise, all of which an HF receiver can hear.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G4E08":"Each cell makes only about half a volt. Connecting cells in series adds up the voltage, and connecting those strings in parallel adds up the current.\n\nShunt, bypass and bridge aren't ways to wire cells.","G4E09":"A silicon cell produces roughly 0.5 to 0.6 volt open-circuit, set by the physics of the silicon junction.\n\nThat's why a panel for charging 12-volt batteries uses around 36 cells in series: about 18 volts, enough to push charge into the battery.","G4E10":"When the panel isn't producing, the battery's voltage could push current backward through it. A diode lets current flow only from panel to battery.\n\nIt doesn't regulate voltage or limit current.","G4E11":"Lithium batteries must be charged to precise voltages and protected from overcharging. A charge controller manages that.\n\nA series resistor isn't a charge controller.","G5A01":"When inductive and capacitive reactance are equal, they cancel. In a series circuit nothing is left to oppose current except the resistance, so impedance drops to a minimum.\n\nIn a parallel LC circuit it's the opposite: impedance is very high at resonance.","G5A02":"Capacitors and inductors oppose AC in a way that depends on frequency, without turning energy into heat the way resistance does. That opposition is reactance.\n\nResistance opposes DC and AC alike; reactance is about AC.","G5A03":"An inductor's opposition to AC is inductive reactance, measured in ohms.\n\nConductance and admittance measure how easily current flows, and reluctance belongs to magnetic circuits.","G5A04":"A capacitor's opposition to AC is capacitive reactance, also measured in ohms.\n\nSame answer as for inductors: reactance.","G5A05":"An inductor resists changes in current, and higher frequencies change faster, so they meet more opposition. Inductive reactance is X = 2πfL, rising in step with frequency.\n\nAmplitude doesn't affect reactance.","G5A06":"A capacitor passes higher frequencies more easily. Capacitive reactance is X = 1/(2πfC), so it falls as frequency rises.\n\nMemory hook: capacitors pass highs, inductors pass lows.","G5A07":"Impedance measures opposition to current; admittance measures how readily current flows. One is the reciprocal of the other.\n\nFor a pure resistance, the inverse is conductance; for a pure reactance, it is susceptance.","G5A08":"Just as resistance is R = E/I, impedance is Z = E/I for AC, including the effects of reactance.\n\nThe product of voltage and current is power.","G5A09":"Reactance, resistance and impedance are all opposition to current, so all are measured in ohms.\n\nFarads measure capacitance, and siemens measure conductance or admittance.","G5A10":"A transformer matches by its turns ratio, a pi-network (two capacitors and an inductor, as in many antenna tuners and amplifiers) by its component values, and a carefully chosen length of transmission line by how it transforms impedance.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G5A11":"In formulas, X stands for reactance (XL for inductive, XC for capacitive).\n\nZ is impedance, B is susceptance, and Y is admittance.","G5A12":"At the resonant frequency the two reactances are equal and opposite, so they cancel, leaving only resistance.\n\nResistance isn't cancelled, and the circuit doesn't radiate its energy.","G5B01":"Doubling power is a 3 dB increase, and halving it a 3 dB decrease.\n\n6 dB is four times the power, and 10 dB is ten times.","G5B02":"Each branch draws its own current from the source, and the source supplies them all at once, so the total is the sum.\n\nAdding branches increases the total current.","G5B03":"Use P = E²/R. 400 squared is 160,000, and 160,000 ÷ 800 = 200 watts.\n\nCheck: the current is 400 ÷ 800 = 0.5 A, and 400 V × 0.5 A = 200 W.","G5B04":"Use P = E × I. 12 × 0.2 = 2.4 watts.\n\nThe most direct power formula: volts times amps.","G5B05":"Use P = I² × R, with the current in amps: 7.0 mA is 0.007 A. 0.007 squared is 0.000049, and 0.000049 × 1,250 = 0.061 watt, or about 61 milliwatts.\n\n61 watts has the right digits but the wrong unit, 1,000 times too big. Square 0.007 A, not 7, and the result comes out in watts: 0.061 W.","G5B06":"Halve the peak-to-peak value for peak: 100 V. Multiply by 0.707 for RMS: 70.7 V. Then P = E²/R: 70.7 squared is about 5,000, and 5,000 ÷ 50 = 100 watts.\n\nSkipping the RMS step (using 100 V peak) would give 200 W, twice the right answer.","G5B07":"RMS (root mean square) is defined so that, for example, 120 V RMS AC delivers the same power to a resistor as 120 V DC.\n\nThat's why AC voltages are usually given as RMS.","G5B08":"Peak = RMS × 1.414, so 120 × 1.414 = 169.7 V peak. Peak-to-peak is twice that: 339.4 V.\n\n169.7 V is only the peak.","G5B09":"RMS = peak × 0.707, so 17 × 0.707 = 12.0 V.\n\nHalving it would give 8.5 V, which isn't how RMS works.","G5B10":"1 dB down leaves 10^(−0.1), about 0.794, of the power: 79.4% remains, so 20.6% is lost.\n\nA small number of decibels can be a big share of your power, which is why feed-line loss matters.","G5B11":"An unmodulated carrier is steady: its peak envelope power and average power are the same, so the ratio is 1.\n\n1.414 and 0.707 are voltage ratios for a sine wave, not power ratios.","G5B12":"From P = E²/R, E = √(P × R) = √(1200 × 50) = √60,000 ≈ 245 V.\n\n346 V would be the peak value.","G5B13":"With no modulation the envelope never changes, so PEP equals average power.\n\nThe ratio is 1.00.","G5B14":"Halve for peak: 250 V. Multiply by 0.707 for RMS: about 176.8 V. Then P = E²/R: 176.8 squared is about 31,250, and 31,250 ÷ 50 = 625 watts.\n\nSame steps as the 200-volt question.","G5C01":"AC in the primary creates a changing magnetic field, which passes through the secondary and induces a voltage there. Coupling through a shared magnetic field is mutual inductance.\n\nCapacitive coupling isn't what makes the secondary voltage; magnetic coupling is.","G5C02":"The voltage ratio follows the turns ratio in either direction. Input to the winding with fewer turns makes it a 1:4 step-up, so the output is four times the input.\n\nNo added resistance is needed.","G5C03":"Use 1/R = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/50 = 0.1 + 0.05 + 0.02 = 0.17. So R = 1/0.17 ≈ 5.9 ohms.\n\nParallel resistance is always less than the smallest resistor, here 10 ohms.","G5C04":"For two resistors: (100 × 200)/(100 + 200) = 20,000/300 ≈ 67 ohms.\n\nIt must be less than 100 ohms, the smaller resistor.","G5C05":"A transformer passes power through, minus losses. If voltage goes up on the secondary, current must go down there, so the primary carries more current and needs thicker wire.\n\nIt isn't about coupling or matching volumes.","G5C06":"The turns ratio is 1500/500 = 3, so the voltage triples: 120 × 3 = 360 V.\n\nVoltage follows the turns ratio directly.","G5C07":"Impedance follows the square of the turns ratio. The impedance ratio is 600/50 = 12, so the turns ratio is √12 ≈ 3.46, about 3.5 to 1.\n\n12 to 1 is the impedance ratio, not the turns ratio.","G5C08":"Capacitors in parallel add. 750 pF is 0.75 nF, so 5.0 + 5.0 + 0.75 = 10.75 nF.\n\nConvert to the same unit before adding.","G5C09":"Capacitors in series combine like resistors in parallel. For equal values, divide one by how many there are: 100 ÷ 3 ≈ 33.3 µF.\n\nIn parallel they would add to 300 µF.","G5C10":"Inductors combine like resistors. In parallel, equal values divide: 10 ÷ 3 ≈ 3.3 mH.\n\nWatch the units: millihenries, not henries.","G5C11":"Inductors in series add, like resistors: 20 + 50 = 70 mH.\n\nThe 14.3 answer is the parallel result.","G5C12":"Capacitors in series use the product-over-sum formula: (20 × 50)/(20 + 50) = 1,000/70 ≈ 14.3 µF.\n\nThe series total is always less than the smaller capacitor.","G5C13":"Capacitors in parallel add their values, so the total goes up.\n\nIn series the total goes down, and inductors don't add capacitance.","G5C14":"Inductors in series add their values, like resistors, so the total goes up.\n\nIn parallel the total goes down.","G6A01":"Taking a lead-acid battery much below 10.5 volts damages its plates and shortens its life.\n\nRadios also misbehave as voltage drops, so 10.5 V is a sensible stopping point.","G6A02":"All batteries have some internal resistance, which limits how much current they can supply. The lower it is, the more current they can deliver before their voltage sags much.\n\nIt doesn't raise the voltage or guarantee long life.","G6A03":"Germanium diodes have a low forward threshold, about 0.3 V, which makes them useful in sensitive detectors such as crystal radios.\n\nSilicon diodes need about 0.7 V.","G6A04":"An electrolytic uses a very thin oxide layer as its dielectric, so it packs a lot of capacitance into a small can. That makes it ideal for power supply filtering.\n\nIt has loose tolerance and relatively high leakage, and isn't an RF capacitor.","G6A05":"Silicon diodes have a forward threshold of about 0.6 to 0.7 V; the exam answer is 0.7 V.\n\nGermanium is about 0.3 V.","G6A06":"A wire-wound resistor is literally a coil of resistance wire, which acts as an inductor. At RF that inductance adds reactance and upsets the circuit.\n\nUse carbon-composition, film, or surface-mount resistors, which have far less inductance.","G6A07":"A switch is either on or off. At saturation the transistor conducts fully, and at cutoff it doesn't conduct at all.\n\nThe active region between them is for amplifiers.","G6A08":"Ceramic disc capacitors are cheap and everywhere, which is their main advantage.\n\nMany types have loose tolerance and change value with temperature, and electrolytics beat them on capacitance per volume.","G6A09":"MOSFET stands for metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor. The oxide is the insulating layer between gate and channel, which gives the gate a very high input impedance.\n\nA back-biased junction gate describes a JFET.","G6A10":"The control grid is a wire mesh near the cathode. A small voltage on it controls a large flow of electrons to the plate, which is how a tube amplifies.\n\nScreen and suppressor grids improve performance; the control grid is the one used to regulate the flow from cathode to plate.","G6A11":"The capacitance between an inductor's turns forms a parallel resonant circuit with its inductance. Above that resonant frequency, the capacitance dominates and the part acts like a capacitor.\n\nIt doesn't fail or generate harmonics.","G6A12":"Capacitance between the control grid and plate feeds output back to the input and can make an amplifier oscillate. The screen grid sits between them and shields one from the other.\n\nThat's why tetrodes can be used as grid-driven RF amplifiers without neutralization, where a triode usually needs it.","G6B01":"Manufacturers make ferrites from different blends of materials, each called a mix, suited to particular frequency ranges. How a ferrite behaves across frequency depends on its material mix; size and shape mainly affect inductance and power handling.","G6B02":"An MMIC puts a microwave circuit, most often a complete RF amplifier, on one chip, usually with 50-ohm input and output. Hams use them in preamps and microwave gear.\n\nMonolithic means built on a single piece of semiconductor.","G6B03":"CMOS draws significant current only when switching, so it uses very little power when idle. TTL draws current all the time.\n\nThat's why battery-powered devices use CMOS.","G6B04":"BNC is the common bayonet connector on test equipment and handheld radios. It keeps a good match up to around 4 GHz.\n\nType N goes higher, to about 10 GHz.","G6B05":"A ferrite core concentrates magnetic field, so a few turns give a lot of inductance. Different mixes suit different frequencies. And a toroid's ring shape keeps the field inside the core, so it doesn't couple to nearby circuits.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G6B06":"An operational amplifier amplifies continuously varying signals, which is analog work. Hams use them in audio filters and amplifiers.\n\nIt isn't digital logic or an MMIC.","G6B07":"Type N connectors are larger, weatherproof with their gaskets, and keep a good match well into the microwave range, about 10 GHz.\n\nThey're common on VHF and UHF antennas and feed lines.","G6B08":"Like any diode, an LED conducts when forward biased, and that current makes it emit light.\n\nReverse biased, it's off.","G6B10":"Common-mode current flows on the outside of a coax shield. A ferrite around the cable makes that path inductive and lossy, so the current is choked off. The signal inside the coax isn't affected, because its fields cancel outside the cable.\n\nIt doesn't convert or cancel the current.","G6B11":"SMA connectors are small and screw on. They're common on handheld radios, SDR receivers and microwave gear.\n\nSMA stands for SubMiniature version A. It isn't an adapter, a serial-access connector, or a push-on high-voltage connector.","G6B12":"RCA phono plugs, the kind once used for home stereo, are used for audio, keying and control lines on transceivers.\n\nPL-259, BNC and type N are RF connectors.","G7A01":"Filter capacitors can hold a dangerous charge long after a supply is switched off. A bleeder resistor across them slowly drains that charge so the supply is safe to work on.\n\nIt isn't a fuse, and it has nothing to do with ground loops.","G7A02":"Capacitors store charge and fill in the gaps between rectified pulses; inductors resist changes in current. Together they smooth pulsing DC into steady DC.\n\nDiodes do the rectifying, before the filter.","G7A03":"Each diode conducts on alternate half-cycles, using one half of the center-tapped winding, so both halves of the AC wave become DC.\n\nA full-wave bridge uses four diodes and no center tap.","G7A04":"One diode passes current during one half of each cycle and blocks the other.\n\nIts ripple frequency is half that of full-wave, not twice, and it delivers less current.","G7A05":"A full AC cycle is 360 degrees. A half-wave rectifier uses one half of it, 180 degrees, and throws away the other.\n\nHalf the wave, half of 360.","G7A06":"Full-wave rectification turns both halves of the cycle into DC pulses, so nothing is wasted.\n\nThat's why its output is smoother and easier to filter.","G7A07":"Each half-cycle becomes a pulse, so 60 Hz AC gives 120 pulses a second, all in one direction.\n\nA filter turns those pulses into steady DC.","G7A08":"A switchmode supply chops the power at tens of kilohertz or more. Transformers and filter parts for high frequencies are much smaller and lighter than those for 60 Hz.\n\nThey use more parts, not fewer, and can create RF noise.","G7A09":"Symbol 1 shows a vertical bar for the channel, with drain and source leads, and an arrow on the gate lead pointing in. That's an N-channel junction FET.\n\nSymbol 2, with an arrow on the emitter, is a bipolar transistor.","G7A10":"A Zener diode looks like an ordinary diode, but the bar has bent ends, like a stretched Z.\n\nSymbol 4 is a varactor, a diode used as a variable capacitor.","G7A11":"Symbol 2 has a base, a collector and an emitter, with the arrow on the emitter pointing out. In an NPN transistor the arrow points out; in PNP it points in.\n\nMemory hook: NPN, Not Pointing iN.","G7A12":"Symbol 6 shows two coils side by side with straight lines between them. The solid lines mean a solid magnetic core, such as laminated iron.\n\nSymbol 7 is a single coil, so it's an inductor.","G7A13":"Symbol 7 is one coil with an extra connection partway along it, the tap. Here the tap feeds back to the FET's source to keep the oscillator going.\n\nSymbol 11 is a potentiometer.","G7B01":"Capacitance inside a tube or transistor feeds some output back to the input, which can make an amplifier oscillate on its own. Neutralizing adds a small, opposite feedback that cancels it.\n\nIt doesn't affect modulation or frequency.","G7B02":"A Class C amplifier conducts for less than half of each cycle, so it wastes the least power as heat, often 70% efficient or more.\n\nClass A is the least efficient.","G7B03":"AND means both: input A and input B must be high (1) for the output to be high.\n\nA and D both describe NAND (the opposite of AND), and C describes NOR.","G7B04":"Class A amplifiers conduct through the whole cycle, which makes them the most linear but the least efficient.\n\nClass AB conducts between 50 and 100%, Class B 50%, and Class C less than 50%.","G7B05":"Each bit doubles the number of combinations: 2 × 2 × 2 = 8, counting from 000 to 111 (0 to 7).\n\nThe number of states is 2 to the power of the number of bits.","G7B06":"On each clock pulse, every stage passes its bit to the next, so data shifts along the chain one place at a time.\n\nIt isn't a mixer or made of op amps.","G7B07":"The amplifier's output is fed back to its input through a filter. The filter allows feedback at just one frequency, so the circuit sustains a sine wave there.\n\nFeed-forward and mixers don't make oscillators.","G7B08":"Efficiency compares what you get out with what you put in. For a 100-watt output from 200 watts of DC input, efficiency is 50%.\n\nThe other formulas mix up inputs and outputs.","G7B09":"The tank circuit resonates at one frequency, set by its L and C, and the oscillator runs there. Change either and the frequency moves; that's how a VFO tunes.\n\nCounters and dividers belong to digital circuits.","G7B10":"Linear means the output is a faithful, larger copy of the input. That's essential for SSB and AM, whose amplitude carries information.\n\nClass C isn't linear, and \"linear\" isn't just any amplifier used with a transceiver.","G7B11":"FM's amplitude stays constant, and the information is in the frequency. A Class C amplifier distorts amplitude but keeps frequency, so it works for FM, efficiently.\n\nSSB and AM need a linear amplifier.","G7C01":"The balanced modulator leaves two sidebands with the carrier removed. A sharp filter passes the wanted sideband and blocks the other, producing SSB.\n\nOscillators and amplifiers don't select sidebands.","G7C02":"It combines audio and a carrier so that the carrier cancels, leaving both sidebands. That double-sideband signal is the first step in making SSB.\n\nIt doesn't produce FM or audio.","G7C03":"A transmitter delivers full power only into the load it's designed for. A matching transformer converts the load's impedance so both sides see what they need.\n\nIt doesn't reduce power or ripple.","G7C04":"A product detector mixes the incoming SSB signal with a locally generated carrier, recovering the original audio. It's also used for CW.\n\nIt isn't test gear or a frequency multiplier.","G7C05":"A direct digital synthesizer generates its signal digitally, timed by a crystal clock. It can tune over a wide range, in fine steps, with crystal stability.\n\nIts output is low power and not perfectly pure.","G7C06":"A DSP filter is software, so changing its width or shape is just a calculation. Analog filters need different physical parts for each shape.\n\nIt doesn't reduce mixing products or suit VHF especially.","G7C07":"Even the signals a filter is meant to pass lose a little strength going through it. That loss, in dB, is the insertion loss.\n\nUltimate rejection is about signals outside the passband.","G7C08":"Sensitivity is how weak a signal the receiver can detect. Low noise and enough gain at the input help, and narrower bandwidth lets in less noise.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G7C09":"I (in-phase) and Q (quadrature) are two copies of the signal shifted by a quarter cycle, 90 degrees. Together they capture both amplitude and phase.\n\nQuadrature means 90 degrees.","G7C10":"Because I and Q describe both amplitude and phase, software can build or decode AM, FM, SSB, CW and digital modes from them.\n\nIt doesn't remove the need for good converters.","G7C11":"In a software-defined radio, the hardware mostly converts between radio and digital signals; everything else happens in software.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G7C12":"The cutoff frequency is defined as the point where the filter's output drops to half power, 3 dB down.\n\nRolloff describes how steeply it falls after that.","G7C13":"Far outside the passband, a filter's attenuation stops increasing and levels off. That maximum attenuation is its ultimate rejection.\n\nInsertion loss is the loss inside the passband.","G7C14":"Bandwidth is the width between the two points where the output has fallen to half power, 3 dB down.\n\nThe other pairs aren't how bandwidth is defined.","G8A01":"In direct FSK, the transmitter's oscillator is shifted between two frequencies by the data signal itself, making mark and space.\n\nThe alternative, AFSK, sends audio tones into an SSB transmitter instead.","G8A02":"Phase modulation shifts the timing of the carrier's waves back and forth with the signal. It's closely related to FM.\n\nThe other terms aren't the name of this process.","G8A03":"FM moves the carrier's frequency up and down with the signal, while its amplitude stays constant.\n\nFrequency conversion is mixing to another frequency, not modulation.","G8A04":"A reactance modulator varies a reactance in step with the audio. Applied to an amplifier stage, it shifts the phase of the signal passing through.\n\nApplied to an oscillator, it would produce FM.","G8A05":"AM changes the strength of the carrier with the signal, so the power rises and falls.\n\n\"Power modulation\" isn't a standard term.","G8A06":"QPSK31 uses four phases instead of two, at the same symbol rate, so its bandwidth matches BPSK31. Its encoding adds error correction. And because the phase shifts reverse on the opposite sideband, both stations must use the same sideband.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G8A07":"SSB sends one sideband and no carrier, about 2.4 to 3 kHz wide. FM and PM voice take much more bandwidth, and vestigial sideband keeps part of the second sideband.\n\nThat narrowness is why SSB rules HF voice.","G8A08":"Driving a transmitter past its limits distorts the signal and creates splatter, which spreads it over excessive bandwidth and interferes with nearby stations.\n\nIt doesn't cause drift or too little audio.","G8A09":"FT8 sends one of eight tones at a time, each a set spacing apart, in timed 15-second cycles.\n\nIt isn't AM or spread spectrum.","G8A10":"Too much drive pushes the signal into the transmitter's limits, clipping the peaks of the modulation envelope flat. The result is distortion and splatter.\n\nIt doesn't mean the ALC is right or the carrier suppressed.","G8A11":"Picture the RF wave on an oscilloscope: the outline drawn through all its peaks is the envelope, and for AM it follows the audio.\n\nIt isn't the carrier, the bandwidth or spurious signals.","G8A12":"Four phase states can represent two bits each (00, 01, 10, 11), so QPSK carries twice as much data per symbol as two-phase BPSK.\n\nThe other answers are invented descriptions.","G8A13":"A link budget tallies everything between transmitter and receiver: start with power, add antenna gains, subtract feed-line and path losses, and you get the expected received signal level.\n\nIt isn't about money.","G8A14":"If a receiver needs a certain level to work and the link delivers more, the extra is the margin, the room left for fading.\n\nIt's the cushion between what arrives and what's required.","G8B01":"In a superheterodyne receiver, the local oscillator is tuned so that mixing it with the signal you want produces the fixed intermediate frequency.\n\nThe IF stays the same; the LO moves.","G8B02":"The mixer produces the IF from signals on both sides of the local oscillator. A signal on the far side, two IFs away from the one you want, also lands on the IF: the image.\n\nFilters before the mixer reduce it.","G8B03":"Heterodyning combines two frequencies to make new ones, their sum and difference. It's the principle behind the superheterodyne receiver.\n\nSynthesizing means generating frequencies; it isn't the name for mixing two signals.","G8B04":"Many VHF FM transmitters generate a signal at a lower frequency and multiply it up, for example by 12, to reach 2 meters.\n\nA mixer combines two frequencies instead.","G8B05":"Third-order products like 2F1 − F2 fall just beside the two original signals, right where they cause the most trouble. Even-order products fall far away and are easy to filter out.\n\nThat's why receiver specs focus on third-order intercept.","G8B06":"Carson's rule: bandwidth ≈ 2 × (deviation + highest modulating frequency) = 2 × (5 + 3) = 16 kHz.\n\nAdding deviation and audio gives only one side; double it for both sides.","G8B07":"146.52 ÷ 12.21 = 12, so the oscillator's signal is multiplied by 12, and so is its deviation. To end up with 5 kHz, the oscillator needs 5,000 ÷ 12 ≈ 416.7 Hz.\n\nMultipliers multiply deviation along with frequency.","G8B08":"Duty cycle is how much of the time a mode transmits at full power. SSB voice averages low, but FM, RTTY and many data modes run near 100%, which can overheat a transmitter rated for voice. Reduce power if needed.\n\nIt isn't about tuning or break-in.","G8B09":"A wider filter lets in more noise without adding any signal. Matching it to the signal's width keeps all the signal and as little noise as possible.\n\nNo FCC rule requires it.","G8B10":"Sending symbols faster means the signal changes faster, which spreads it over more frequencies.\n\nThat's why slow modes like FT8 and WSPR are so narrow.","G8B11":"Mixing a 10 MHz signal with a 19 MHz LO produces 29 MHz and 9 MHz, plus the originals. A filter picks the one wanted.\n\nThe output frequencies aren't the ratio, average or arithmetic product of the input frequencies.","G8B12":"When signals pass through something non-linear, such as an overloaded amplifier or a corroded connection, they mix and create spurious new frequencies.\n\nHeterodyning is the same process done on purpose.","G8B13":"The order is the sum of the multipliers: 2 + 1 = 3, which is odd. 3F1 − F2 is fourth order (3 + 1) and 5F1 − 3F2 is eighth order (5 + 3), both even.\n\nSo only 2F1 − F2 is odd-order.","G8C02":"WSPR (Weak Signal Propagation Reporter) stations send short, low-power beacon signals, and stations worldwide report what they decode online, mapping propagation.\n\nPSK31 and MFSK16 are keyboard chat modes.","G8C03":"Like an envelope's address, a packet's header says where it came from, where it's going, and how to handle it.\n\nThe preamble only helps the receiver synchronize.","G8C04":"Baudot, used for RTTY, encodes each character in 5 bits, with start and stop bits marking each character. Five bits give only 32 codes, so letters and figures share them with shift codes.\n\nThe 7-bit code with parity is ASCII.","G8C05":"ARQ (automatic repeat request) checks each packet. ACK means received correctly; NAK (negative acknowledgment) means it arrived with errors, so send it again.\n\nNAK is the \"say again\".","G8C06":"If a packet keeps failing, the stations give up after a set number of tries and disconnect, rather than retrying forever.\n\nNothing overflows or reroutes.","G8C07":"FT8 can decode signals down to about −20 dB, well below what you could hear. That's why it works so well with weak signals and low power.\n\nMSK144 is for meteor scatter, and AMTOR and MFSK32 need stronger signals.","G8C08":"Varicode gives the most common characters, mostly lower-case letters, the shortest codes. Capitals get longer codes, so typing in capitals takes longer to send.\n\nStandard (BPSK) PSK31 has no error correction, and it needs less power than RTTY for similar error rates.","G8C09":"Mesh nodes connect to several neighbors, so data can find another route around a failed node.\n\nMore nodes don't strengthen signals or reduce interference.","G8C10":"FEC adds extra, calculated information that lets the receiver detect and correct errors without asking for a resend.\n\nA parity bit only detects an error; it can't fix it.","G8C11":"The names come from old telegraph and teleprinter days: mark for one state, space for the other.\n\nDots and dashes belong to Morse.","G8C12":"Varicode is a variable-length code: common characters get short codes, rare ones longer, so typical text goes faster.\n\nViterbi is a decoding method, not a character code.","G8C13":"A clean data signal shows as a neat trace. Extra lines beside it are splatter from overdriving the transmitter.\n\nReduce the audio drive until they disappear.","G8C14":"Each new line of the display is a moment in time, sliding down the screen like a waterfall, with stronger signals shown brighter.\n\nThe other answers swap the axes.","G8C15":"FT8 reports signal-to-noise ratio, measured as if in a 2.5 kHz bandwidth. +3 is a strong FT8 signal; reports often run down to −20.\n\nThey aren't S-meter readings.","G8C16":"These systems carry digitized voice, mostly on VHF and UHF repeaters.\n\nWSPR, FT8 and Winlink carry data, not voice.","G9A01":"The farther apart the wires and the thinner they are, the higher the impedance. Length and frequency don't change it.\n\nThat's why window line, with wide spacing, is around 450 ohms.","G9A02":"With high SWR, power travels back and forth along the line and current peaks are higher, so more energy is lost as heat in a line that has loss.\n\nOn a nearly lossless line, high SWR costs little.","G9A03":"Window line, two wires held apart by plastic with windows cut out, is sold as 450-ohm line. Its low loss makes it popular for multiband wire antennas.\n\n50 and 75 ohms are coax values.","G9A04":"When the impedances differ, part of the power bounces back toward the transmitter. When they match, nearly all of it goes into the antenna.\n\nResonance alone doesn't cause reflection.","G9A05":"Coax losses grow as frequency rises, so a run fine on HF can be very lossy on UHF.\n\nThere's no \"Marconi's Law of Attenuation\".","G9A06":"Manufacturers rate their cables in dB of loss per 100 feet at given frequencies.\n\nOhms describe impedance, not loss.","G9A07":"Standing waves come from reflections, and reflections come from a mismatch. Match the antenna to the line and they disappear.\n\nLine length doesn't eliminate standing waves.","G9A08":"A tuner at the transmitter end matches the transmitter to the line's input, but the mismatch at the antenna is still there, so the line still carries standing waves at 5:1.\n\nTo fix the line's SWR, match at the antenna.","G9A09":"For a resistive load, SWR is the larger impedance divided by the smaller: 200 ÷ 50 = 4, written 4:1.\n\nSWR is always written as a number to 1, never 1:4.","G9A10":"Larger over smaller: 50 ÷ 10 = 5, so 5:1.\n\nIt works the same whether the load is higher or lower than the line.","G9A11":"Reflected power is weakened on its way back down a lossy line, so less of it reaches the meter, and the SWR there reads lower than at the antenna.\n\nA lossy line can make a bad antenna look good.","G9B01":"Without a separate feed line or proper ground, the transmitter itself becomes part of the antenna, and RF flows on the equipment. That can cause RF burns and interference.\n\nIt needn't be a wavelength long.","G9B02":"With flat radials, a quarter-wave ground plane is about 36 ohms. Bending the radials down at roughly 45 degrees raises it to about 50 ohms, a good match for coax.\n\nSloping them up would lower it.","G9B03":"It radiates equally in every compass direction around it, which is why verticals are popular for general coverage.\n\nIsotropic would mean equally in all directions including up, which no real antenna does.","G9B04":"A dipole radiates best broadside, perpendicular to the wire, and hardly at all off its ends, making two lobes.\n\nThe lobes are off the sides, not the ends.","G9B05":"Close to the ground, the ground reflection reshapes the pattern so a low dipole sends much of its energy upward, nearly the same in every direction. That's what makes it good for NVIS.\n\nHeight definitely affects the pattern.","G9B06":"Radials on or just under the ground form a ground system that reduces losses in the soil beneath the antenna.\n\nElevated verticals use raised radials, but ground-mounted ones lie on the ground.","G9B07":"A half-wave dipole is about 73 ohms in free space. Near the ground its impedance rises and falls with height, but once it is below about a quarter wavelength it drops steadily as it gets lower, toward 1/10 wavelength.\n\nThat's why a low dipole often matches 50-ohm coax well.","G9B08":"At the center, current is highest and voltage lowest, giving low impedance. Toward the ends, current falls and voltage rises, so impedance climbs.\n\nOff-center-fed antennas use this to pick a convenient impedance.","G9B09":"A vertical depends on the ground beneath it as part of the antenna system, and poor soil wastes power. A horizontal antenna up in the air doesn't, so it loses less.\n\nIt needs no radials at all.","G9B10":"Use 468 ÷ frequency in MHz: 468 ÷ 14.25 ≈ 32.8 feet.\n\nThe 468 comes from half a wavelength in free space (492 feet ÷ MHz), shortened slightly for a real wire.","G9B11":"468 ÷ 3.55 ≈ 131.8 feet.\n\nLower frequency, longer antenna: 80 meters needs a lot of wire.","G9B12":"A quarter wave is half of a half wave: 234 ÷ frequency in MHz. 234 ÷ 28.5 ≈ 8.2 feet.\n\nThat's a typical 10-meter whip.","G9C01":"Thicker elements have a lower Q, so the antenna stays well matched over a wider range of frequencies.\n\nLoading coils narrow the bandwidth.","G9C02":"The driven element is essentially a half-wave dipole, the part the feed line connects to.\n\nThe reflector and directors are near that length too.","G9C03":"The slightly longer reflector pushes energy forward, and the slightly shorter director pulls it forward, so the beam points from the reflector toward the director.\n\nMemory hook: directors are shorter, they lead the way.","G9C04":"dBi compares with an isotropic antenna, dBd with a dipole. A dipole already has 2.15 dB of gain over isotropic, so the same antenna's gain is 2.15 higher in dBi.\n\nThat's why advertisers like quoting dBi.","G9C05":"Each added director, with a longer boom to hold it, focuses the beam more, raising gain and narrowing the beamwidth.\n\nBeamwidth gets narrower, not wider.","G9C07":"A good beam sends most of its power forward and little backward. Front-to-back ratio, in dB, measures that difference.\n\nIt isn't about counting elements.","G9C08":"Every directional antenna has a strongest direction, its main lobe. That's where you point it.\n\nIt isn't a voltage or current point on an element.","G9C09":"Doubling the antenna area roughly doubles the gain, and doubling is 3 dB.\n\nIn practice spacing affects it, but 3 dB is the rule of thumb.","G9C10":"Yagi design is a balance between gain, front-to-back ratio and bandwidth, and boom length, element count and spacing all shift that balance.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G9C11":"A Yagi's driven element usually has a low impedance, and it is made slightly short so it looks capacitive. A hairpin, a shorted stub across the feed point, adds inductance that works with that capacitance to raise the impedance to match the feed line.\n\nIt isn't a coax section or a capacitor.","G9C12":"A gamma match connects to the element away from its center, where the impedance is right, so the element can be mounted directly to a metal boom.\n\nIt does use a capacitor, and it's a single-band match.","G9D01":"A low horizontal dipole sends most of its energy nearly straight up, which NVIS needs for coverage out to a few hundred miles.\n\nHigher dipoles and verticals send energy at lower angles, for longer distances.","G9D02":"At the end of a half-wave wire, current is near zero and voltage high, so impedance is thousands of ohms. End-fed half-waves need a matching transformer, often 49:1.\n\nCenter feeding gives the low impedance.","G9D03":"A halo is a horizontal dipole bent into a circle, giving roughly omnidirectional, horizontally polarized radiation. Popular for VHF SSB mobile.\n\nIt doesn't favor the feed point side.","G9D04":"A trap is a parallel tuned circuit in the element. On its resonant band it blocks current, making the antenna electrically shorter; on lower bands it lets the whole wire work.\n\nIt doesn't filter spurious signals.","G9D05":"Stacking one above another squeezes the vertical pattern, concentrating energy at lower angles for more gain toward the horizon.\n\nPolarization stays horizontal.","G9D06":"Different elements become active at different frequencies, so a log-periodic covers a wide range, often several bands, with a steady match.\n\nIt has less gain per element than a Yagi.","G9D07":"The elements get progressively shorter and closer together toward the front, each element shorter and closer than the one behind it by the same ratio. That design is what gives the wide bandwidth.\n\nThe name describes the structure.","G9D08":"A motor, originally from a cordless screwdriver, moves the base loading coil in and out of the lower mast. Contacts at the top of the mast short out the part of the coil inside, so the inductance in circuit changes and the antenna tunes to each frequency.\n\nThe antenna's height shifts a little as the coil moves, but what tunes it is the change in inductance, not the whip length.","G9D09":"A Beverage is a long wire strung low, often a wavelength or more, that hears well in one direction while rejecting noise from others. It's too inefficient to transmit with.\n\nIt's a receive-only antenna, used mostly on 160 and 80 meters.","G9D10":"A small loop hears best in the plane of the loop and has deep nulls perpendicular to it, which makes it good for direction finding and nulling noise.\n\nIt isn't omnidirectional.","G9D11":"A multiband antenna works on several bands, so a harmonic of your signal may fall on another band it handles well and get radiated efficiently.\n\nThey don't necessarily need a tuner or open-wire line.","G9D12":"Hung from one high point in the middle with the ends sloping down, the dipole forms an upside-down V.\n\nAn inverted L and a sloper are different shapes.","G0A01":"Like a microwave oven, RF energy is absorbed by the body and turns into heat. At high enough levels that can damage tissue, especially the eyes.\n\nRF isn't ionizing radiation, so it doesn't cause radiation poisoning.","G0A02":"Power density is how much RF passes through an area. Frequency matters because the body absorbs some frequencies more than others. Duty cycle sets how much of the time the transmitter is actually putting out power.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G0A03":"The FCC accepts calculation, modeling or measurement, as described in OET Bulletin 65: its formulas and tables, antenna modeling software, or measurement with calibrated equipment.\n\nAll three choices are correct.","G0A04":"Exposure limits apply to the average over a set time, 6 minutes for controlled environments and 30 for the general public. Brief high exposure can be fine if the average stays within limits.\n\nIt isn't about the transmitter's daily average power.","G0A05":"The rules require you to keep people out of areas where RF exceeds the limits, for example by moving the antenna, reducing power, or controlling access.\n\nThere's no EIS-97 form, and neighbors can't sign away the limits.","G0A06":"Stations that don't qualify for exemption must be evaluated, using the methods in the FCC's OET Bulletin 65.\n\nNo FCC permission is needed, and the World Meteorological Organization has nothing to do with it.","G0A07":"Exposure is averaged over time. A mode that transmits only part of the time, like SSB or CW, puts out less average power than one at full power continuously, so you can run more peak power and stay within limits.\n\nNo duty cycle is exempt.","G0A08":"Every station needs an evaluation, unless it's exempt, and if it finds areas over the limit, people must be kept out of them.\n\nPosting the rule or notifying neighbors isn't required.","G0A09":"Accurate measurement needs equipment whose readings are known to be right: a calibrated meter and an antenna with known characteristics.\n\nAn SWR meter, a receiver or an oscilloscope can't measure field strength accurately.","G0A10":"The simplest fix is operational: don't aim the beam at the neighbor's area when they could be exposed, for example with rotator limits.\n\nA higher-gain antenna would make it worse, and a higher front-to-back ratio only reduces radiation off the back of the antenna, not the main lobe.","G0A11":"Indoor antennas put people close to the radiating element. Evaluate the RF levels in the rooms where people are, and reduce power or move the antenna if needed.\n\nShielding the antenna would stop it radiating.","G0A12":"Since 2021, the FCC's rules apply to essentially every transmitter. Only stations averaging one milliwatt or less are automatically exempt. Stations above that can still be exempt based on their power and distance from people; otherwise they must be evaluated.\n\nAmateurs aren't exempt, and there's no 500-watt threshold.","G0B01":"A 240-volt circuit has two hot wires, a neutral and a ground. Fuses and breakers go in the hot wires, which carry the voltage. Interrupting neutral or ground would leave things live while seeming off.\n\nNever fuse the neutral or ground.","G0B02":"The National Electrical Code matches wire size to breaker size so wiring can't overheat before the breaker trips. 20 amps needs 12-gauge copper.\n\nSmaller gauge numbers mean thicker wire.","G0B03":"14-gauge copper is rated for 15 amps in household wiring. A larger breaker could let the wire overheat.\n\nMemory hook: 14-gauge, 15 amps; 12-gauge, 20 amps.","G0B04":"The goal is to send a lightning surge into the earth before it enters the house. Putting the lightning ground at the equipment would route the surge through the house. The ground rods go outside, and the station's equipment ground is bonded to them.\n\nArrestors go where the feed lines enter the building.","G0B05":"A ground fault circuit interrupter compares current in the hot and neutral wires. If some current is missing, it's leaking to ground, perhaps through a person, and the GFCI cuts power in milliseconds.\n\nNormal current from hot to neutral doesn't trip it.","G0B06":"The NEC sets standards for safe wiring, grounding and installation. It doesn't cover bandwidth, modulation or RF exposure, which are FCC matters.\n\nIt's about not starting fires or shocking anyone.","G0B07":"A harness only protects you if it can hold you. Check its weight rating and age, and replace it when it's past its service life.\n\nHolding on with one hand isn't the rule. Stay tied off to the tower at all times and keep three points of contact while climbing. Carry only light tools, tethered so they can't fall, and haul heavy ones up on a rope.","G0B08":"Lockout-tagout means switching off and locking the power, with a tag saying why, so no one can turn it back on while you're aloft.\n\nNever unground a tower.","G0B09":"Generators produce carbon monoxide, which is deadly and has no smell. Run one only outdoors, away from doors, windows and vents, never in a house or garage, even with the doors open.\n\nStore fuel away from the generator, and ground it as its instructions say.","G0B10":"Lead is toxic when swallowed. Traces on your fingers can reach your food, so wash your hands after soldering.\n\nSolder doesn't turn into poisonous gas from RF.","G0B11":"If grounds aren't connected, a strike can raise one to a huge voltage relative to another, and current jumps between them through your equipment. Bonding keeps them at the same potential.\n\nBends should be gentle, never sharp right angles.","G0B12":"An interlock switch cuts the power when a cover is removed, and some designs also short the high-voltage capacitors. Still, never assume the capacitors are discharged; filter capacitors can hold a lethal charge.\n\nIt isn't about warranties or overheating.","G0B13":"Placed at the entry point and connected to the outside ground system, arrestors send surges to earth before they reach your equipment.\n\nNot on the antenna, and not in series with ground leads."};
